{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghHvcMqGWYjMAbo3UJiR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EPW1Z9PzU5hSNnCRVtLxoY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","offset":250,"limit":250},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10433,"cache_read_input_tokens":24533,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10433},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"a9355ba8-44fd-4b75-9b6a-befc21ca27e8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:55:40.473Z","request_id":"req_011CghHvc8wbPoNqT5Hz1hTp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01EPW1Z9PzU5hSNnCRVtLxoY":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","offset":250,"limit":250}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EPW1Z9PzU5hSNnCRVtLxoY","type":"tool_result","content":"250\tThe engine's `TileMap` is a heightmap: one wall type per cell, no passages under things. Building needs real 3D blocks (roofs, bridges, tunnels, mixed-material walls), so the game has its own `VoxelWorld`. It plugs into the engine where the engine expects a map.\n251\t\n252\t#### 6.1.1 Data\n253\t- Block size `T = 16` world units (one tile). Axes: x east, y south, z up.\n254\t- Story world: `W = 144, D = 144` columns, `H = 24` levels. Bee world: `48 × 48 × 16`.\n255\t- `vox: Uint8Array(W*D*H)`, column-major: `idx(x,y,z) = (y*W + x)*H + z`, so a column is contiguous.\n256\t- `meta: Map<idx, {rot, hp}>` holds stair rotation and partial damage. Damage heals back after 4 s without hits.\n257\t- `colVer: Uint32Array(W*D)` holds per-column versions for render caches. `topZ: Uint8Array(W*D)` is the highest solid level + 1, kept on edit.\n258\t- **Objects** (furniture, doors, lights, plants) are *not* voxels. They live in `DQ.Objects` with an anchor cell `(x,y,z)` and occupy that cell (doors and portals occupy 2 levels). `world.objAt(x,y,z)` is indexed by `Map<idx, obj>`.\n259\t- Block properties from `DQ.BLOCKS`: `solid`, `opaque`, `liquid`, `hp`, `tier`, `drop`, `tex`, `light`, `shape` (`'cube' | 'stair'`), `placeable`.\n260\t- **Level 0 is Backing Cloth** (unbreakable). Below and outside the world is the **Unravel**, a void. Anything falling below z = −16 (through a void hole or off the world's edge) respawns at its last safe position after a 0.6 s \"unravel\" effect. The builder takes 4 damage.\n261\t\n262\t#### 6.1.2 API (`DQ.VoxelWorld`)\n263\t```\n264\tget(x,y,z) → id            set(x,y,z,id,{by, silent}) → bool (emits blockPlaced/blockBroken, bumps colVer of the column and 4 neighbours)\n265\tsolidAt(x,y,z)  opaqueAt(x,y,z)  liquidAt(x,y,z)   (integer cells; outside the world and below level 0 is void, which reads as air)\n266\ttopZ(x,y)                  standLevels(x,y) → levels L where solid(L-1) and air(L), air(L+1)\n267\tgroundAt(px,py,r,z,step)   highest solid top ≤ z+step under the circle footprint, −Infinity over void (world units; feeds E.Body-style physics)\n268\tceilingAt(px,py,r,z)       lowest solid bottom above z within the footprint (head bumps)\n269\tcollide(body)              push a circle out of blocks overlapping [z+step, z+h) (same algorithm as TileMap.collide, per level)\n270\traycast(o,d,maxT) → {x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, t} | null          3D DDA (Amanatides–Woo) through cells\n271\tpickScreen(sx,sy) → {x,y,z, n:[nx,ny,nz], obj} | null     view ray through the screen pixel (see 6.1.5)\n272\tbounds(view) → {x0,y0,x1,y1}                             camera clamp (as TileMap.bounds)\n273\tT, solids=[] , _liftOrder(view,x,y,z,k)                  the renderer's hooks (see 6.1.4)\n274\tdiff() → [[idx, id], ...]   applyDiff(list)               save and network: cells that differ from the generated base world\n275\thash() → u32                                              tests and net convergence\n276\t```\n277\tGeneration writes `base` (a copy of `vox` taken after `generateWorld`), so `diff()` is a cheap scan. Saves store only the diff (typically under 30 KB).\n278\t\n279\t#### 6.1.3 Rendering: face-sprite blitting per block, queued per column run\n280\t- **Face atlas.** For each `(view id, zoom step)` the renderer pre-renders a small sprite for every material and visible face: `top`, `faceA` (the camera-facing side on the viewer's left) and `faceB` (right). In three-quarter view there is `top` and `front`. Each material gets 4 variants (picked by `hash(x,y,z) & 3`).\n281\t  - Sprites are rasterized per pixel with `E.tones(base)`. The pattern functions are in §8.1. Each face is an exact parallelogram of the projected block face, snapped to whole pixels. All blocks of a view and zoom project to the same shapes, so one atlas serves the whole world. It is rebuilt only when the zoom step changes. A 90° turn keeps iso faces identical, and the renderer only swaps which world face maps to A/B.\n282\t  - Light: top = `base`, faceA = `sh`, faceB = `deep`, following the engine's \"light from the viewer's upper left\".\n283\t- **Zoom steps.** Zoom is quantized to `[0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]` (default 1). Our own zoom actions (− / =, right stick ↑/↓) step through them with `game.setZoom(step)`. The play scene sets `camera: { zoom: false }`, so the engine's continuous player zoom stays off and the atlas only ever needs these five scales. The chosen step is remembered in our settings (`dqZoom`).\n284\t- **Run queueing.** For each visible column, split its opaque solid blocks into runs `[z0, z1)`. Queue each run once:\n285\t  ```js\n286\t  r.queue(cx*16+8, cy*16+8, z0*16, g => drawRun(g, r, cx, cy, z0, z1), { occluder: true, top: z1*16 })\n287\t  ```\n288\t  `drawRun` blits, bottom to top, each block's visible faces: a side face shows where the neighbour column is not opaque at that level, and the top face shows where the block above is not opaque or is cut away. Then come overlays: crack stages on damaged blocks, contact AO (a 2-px dark band on side faces at a run's bottom where it meets the ground), top-face edge AO (a dark band along an edge whose neighbour is one level higher), and sun-shadow tint on tops that have a taller column within 2 cells toward the light. Overlays use `px.blend(g, a, 'multiply', ...)` with shared per-view overlay sprites.\n289\t- **Visible columns.** Invert the four screen corners to the ground plane at `z = 0` and at `z = H*16`. Take the cell bounding box plus 2 cells of margin, then clamp it. That is about 500–700 columns at zoom 1.\n290\t- **Liquids.** Water blocks are queued separately: `r.queue(..., z*16, drawWater)`. The water top is drawn with `px.blend(.7)` and a 4-frame shimmer, plus side faces next to air. Blocks under water get a blue multiply tint.\n291\t- **Void and fringe.** Behind the world, draw a deep-navy gradient (`r.sky(['#06051a','#0d0b2a','#141238'])`) with drifting cream \"lint motes\" and a faint stitched grid. Edge columns and void holes get frayed fringe threads: 1-px lines hanging 1–2 blocks below the lowest block, swaying with time. These are queued just before their column.\n292\t- **Cutaway** (seeing inside roofs, tunnels and canopies). Each frame, set `cut = heroLevel + 2` when an opaque block sits at a level in `[heroLevel+2, heroLevel+6]` in either of these places:\n293\t  - the hero's own column;\n294\t  - any of the 3 columns toward the camera. Step from the hero's cell by `(round(view.fx), round(view.fy))`, the ground direction that points at the camera; in iso both components are ±1.\n295\t\n296\t  Ordinary 2-high room walls (levels `heroLevel` and `heroLevel+1`) are never cut; the x-ray silhouette shows the hero behind them.\n297\t\n298\t  Blocks at `z ≥ cut` within radius 6.5 cells of the hero are skipped. A 1.5-cell band at the edge is drawn every other pixel (`px.polyDither`). A block just below the cut with a block above it gets a \"cross-section\" overlay: a darker top with a diagonal stitch hatch. The cut eases in and out over 0.2 s (the radius grows from 0). It stays on for 0.5 s after the hero leaves cover (hysteresis), so it does not flicker at doorways. Dev toggle: \"Cutaway: auto / always / off\".\n299\t- **X-ray.** The builder, remote players and bosses use `r.actor(..., { xray: { color, actors: false } })`. They show as tinted silhouettes behind blocks (the engine does this through `occluder` queue items).\n300\t- **Draw cost guard.** No baking hitches: blits are cheap. If a frame queues more than 2,500 runs (extreme zoom-out), draw the far columns' top faces only.\n301\t\n302\t#### 6.1.4 Depth sorting\n303\tThe renderer sorts by `view.order(x,y,z) = fx*x + fy*y + z*1e-3`. Runs in one column sort bottom to top, and columns sort by depth. For things that stand on blocks or sit under overhangs, the renderer calls `r._map._liftOrder(view, x, y, z, k)` for every queued item above `z > .5`. `VoxelRenderer.queue` sets `r._map = world`. Call it before queuing any actor or object: the renderer reads `r._map` at queue time and resets it to null at the start of every frame. Implement both directions:\n304\t- **Lift.** If the item's cell has a run whose top is `≤ z+1` and it is the highest such run, return `max(k, order(center, run.z0*16) + .002 + z*1e-5)`.\n305\t- **Sink.** If the item's cell has a run with `z0*16 ≥ z + 20` (an overhang above its head), return `min(k, order(center, thatRun.z0*16) - .002)`.\n306\t\n307\tAlso provide `world.T = 16` and `world.solids = []` (the renderer reads both). Test with a diorama (§11.2): a hero under a roof, on a bridge with a body below, beside and behind 2- and 3-high walls, on stairs, and in a doorway. Write a filmstrip check in all 4 rotations.\n308\t\n309\t#### 6.1.5 Picking (mouse and touch-free targeting)\n310\tUse the line of sight through a screen pixel, as `TileMap.pick` computes it: P and N from the view rows. Clip to the world box and run the 3D DDA from the camera side. The first opaque or solid block hit, with the entry face normal, is the **break target**. `hit + normal` is the **place target**. Objects take part through `world.objAt` (placed objects count as hit targets, so the mouse can click a bench or a bed). Actors are picked with the engine's `{ pick: entity }` on `r.actor`/`r.shape` and `r.pick(sx, sy)` (the last frame's draws). Actor picks win over blocks when both are under the pointer. Respect the cutaway: cut blocks are not pickable.\n311\t\n312\t#### 6.1.6 Physics (`DQ.VoxelBody extends E.Body`)\n313\t- Builder body: `r = 5, h = 28` (fits under a 2-block ceiling), `eye = 22, chest = 16`, `step = 2`, `gravity = 800`.\n314\t- **`update(dt, world)`.** Run `E.Body.update` (it calls `world.collide` and `world.groundAt`). Then check the ceiling: if `vz > 0` and `z + h > ceilingAt(...)`, clamp `z` and set `vz = 0`.\n315\t  - **Stairs:** in a stair cell the ground is a ramp along the stair's `rot` (0 → 16 over the cell), so walking up stairs needs no jump.\n316\t  - **Water:** in water, speed ×0.55. A column with water 2 or more levels deep blocks walkers like a wall (bodies cannot enter, and a falling body is pushed out to `lastSafe`). The builder cannot swim, as in the source.\n317\t- **Auto-hop** (setting, on by default). Moving into a 1-block ledge with 2 free levels above it, on the ground, for over 0.08 s triggers `jump(215)` with a small forward boost.\n318\t- **Safe positions.** Store `lastSafe` every 0.5 s while standing on solid ground that is not near a void edge.\n319\t- **No block may be placed inside a body.** `Builder.place` checks every builder, resident and monster AABB first, then shows \"Something's in the way!\"\n320\t- **`world.escape(body)`** moves a buried body to the nearest standable cell within 4 cells. It runs after any set() that could bury something (worldgen stamps, Tangle blocks).\n321\t\n322\t### 6.2 Camera and views\n323\t- `game.follow(builder.body, { z: 14, lead: 18 })`, with `game.cam.bounds = v => world.bounds(v)` and `cam.smooth = .12`.\n324\t- **Rotation:**\n325\t  - Q/E keys, or a right-stick flick (`input.flick('x')`), turn by ±90° with `game.rotateView(±90)`.\n326\t  - Cover the snap with a 0.18 s crossfade: grab the previous frame's buffer before turning and blend it out with `px.blend` on the overlay. Play a soft \"whoosh\" (`sfx('whoosh', {vol:.2})`).\n327\t  - Movement keeps working in screen space (`view.screenDirToGround`).\n328\t- **Zoom:** right stick ↑/↓ or `−`/`=` step through the zoom levels (§6.1.3). In play the mouse wheel belongs to the hotbar; it zooms only on the Map screen. R3/R resets rotation and zoom (`game.resetCamera()`, then zoom step 1).\n329\t- **Settings → Camera view:** Isometric (default) or Three-quarter. It switches with `game.setView`; the atlas has entries for both.\n330\t- Cutscenes take the camera with tweens on a proxy focus object and give it back on exit.\n331\t\n332\t### 6.3 The Builder (player)\n333\t- **Look:** chosen at New Game. Look A: spiky hair and a bandana. Look B: ponytail and an apron. Four palette swatches. Name entry (default \"Patch\") on an on-screen letter grid that works with a pad, keys or mouse; typing letters works directly. The builder never speaks (Dragon Quest tradition); choices are shown as menu options.\n334\t- **Rig:** `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.0, build: 'chibi', ...look })`. Its head top must stay ≤ 28 units; assert in a test via `rig.worldOffset` of the head joint.\n335\t- **Stats:**\n336\t  - HP 40 (+10 per Heartpatch, ×3 in the story, so 70 max). Belly 100. Base attack 4.\n337\t  - Equipment slots: weapon, body, shield.\n338\t  - No experience levels. Growth comes from Heartpatches, gear and the town (as in DQB1).\n339\t- **Movement (tunable):** walk 78, sprint ×1.45 (Belly drains ×1.5), accel 900, friction 10 when no input, jump 215, air control 0.6, coyote 0.08 s, jump buffer 0.12 s.\n340\t- **Swing** (□ / LMB / J), using the equipped weapon:\n341\t  - A tap swings a 2-hit combo (`E.Combo(['slash','backslash'], { window: .3 })`): 0.38 s per swing, active from 0.35 to 0.6 of it.\n342\t  - Each swing hits monsters in a 70° arc within 22 units (`E.inArc` with `dz` ±14).\n343\t  - If no monster is in the arc, the swing hits the **break target**:\n344\t    1. It deals the weapon's `blockDmg`; the block's HP is `DQ.BLOCKS[id].hp`.\n345\t    2. Crack overlays show at 33%, 66% and 90%.\n346\t    3. At 0 HP the block breaks: fabric-scrap particles in its tones, a *rrrip* sfx, and the drop spawns.\n347\t    4. Blocks above a tool's tier go *clink*: sparks, no damage, and the prompt \"Too tough for your Darning Stick!\"\n348\t- **Spin Stitch** (unlocked in M5): hold swing for 0.6 s. A charge glow and rising tone play. Release for a 360° `spin` move: 1.5× damage, knockback 180, radius 26. It does not break blocks.\n349\t- **Place** (△ / RMB / K), using the selected hotbar item:\n350\t  - **Block:** set at the place target, if it is in reach (4.5 blocks from chest to cell centre), empty, not inside a body, and inside the world. The block pops in with a squash tween (scale 0.6 → 1.08 → 1 over 0.12 s), a stitch spark and a \"stitch\" sfx.\n351\t  - **Object:** placed with facing = the builder's facing, snapped to 90°. A green or red ghost preview shows validity.\n352\t  - **Usable** (food, Heartpatch, Banner): use it.\n353\t  - A full hotbar is fine; place takes one item from the bag stack.\n354\t- **Interact** (○ / F / RMB on an NPC or object): talk, open a chest, use a station, sleep, man a ballista, travel by portal, pick up the Lost Bundle, plant the Banner (on the marked spot).\n355\t- **Eat:** use food from the hotbar or bag. Belly and HP restore (§7.3), with a munch ×3 animation.\n356\t- **Hurt:** i-frames 0.8 s, knockback 90, flash, `expr: 'wince'`, a rumble of .35.\n357\t- **KO** at 0 HP:\n358\t  1. The `'die'` pose plays, then an *unravel* effect: the rig dissolves into thread particles.\n359\t  2. Granny says \"Oh, dearie, you've come undone!\"\n360\t  3. Fade, then respawn at the Banner (or the start point before it exists) with full HP and Belly 60.\n361\t  4. A **Lost Bundle** stays at the KO spot (map marker) holding 50% (rounded down) of each material stack. Tools and key items are never lost. On Relaxed, nothing is lost. During the siege or boss, KO instead retries from the siege checkpoint (§6.12).\n362\t- **Belly** drains 1 point every 9 s of real time (100 → 0 in 15 minutes); sprint ×1.5, fighting ×1.2. At 0, HP drops 1 every 4 s. \"Your belly is rumbling!\" warnings show at 25% and 10%.\n363\t\n364\t### 6.4 Input, controls, prompts (controller first, keyboard and mouse native)\n365\t\n366\tAction presets (`DQ.Controls.PLAY`, `DQ.Controls.MENU`) are passed to `game.input.use(...)`. The engine keeps the d-pad and arrow keys on movement and menus. The tables below are the defaults; players rebind through the standard Controls screen (`menu.controls` names them).\n367\t\n368\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyphs | Keyboard / mouse |\n369\t|---|---|---|---|\n370\t| Move | Left stick / D-pad | LS / D-pad | WASD / arrows |\n371\t| Camera turn 90° | Right stick ←/→ (flick) | RS | Q / E |\n372\t| Zoom | Right stick ↑/↓ | RS | − / = |\n373\t| Reset camera | R3 | RS click | R |\n374\t| Jump (menus: confirm) | ✕ | A | Space (menus: Enter/Space) |\n375\t| Swing / break (hold: Spin Stitch) | □ | X | Left click (keyboard-only: J) |\n376\t| Place / use item | △ | Y | Right click (keyboard-only: K) |\n377\t| Talk / interact (menus: back) | ○ | B | F (menus: Esc/Backspace) |\n378\t| Hotbar previous / next | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Wheel, 1–8, [ / ] |\n379\t| Sprint (hold; toggle in Settings) | R2 | RT | Left Shift |\n380\t| Builder's Eye (precision cursor, hold) | L2 | LT | C |\n381\t| Satchel (Bag, Map, Journal) | Create | View | Tab / I (Bag), M (Map), N (Journal) |\n382\t| Map shortcut | Touchpad click (button 17, if reported) | — | M |\n383\t| Pause menu | Options | Menu | Esc / P |\n384\t\n385\t- **Cursor modes** (`Controls.cursorMode`):\n386\t  - **Auto** (stick or keys used last). The target cell is the neighbour cell in the builder's facing, rounded to 8 directions.\n387\t    - Break target: the first solid block in that cell at level L0 (feet), then L0+1, then L0−1.\n388\t    - Place target: L0−1 if it is empty (fills holes), else L0, else L0+1, else L0+2. So walls 3 high can be stacked from the ground and pits get filled.\n389\t  - **Mouse** (the mouse moved or clicked in the last 2 s). The target follows `world.pickScreen` under the pointer. LMB swings at that block and RMB places there. The builder turns to face the target.\n390\t  - **Builder's Eye** (hold L2 / C). The builder stands still and a precision cursor appears at the auto target.\n391\t    - Stick, d-pad or WASD step the cursor one cell (repeat .16 s), clamped to reach.\n392\t    - R1 or Space raises the cursor one level; R2 or Shift lowers it.\n393\t    - Swing and place act at the cursor.\n394\t    - On screen: a grid of stitched dots around the cursor and a level number.\n395\t- **Cursor display:** a stitched wireframe cube (12 projected edges, 1-px dashed cream thread, pulsing). The break target highlights the block's faces. The place target shows a 50%-alpha ghost of the held block or object. Out of reach, it turns red and dashed, with \"Too far\".\n396\t- **Glyph icons** (`DQ.Glyphs.draw(g, actionOrCode, x, y)`). The engine font lacks ✕○□△, so the game draws its own 9×9 pixel icons:\n397\t  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#55dca8`, on dark discs.\n398\t  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow letters on discs.\n399\t  - L1/R1/L2/R2/LB/RB/LT/RT as rounded rectangles. Sticks as rings with L or R. Options/Create as pills.\n400\t  - Keyboard keycaps are rounded light-grey rectangles with the key's label from `input.label()`; Space is wide.\n401\t  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right button filled, or a wheel.\n402\t  - Which family shows follows `input.lastDevice`: gamepad → `settings.glyphs` (PlayStation default, Xbox optional); keyboard → keycaps; mouse → mouse icons for mouse-bound actions and keycaps for the rest.\n403\t  - Every prompt is built from `game.input.label(action)`, never hard-coded.\n404\t- **Context prompts** (bottom left, at most 3): e.g. `□ Break Earth`, `△ Place Cobble`, `○ Talk to Wren`, `○ Craft`, `○ Sleep`, `○ Man the Ballista`. They change with the cursor target and the hotbar item.\n405\t- **Menus:** all of the game's own screens use `MENU` actions. Mouse hover moves the focus, click confirms, wheel scrolls lists. L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs.\n406\t- **Rumble:** hits .35, block break .15, boss slam .8 (respects the vibration setting).\n407\t- **AI Hub rule:** while Create (Pad8) is held in a session, ignore Options. In a session, the Satchel opens on Create *release* if Options was not pressed during the hold.\n408\t- **Touch-only devices:** see §6.22.\n409\t- **Action map** (`DQ.Controls.PLAY`, in the engine's preset format `{ action: [codes] }`; the engine adds `up/down/left/right/start/pause/confirm/cancel` to every preset):\n410\t  ```js\n411\t  jump: ['Space','Pad0'], swing: ['Mouse0','KeyJ','Pad2'], place: ['Mouse2','KeyK','Pad3'], interact: ['KeyF','Pad1'],\n412\t  sprint: ['ShiftLeft','Pad7'], eye: ['KeyC','Pad6'], hotPrev: ['BracketLeft','Pad4'], hotNext: ['BracketRight','Pad5'],\n413\t  hot1..hot8: ['Digit1'..'Digit8'], camLeft: ['KeyQ'], camRight: ['KeyE'], zoomIn: ['Equal'], zoomOut: ['Minus'],\n414\t  camReset: ['KeyR','Pad11'], satchel: ['Tab','KeyI','Pad8'], map: ['KeyM','Pad17'], journal: ['KeyN']\n415\t  ```\n416\t  - The right stick is read from `input.padAim`: flicks give `camLeft`/`camRight` through `input.flick('x')`, and holding ↑/↓ zooms (one step per 0.35 s). The wheel comes from `input.wheelSteps()`.\n417\t  - `DQ.Controls.MENU` uses the engine's defaults, plus `tabPrev: ['KeyQ','Pad4']` and `tabNext: ['KeyE','Pad5']`.\n418\t  - Verify that the browser's context menu (right click) and Tab focus moves are suppressed on the canvas. The engine prevents defaults for bound keys; check this in the browser suite.\n419\t- **Intent** (`Controls.intent()` → one plain object per step). This is the only thing `Builder.update` reads, so the player, the attract AI, the tests and the Bee bots all drive the same code path:\n420\t  ```js\n421\t  { move: [gx, gy],            // ground direction, length ≤ 1 (already turned through view.screenDirToGround)\n422\t    jump, jumpHeld, sprint,     // booleans\n423\t    swing, swingHeld, swingReleased, place, interact,\n424\t    hot: null | -1 | +1 | 0..7, // previous/next or a direct slot\n425\t    eye: false,                 // Builder's Eye held\n426\t    cursor: null | { x, y, z }, // an explicit target cell (mouse pick, Builder's Eye, AI)\n427\t    camTurn: 0 | -1 | 1, zoom: 0 | -1 | 1 }\n428\t  ```\n429\t\n430\t### 6.5 Items, bag, hotbar, chests\n431\t- **Bag:** 40 slots, stacks of 99. Key items are separate and unlimited.\n432\t- **Hotbar:** 8 slots that reference item ids in the bag, DQB style. A count of 0 shows a greyed slot. Pickups fill an empty hotbar slot with new placeable or usable items.\n433\t- **Pickups:** broken blocks and defeated monsters drop item entities that bounce out, bob and spin. After 0.3 s they magnetize to a builder within 1.5 blocks and are auto-picked with a \"pickup\" blip. A toast shows \"+3 Straw\" (stacked toasts, bottom right). If the bag is full, the drop stays and the toast says \"Your bag is full!\". Drops despawn after 5 minutes.\n434\t- **Chests:** 20 slots. Interact opens a two-pane screen (bag | chest) with \"Store all materials\".\n435\t- **Town Chest:** from Town Level 2, any chest inside the border can be made the Town Chest (interact → \"Make this the Town Chest\"). Residents put gifts in and take materials out of it for Patterns, and Marigold cooks from it. Exactly one Town Chest exists.\n436\t\n437\t### 6.6 Crafting, stations, Notions\n438\t- **Stations:** Pocket (from the Bag tab \"Pocket Notions\"), Mending Bench, Hearth Pot, Thimble Forge. A station screen lists known recipes for that station.\n439\t  - Each row: icon, name, and ingredients as icons with have/need counts. Have ≥ need shows white with a tick icon, otherwise red with a cross icon (counts in text too, never colour alone). The pixel font has no ✓ or ✗, so draw them as `DQ.Glyphs` icons.\n440\t  - Quantity picker ×1, ×5 or Max. Confirm crafts.\n441\t  - At most 6 rows per page. A large detail pane shows the description.\n442\t- **Crafting feedback:** the builder hammers at the station (a `bash` loop for 0.5 s), sparkles fly, a \"ta-da\" chime plays, and the item's icon pops above the station. Crafted items go into the bag.\n443\t- **Notions** (inspiration). Each recipe has `notion: { on: 'pickup'|'quest'|'level'|'talk'|'start', key }`. The first time the trigger fires:\n444\t  1. The recipe unlocks.\n445\t  2. The builder strikes a thinking pose (a `kneel` blend for 0.4 s, then `'cheer'`).\n446\t  3. A thought bubble shows the item icon above the head for 2 s.\n447\t  4. A harp-gliss jingle plays.\n448\t  5. The popup reads \"**You've got a notion!** Straw Bed. Craft it at a Mending Bench.\"\n449\t  - Popups queue and never overlap.\n450\t  - The Journal's Notions page lists all known recipes.\n451\t\n452\t### 6.7 Objects\n453\tData is in §7.5. Behaviour:\n454\t- **Doors** (2 levels high, 1 cell): they swing open with a tween when a builder or resident is within 1 cell, and close after they leave. Monsters treat closed doors as walls and attack them (door HP 30/50). A broken door drops nothing and breaks the room.\n455\t- **Lights** (torch, lantern, Glow Patch, hearth, forge, banner): each frame, add `game.lights.add(x, y, z+12, radius, intensity, { color })` for lights within the view plus 2 cells. Torch flames come from the engine's shape `flames`. Torches count as room lights.\n456\t- **Beds:** interact at night (19:00–05:00) with no monster within 8 blocks: fade to black, a \"Zzz\" jingle, and time jumps to 06:00. HP is restored, Belly −10, and an autosave runs. During the day: \"It's not bedtime yet!\" Residents use beds at night (§6.10).\n457\t- **Stations:** interact opens crafting. A station must be inside the town border to count for rooms; it works anywhere.\n458\t- **Pin Trap:** a floor object. Monsters on it take 8 damage every 0.8 s and are slowed ×0.6. Builders and residents are unharmed.\n459\t- **Bobbin Ballista** (HP 150):\n460\t  - Unmanned: during a siege it auto-fires at the nearest regular monster within 10 blocks every 5 s (20 damage); it never targets bosses.\n461\t  - Manned (interact):\n462\t    1. The builder sits behind it and the camera eases toward the ballista's facing.\n463\t    2. Stick or mouse aims within ±60°, with aim-assist snapping to the nearest monster in a 12° cone.\n464\t    3. □/LMB fires a spool bolt: 260 u/s, 30 damage, pierces 1. It counts as a *Tangle hit* on Gnarlgrim.\n465\t    4. Reload takes 2.5 s (a crank animation plus a click-clack).\n466\t    5. ○ dismounts.\n467\t  - When destroyed it becomes a \"Broken Ballista\". Interact with 2 Planks to repair it fully.\n468\t- **Bobbin Portals** (prebuilt at POIs): touching one *discovers* it (a jingle and \"Portal attuned: Corduroy Crags\"). Interact opens a travel list of discovered portals: a swirl transition to the destination, ending in front of that portal.\n469\t- **Plants** (tufts, cotton puffs, berry bushes, flowers, puffballs, dead bushes): object-plants that break in one hit with any tool and drop items (§7.3). They regrow at their original spot after 1 in-game day if the cell is still free.\n470\t- **Ancient Buttons** (S3 collectibles): big golden button objects that glint. Interact to collect.\n471\t- **The Banner of Mending:** interact opens the Banner screen: town name, level, a Cheer bar to the next level, the room list, residents and their mood, and the next unlock.\n472\t\n473\t### 6.8 Rooms (`DQ.Rooms`)\n474\t**The rule** (as in the source): a room is an enclosed floor area of **2 to 64 cells**, surrounded on every side by walls **at least 2 blocks high**. It must have **at least one door** in its perimeter and **at least one light** inside. A roof is optional. The room must be inside the town border to count for Cheer, but rooms are detected anywhere so the HUD can say \"Rooms only count inside the town border!\"\n475\t\n476\t**Algorithm** (debounced 0.3 s after any `blockPlaced`, `blockBroken`, `objectPlaced` or `objectRemoved` inside the border + 2 cells):\n477\t1. Collect candidate floor levels L in the region: levels where a cell has solid(L−1) and air at L and L+1, or an object at L that is not a door.\n478\t2. For each L, classify every cell in the region:\n479\t   - WALL: opaque solid blocks at both L and L+1.\n480\t   - DOOR: a door object at L, set in a straight wall: the two cells beside it along the wall are WALL or DOOR. A door on a corner does not count.\n481\t   - FLOOR: not solid at L and L+1, solid below, an object allowed.\n482\t   - OPEN: anything else, including low walls of 1 block and void.\n483\t3. Flood-fill FLOOR cells (4-connected). A component is a room if it:\n484\t   - never reaches an OPEN cell, a region edge or the border;\n485\t   - has a size of 2–64;\n486\t   - touches ≥ 1 DOOR;\n487\t   - contains ≥ 1 light object.\n488\t4. Classify it by the objects on its cells against `DQ.ROOMS`, in priority order. The first match wins; otherwise it is a **Snug**.\n489\t5. Match it to the previous room set by cell overlap ≥ 70%. Emit `roomFormed` (new, or its type changed) and `roomLost`.\n490\t6. **Cheer** is awarded the first time a room of each type forms in a \"slot\". A room's identity is its type plus a hash of its rounded centre, so rebuilding the same room does not pay again; each type pays at most `maxAwards` times (§7.6).\n491\t7. On `roomFormed`: a big centred banner (\"ROOM COMPLETE! Bedroom\") in stitched letters, a fanfare jingle, confetti scraps over the room, and a room-name label floating over its centre for 3 s. Residents react with `cheer` poses.\n492\t\n493\tThe overlay \"Dev → Show rooms\" tints room cells by type and shows OPEN leak cells in red with a cross icon. Use it to explain failures.\n494\t\n495\t**Leak hints:** when a nearly enclosed area fails, the builder looks at the Banner screen or uses Builder's Eye over it. The HUD names the first missing requirement: \"Missing: a door\" / \"a light\" / \"walls must be 2 blocks high\" / \"too big (max 64)\".\n496\t\n497\t### 6.9 Town: Banner, border, Cheer, levels, Patterns (`DQ.Town`)\n498\t- **Planting.** Use the Banner on the marked plaza spot in Thimbleton. That runs a cutscene: the banner rises, a golden light ring expands, and the **border stitch** runs around the town square.\n499\t  - The border is a square of side `2r+1` around the banner, with `r` from §7.7."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"66500978-1efa-48d6-8035-21bca65cd9e4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:55:40.641Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","content":"The engine's `TileMap` is a heightmap: one wall type per cell, no passages under things. Building needs real 3D blocks (roofs, bridges, tunnels, mixed-material walls), so the game has its own `VoxelWorld`. It plugs into the engine where the engine expects a map.\n\n#### 6.1.1 Data\n- Block size `T = 16` world units (one tile). Axes: x east, y south, z up.\n- Story world: `W = 144, D = 144` columns, `H = 24` levels. Bee world: `48 × 48 × 16`.\n- `vox: Uint8Array(W*D*H)`, column-major: `idx(x,y,z) = (y*W + x)*H + z`, so a column is contiguous.\n- `meta: Map<idx, {rot, hp}>` holds stair rotation and partial damage. Damage heals back after 4 s without hits.\n- `colVer: Uint32Array(W*D)` holds per-column versions for render caches. `topZ: Uint8Array(W*D)` is the highest solid level + 1, kept on edit.\n- **Objects** (furniture, doors, lights, plants) are *not* voxels. They live in `DQ.Objects` with an anchor cell `(x,y,z)` and occupy that cell (doors and portals occupy 2 levels). `world.objAt(x,y,z)` is indexed by `Map<idx, obj>`.\n- Block properties from `DQ.BLOCKS`: `solid`, `opaque`, `liquid`, `hp`, `tier`, `drop`, `tex`, `light`, `shape` (`'cube' | 'stair'`), `placeable`.\n- **Level 0 is Backing Cloth** (unbreakable). Below and outside the world is the **Unravel**, a void. Anything falling below z = −16 (through a void hole or off the world's edge) respawns at its last safe position after a 0.6 s \"unravel\" effect. The builder takes 4 damage.\n\n#### 6.1.2 API (`DQ.VoxelWorld`)\n```\nget(x,y,z) → id            set(x,y,z,id,{by, silent}) → bool (emits blockPlaced/blockBroken, bumps colVer of the column and 4 neighbours)\nsolidAt(x,y,z)  opaqueAt(x,y,z)  liquidAt(x,y,z)   (integer cells; outside the world and below level 0 is void, which reads as air)\ntopZ(x,y)                  standLevels(x,y) → levels L where solid(L-1) and air(L), air(L+1)\ngroundAt(px,py,r,z,step)   highest solid top ≤ z+step under the circle footprint, −Infinity over void (world units; feeds E.Body-style physics)\nceilingAt(px,py,r,z)       lowest solid bottom above z within the footprint (head bumps)\ncollide(body)              push a circle out of blocks overlapping [z+step, z+h) (same algorithm as TileMap.collide, per level)\nraycast(o,d,maxT) → {x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, t} | null          3D DDA (Amanatides–Woo) through cells\npickScreen(sx,sy) → {x,y,z, n:[nx,ny,nz], obj} | null     view ray through the screen pixel (see 6.1.5)\nbounds(view) → {x0,y0,x1,y1}                             camera clamp (as TileMap.bounds)\nT, solids=[] , _liftOrder(view,x,y,z,k)                  the renderer's hooks (see 6.1.4)\ndiff() → [[idx, id], ...]   applyDiff(list)               save and network: cells that differ from the generated base world\nhash() → u32                                              tests and net convergence\n```\nGeneration writes `base` (a copy of `vox` taken after `generateWorld`), so `diff()` is a cheap scan. Saves store only the diff (typically under 30 KB).\n\n#### 6.1.3 Rendering: face-sprite blitting per block, queued per column run\n- **Face atlas.** For each `(view id, zoom step)` the renderer pre-renders a small sprite for every material and visible face: `top`, `faceA` (the camera-facing side on the viewer's left) and `faceB` (right). In three-quarter view there is `top` and `front`. Each material gets 4 variants (picked by `hash(x,y,z) & 3`).\n  - Sprites are rasterized per pixel with `E.tones(base)`. The pattern functions are in §8.1. Each face is an exact parallelogram of the projected block face, snapped to whole pixels. All blocks of a view and zoom project to the same shapes, so one atlas serves the whole world. It is rebuilt only when the zoom step changes. A 90° turn keeps iso faces identical, and the renderer only swaps which world face maps to A/B.\n  - Light: top = `base`, faceA = `sh`, faceB = `deep`, following the engine's \"light from the viewer's upper left\".\n- **Zoom steps.** Zoom is quantized to `[0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]` (default 1). Our own zoom actions (− / =, right stick ↑/↓) step through them with `game.setZoom(step)`. The play scene sets `camera: { zoom: false }`, so the engine's continuous player zoom stays off and the atlas only ever needs these five scales. The chosen step is remembered in our settings (`dqZoom`).\n- **Run queueing.** For each visible column, split its opaque solid blocks into runs `[z0, z1)`. Queue each run once:\n  ```js\n  r.queue(cx*16+8, cy*16+8, z0*16, g => drawRun(g, r, cx, cy, z0, z1), { occluder: true, top: z1*16 })\n  ```\n  `drawRun` blits, bottom to top, each block's visible faces: a side face shows where the neighbour column is not opaque at that level, and the top face shows where the block above is not opaque or is cut away. Then come overlays: crack stages on damaged blocks, contact AO (a 2-px dark band on side faces at a run's bottom where it meets the ground), top-face edge AO (a dark band along an edge whose neighbour is one level higher), and sun-shadow tint on tops that have a taller column within 2 cells toward the light. Overlays use `px.blend(g, a, 'multiply', ...)` with shared per-view overlay sprites.\n- **Visible columns.** Invert the four screen corners to the ground plane at `z = 0` and at `z = H*16`. Take the cell bounding box plus 2 cells of margin, then clamp it. That is about 500–700 columns at zoom 1.\n- **Liquids.** Water blocks are queued separately: `r.queue(..., z*16, drawWater)`. The water top is drawn with `px.blend(.7)` and a 4-frame shimmer, plus side faces next to air. Blocks under water get a blue multiply tint.\n- **Void and fringe.** Behind the world, draw a deep-navy gradient (`r.sky(['#06051a','#0d0b2a','#141238'])`) with drifting cream \"lint motes\" and a faint stitched grid. Edge columns and void holes get frayed fringe threads: 1-px lines hanging 1–2 blocks below the lowest block, swaying with time. These are queued just before their column.\n- **Cutaway** (seeing inside roofs, tunnels and canopies). Each frame, set `cut = heroLevel + 2` when an opaque block sits at a level in `[heroLevel+2, heroLevel+6]` in either of these places:\n  - the hero's own column;\n  - any of the 3 columns toward the camera. Step from the hero's cell by `(round(view.fx), round(view.fy))`, the ground direction that points at the camera; in iso both components are ±1.\n\n  Ordinary 2-high room walls (levels `heroLevel` and `heroLevel+1`) are never cut; the x-ray silhouette shows the hero behind them.\n\n  Blocks at `z ≥ cut` within radius 6.5 cells of the hero are skipped. A 1.5-cell band at the edge is drawn every other pixel (`px.polyDither`). A block just below the cut with a block above it gets a \"cross-section\" overlay: a darker top with a diagonal stitch hatch. The cut eases in and out over 0.2 s (the radius grows from 0). It stays on for 0.5 s after the hero leaves cover (hysteresis), so it does not flicker at doorways. Dev toggle: \"Cutaway: auto / always / off\".\n- **X-ray.** The builder, remote players and bosses use `r.actor(..., { xray: { color, actors: false } })`. They show as tinted silhouettes behind blocks (the engine does this through `occluder` queue items).\n- **Draw cost guard.** No baking hitches: blits are cheap. If a frame queues more than 2,500 runs (extreme zoom-out), draw the far columns' top faces only.\n\n#### 6.1.4 Depth sorting\nThe renderer sorts by `view.order(x,y,z) = fx*x + fy*y + z*1e-3`. Runs in one column sort bottom to top, and columns sort by depth. For things that stand on blocks or sit under overhangs, the renderer calls `r._map._liftOrder(view, x, y, z, k)` for every queued item above `z > .5`. `VoxelRenderer.queue` sets `r._map = world`. Call it before queuing any actor or object: the renderer reads `r._map` at queue time and resets it to null at the start of every frame. Implement both directions:\n- **Lift.** If the item's cell has a run whose top is `≤ z+1` and it is the highest such run, return `max(k, order(center, run.z0*16) + .002 + z*1e-5)`.\n- **Sink.** If the item's cell has a run with `z0*16 ≥ z + 20` (an overhang above its head), return `min(k, order(center, thatRun.z0*16) - .002)`.\n\nAlso provide `world.T = 16` and `world.solids = []` (the renderer reads both). Test with a diorama (§11.2): a hero under a roof, on a bridge with a body below, beside and behind 2- and 3-high walls, on stairs, and in a doorway. Write a filmstrip check in all 4 rotations.\n\n#### 6.1.5 Picking (mouse and touch-free targeting)\nUse the line of sight through a screen pixel, as `TileMap.pick` computes it: P and N from the view rows. Clip to the world box and run the 3D DDA from the camera side. The first opaque or solid block hit, with the entry face normal, is the **break target**. `hit + normal` is the **place target**. Objects take part through `world.objAt` (placed objects count as hit targets, so the mouse can click a bench or a bed). Actors are picked with the engine's `{ pick: entity }` on `r.actor`/`r.shape` and `r.pick(sx, sy)` (the last frame's draws). Actor picks win over blocks when both are under the pointer. Respect the cutaway: cut blocks are not pickable.\n\n#### 6.1.6 Physics (`DQ.VoxelBody extends E.Body`)\n- Builder body: `r = 5, h = 28` (fits under a 2-block ceiling), `eye = 22, chest = 16`, `step = 2`, `gravity = 800`.\n- **`update(dt, world)`.** Run `E.Body.update` (it calls `world.collide` and `world.groundAt`). Then check the ceiling: if `vz > 0` and `z + h > ceilingAt(...)`, clamp `z` and set `vz = 0`.\n  - **Stairs:** in a stair cell the ground is a ramp along the stair's `rot` (0 → 16 over the cell), so walking up stairs needs no jump.\n  - **Water:** in water, speed ×0.55. A column with water 2 or more levels deep blocks walkers like a wall (bodies cannot enter, and a falling body is pushed out to `lastSafe`). The builder cannot swim, as in the source.\n- **Auto-hop** (setting, on by default). Moving into a 1-block ledge with 2 free levels above it, on the ground, for over 0.08 s triggers `jump(215)` with a small forward boost.\n- **Safe positions.** Store `lastSafe` every 0.5 s while standing on solid ground that is not near a void edge.\n- **No block may be placed inside a body.** `Builder.place` checks every builder, resident and monster AABB first, then shows \"Something's in the way!\"\n- **`world.escape(body)`** moves a buried body to the nearest standable cell within 4 cells. It runs after any set() that could bury something (worldgen stamps, Tangle blocks).\n\n### 6.2 Camera and views\n- `game.follow(builder.body, { z: 14, lead: 18 })`, with `game.cam.bounds = v => world.bounds(v)` and `cam.smooth = .12`.\n- **Rotation:**\n  - Q/E keys, or a right-stick flick (`input.flick('x')`), turn by ±90° with `game.rotateView(±90)`.\n  - Cover the snap with a 0.18 s crossfade: grab the previous frame's buffer before turning and blend it out with `px.blend` on the overlay. Play a soft \"whoosh\" (`sfx('whoosh', {vol:.2})`).\n  - Movement keeps working in screen space (`view.screenDirToGround`).\n- **Zoom:** right stick ↑/↓ or `−`/`=` step through the zoom levels (§6.1.3). In play the mouse wheel belongs to the hotbar; it zooms only on the Map screen. R3/R resets rotation and zoom (`game.resetCamera()`, then zoom step 1).\n- **Settings → Camera view:** Isometric (default) or Three-quarter. It switches with `game.setView`; the atlas has entries for both.\n- Cutscenes take the camera with tweens on a proxy focus object and give it back on exit.\n\n### 6.3 The Builder (player)\n- **Look:** chosen at New Game. Look A: spiky hair and a bandana. Look B: ponytail and an apron. Four palette swatches. Name entry (default \"Patch\") on an on-screen letter grid that works with a pad, keys or mouse; typing letters works directly. The builder never speaks (Dragon Quest tradition); choices are shown as menu options.\n- **Rig:** `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.0, build: 'chibi', ...look })`. Its head top must stay ≤ 28 units; assert in a test via `rig.worldOffset` of the head joint.\n- **Stats:**\n  - HP 40 (+10 per Heartpatch, ×3 in the story, so 70 max). Belly 100. Base attack 4.\n  - Equipment slots: weapon, body, shield.\n  - No experience levels. Growth comes from Heartpatches, gear and the town (as in DQB1).\n- **Movement (tunable):** walk 78, sprint ×1.45 (Belly drains ×1.5), accel 900, friction 10 when no input, jump 215, air control 0.6, coyote 0.08 s, jump buffer 0.12 s.\n- **Swing** (□ / LMB / J), using the equipped weapon:\n  - A tap swings a 2-hit combo (`E.Combo(['slash','backslash'], { window: .3 })`): 0.38 s per swing, active from 0.35 to 0.6 of it.\n  - Each swing hits monsters in a 70° arc within 22 units (`E.inArc` with `dz` ±14).\n  - If no monster is in the arc, the swing hits the **break target**:\n    1. It deals the weapon's `blockDmg`; the block's HP is `DQ.BLOCKS[id].hp`.\n    2. Crack overlays show at 33%, 66% and 90%.\n    3. At 0 HP the block breaks: fabric-scrap particles in its tones, a *rrrip* sfx, and the drop spawns.\n    4. Blocks above a tool's tier go *clink*: sparks, no damage, and the prompt \"Too tough for your Darning Stick!\"\n- **Spin Stitch** (unlocked in M5): hold swing for 0.6 s. A charge glow and rising tone play. Release for a 360° `spin` move: 1.5× damage, knockback 180, radius 26. It does not break blocks.\n- **Place** (△ / RMB / K), using the selected hotbar item:\n  - **Block:** set at the place target, if it is in reach (4.5 blocks from chest to cell centre), empty, not inside a body, and inside the world. The block pops in with a squash tween (scale 0.6 → 1.08 → 1 over 0.12 s), a stitch spark and a \"stitch\" sfx.\n  - **Object:** placed with facing = the builder's facing, snapped to 90°. A green or red ghost preview shows validity.\n  - **Usable** (food, Heartpatch, Banner): use it.\n  - A full hotbar is fine; place takes one item from the bag stack.\n- **Interact** (○ / F / RMB on an NPC or object): talk, open a chest, use a station, sleep, man a ballista, travel by portal, pick up the Lost Bundle, plant the Banner (on the marked spot).\n- **Eat:** use food from the hotbar or bag. Belly and HP restore (§7.3), with a munch ×3 animation.\n- **Hurt:** i-frames 0.8 s, knockback 90, flash, `expr: 'wince'`, a rumble of .35.\n- **KO** at 0 HP:\n  1. The `'die'` pose plays, then an *unravel* effect: the rig dissolves into thread particles.\n  2. Granny says \"Oh, dearie, you've come undone!\"\n  3. Fade, then respawn at the Banner (or the start point before it exists) with full HP and Belly 60.\n  4. A **Lost Bundle** stays at the KO spot (map marker) holding 50% (rounded down) of each material stack. Tools and key items are never lost. On Relaxed, nothing is lost. During the siege or boss, KO instead retries from the siege checkpoint (§6.12).\n- **Belly** drains 1 point every 9 s of real time (100 → 0 in 15 minutes); sprint ×1.5, fighting ×1.2. At 0, HP drops 1 every 4 s. \"Your belly is rumbling!\" warnings show at 25% and 10%.\n\n### 6.4 Input, controls, prompts (controller first, keyboard and mouse native)\n\nAction presets (`DQ.Controls.PLAY`, `DQ.Controls.MENU`) are passed to `game.input.use(...)`. The engine keeps the d-pad and arrow keys on movement and menus. The tables below are the defaults; players rebind through the standard Controls screen (`menu.controls` names them).\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyphs | Keyboard / mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move | Left stick / D-pad | LS / D-pad | WASD / arrows |\n| Camera turn 90° | Right stick ←/→ (flick) | RS | Q / E |\n| Zoom | Right stick ↑/↓ | RS | − / = |\n| Reset camera | R3 | RS click | R |\n| Jump (menus: confirm) | ✕ | A | Space (menus: Enter/Space) |\n| Swing / break (hold: Spin Stitch) | □ | X | Left click (keyboard-only: J) |\n| Place / use item | △ | Y | Right click (keyboard-only: K) |\n| Talk / interact (menus: back) | ○ | B | F (menus: Esc/Backspace) |\n| Hotbar previous / next | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Wheel, 1–8, [ / ] |\n| Sprint (hold; toggle in Settings) | R2 | RT | Left Shift |\n| Builder's Eye (precision cursor, hold) | L2 | LT | C |\n| Satchel (Bag, Map, Journal) | Create | View | Tab / I (Bag), M (Map), N (Journal) |\n| Map shortcut | Touchpad click (button 17, if reported) | — | M |\n| Pause menu | Options | Menu | Esc / P |\n\n- **Cursor modes** (`Controls.cursorMode`):\n  - **Auto** (stick or keys used last). The target cell is the neighbour cell in the builder's facing, rounded to 8 directions.\n    - Break target: the first solid block in that cell at level L0 (feet), then L0+1, then L0−1.\n    - Place target: L0−1 if it is empty (fills holes), else L0, else L0+1, else L0+2. So walls 3 high can be stacked from the ground and pits get filled.\n  - **Mouse** (the mouse moved or clicked in the last 2 s). The target follows `world.pickScreen` under the pointer. LMB swings at that block and RMB places there. The builder turns to face the target.\n  - **Builder's Eye** (hold L2 / C). The builder stands still and a precision cursor appears at the auto target.\n    - Stick, d-pad or WASD step the cursor one cell (repeat .16 s), clamped to reach.\n    - R1 or Space raises the cursor one level; R2 or Shift lowers it.\n    - Swing and place act at the cursor.\n    - On screen: a grid of stitched dots around the cursor and a level number.\n- **Cursor display:** a stitched wireframe cube (12 projected edges, 1-px dashed cream thread, pulsing). The break target highlights the block's faces. The place target shows a 50%-alpha ghost of the held block or object. Out of reach, it turns red and dashed, with \"Too far\".\n- **Glyph icons** (`DQ.Glyphs.draw(g, actionOrCode, x, y)`). The engine font lacks ✕○□△, so the game draws its own 9×9 pixel icons:\n  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#55dca8`, on dark discs.\n  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow letters on discs.\n  - L1/R1/L2/R2/LB/RB/LT/RT as rounded rectangles. Sticks as rings with L or R. Options/Create as pills.\n  - Keyboard keycaps are rounded light-grey rectangles with the key's label from `input.label()`; Space is wide.\n  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right button filled, or a wheel.\n  - Which family shows follows `input.lastDevice`: gamepad → `settings.glyphs` (PlayStation default, Xbox optional); keyboard → keycaps; mouse → mouse icons for mouse-bound actions and keycaps for the rest.\n  - Every prompt is built from `game.input.label(action)`, never hard-coded.\n- **Context prompts** (bottom left, at most 3): e.g. `□ Break Earth`, `△ Place Cobble`, `○ Talk to Wren`, `○ Craft`, `○ Sleep`, `○ Man the Ballista`. They change with the cursor target and the hotbar item.\n- **Menus:** all of the game's own screens use `MENU` actions. Mouse hover moves the focus, click confirms, wheel scrolls lists. L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs.\n- **Rumble:** hits .35, block break .15, boss slam .8 (respects the vibration setting).\n- **AI Hub rule:** while Create (Pad8) is held in a session, ignore Options. In a session, the Satchel opens on Create *release* if Options was not pressed during the hold.\n- **Touch-only devices:** see §6.22.\n- **Action map** (`DQ.Controls.PLAY`, in the engine's preset format `{ action: [codes] }`; the engine adds `up/down/left/right/start/pause/confirm/cancel` to every preset):\n  ```js\n  jump: ['Space','Pad0'], swing: ['Mouse0','KeyJ','Pad2'], place: ['Mouse2','KeyK','Pad3'], interact: ['KeyF','Pad1'],\n  sprint: ['ShiftLeft','Pad7'], eye: ['KeyC','Pad6'], hotPrev: ['BracketLeft','Pad4'], hotNext: ['BracketRight','Pad5'],\n  hot1..hot8: ['Digit1'..'Digit8'], camLeft: ['KeyQ'], camRight: ['KeyE'], zoomIn: ['Equal'], zoomOut: ['Minus'],\n  camReset: ['KeyR','Pad11'], satchel: ['Tab','KeyI','Pad8'], map: ['KeyM','Pad17'], journal: ['KeyN']\n  ```\n  - The right stick is read from `input.padAim`: flicks give `camLeft`/`camRight` through `input.flick('x')`, and holding ↑/↓ zooms (one step per 0.35 s). The wheel comes from `input.wheelSteps()`.\n  - `DQ.Controls.MENU` uses the engine's defaults, plus `tabPrev: ['KeyQ','Pad4']` and `tabNext: ['KeyE','Pad5']`.\n  - Verify that the browser's context menu (right click) and Tab focus moves are suppressed on the canvas. The engine prevents defaults for bound keys; check this in the browser suite.\n- **Intent** (`Controls.intent()` → one plain object per step). This is the only thing `Builder.update` reads, so the player, the attract AI, the tests and the Bee bots all drive the same code path:\n  ```js\n  { move: [gx, gy],            // ground direction, length ≤ 1 (already turned through view.screenDirToGround)\n    jump, jumpHeld, sprint,     // booleans\n    swing, swingHeld, swingReleased, place, interact,\n    hot: null | -1 | +1 | 0..7, // previous/next or a direct slot\n    eye: false,                 // Builder's Eye held\n    cursor: null | { x, y, z }, // an explicit target cell (mouse pick, Builder's Eye, AI)\n    camTurn: 0 | -1 | 1, zoom: 0 | -1 | 1 }\n  ```\n\n### 6.5 Items, bag, hotbar, chests\n- **Bag:** 40 slots, stacks of 99. Key items are separate and unlimited.\n- **Hotbar:** 8 slots that reference item ids in the bag, DQB style. A count of 0 shows a greyed slot. Pickups fill an empty hotbar slot with new placeable or usable items.\n- **Pickups:** broken blocks and defeated monsters drop item entities that bounce out, bob and spin. After 0.3 s they magnetize to a builder within 1.5 blocks and are auto-picked with a \"pickup\" blip. A toast shows \"+3 Straw\" (stacked toasts, bottom right). If the bag is full, the drop stays and the toast says \"Your bag is full!\". Drops despawn after 5 minutes.\n- **Chests:** 20 slots. Interact opens a two-pane screen (bag | chest) with \"Store all materials\".\n- **Town Chest:** from Town Level 2, any chest inside the border can be made the Town Chest (interact → \"Make this the Town Chest\"). Residents put gifts in and take materials out of it for Patterns, and Marigold cooks from it. Exactly one Town Chest exists.\n\n### 6.6 Crafting, stations, Notions\n- **Stations:** Pocket (from the Bag tab \"Pocket Notions\"), Mending Bench, Hearth Pot, Thimble Forge. A station screen lists known recipes for that station.\n  - Each row: icon, name, and ingredients as icons with have/need counts. Have ≥ need shows white with a tick icon, otherwise red with a cross icon (counts in text too, never colour alone). The pixel font has no ✓ or ✗, so draw them as `DQ.Glyphs` icons.\n  - Quantity picker ×1, ×5 or Max. Confirm crafts.\n  - At most 6 rows per page. A large detail pane shows the description.\n- **Crafting feedback:** the builder hammers at the station (a `bash` loop for 0.5 s), sparkles fly, a \"ta-da\" chime plays, and the item's icon pops above the station. Crafted items go into the bag.\n- **Notions** (inspiration). Each recipe has `notion: { on: 'pickup'|'quest'|'level'|'talk'|'start', key }`. The first time the trigger fires:\n  1. The recipe unlocks.\n  2. The builder strikes a thinking pose (a `kneel` blend for 0.4 s, then `'cheer'`).\n  3. A thought bubble shows the item icon above the head for 2 s.\n  4. A harp-gliss jingle plays.\n  5. The popup reads \"**You've got a notion!** Straw Bed. Craft it at a Mending Bench.\"\n  - Popups queue and never overlap.\n  - The Journal's Notions page lists all known recipes.\n\n### 6.7 Objects\nData is in §7.5. Behaviour:\n- **Doors** (2 levels high, 1 cell): they swing open with a tween when a builder or resident is within 1 cell, and close after they leave. Monsters treat closed doors as walls and attack them (door HP 30/50). A broken door drops nothing and breaks the room.\n- **Lights** (torch, lantern, Glow Patch, hearth, forge, banner): each frame, add `game.lights.add(x, y, z+12, radius, intensity, { color })` for lights within the view plus 2 cells. Torch flames come from the engine's shape `flames`. Torches count as room lights.\n- **Beds:** interact at night (19:00–05:00) with no monster within 8 blocks: fade to black, a \"Zzz\" jingle, and time jumps to 06:00. HP is restored, Belly −10, and an autosave runs. During the day: \"It's not bedtime yet!\" Residents use beds at night (§6.10).\n- **Stations:** interact opens crafting. A station must be inside the town border to count for rooms; it works anywhere.\n- **Pin Trap:** a floor object. Monsters on it take 8 damage every 0.8 s and are slowed ×0.6. Builders and residents are unharmed.\n- **Bobbin Ballista** (HP 150):\n  - Unmanned: during a siege it auto-fires at the nearest regular monster within 10 blocks every 5 s (20 damage); it never targets bosses.\n  - Manned (interact):\n    1. The builder sits behind it and the camera eases toward the ballista's facing.\n    2. Stick or mouse aims within ±60°, with aim-assist snapping to the nearest monster in a 12° cone.\n    3. □/LMB fires a spool bolt: 260 u/s, 30 damage, pierces 1. It counts as a *Tangle hit* on Gnarlgrim.\n    4. Reload takes 2.5 s (a crank animation plus a click-clack).\n    5. ○ dismounts.\n  - When destroyed it becomes a \"Broken Ballista\". Interact with 2 Planks to repair it fully.\n- **Bobbin Portals** (prebuilt at POIs): touching one *discovers* it (a jingle and \"Portal attuned: Corduroy Crags\"). Interact opens a travel list of discovered portals: a swirl transition to the destination, ending in front of that portal.\n- **Plants** (tufts, cotton puffs, berry bushes, flowers, puffballs, dead bushes): object-plants that break in one hit with any tool and drop items (§7.3). They regrow at their original spot after 1 in-game day if the cell is still free.\n- **Ancient Buttons** (S3 collectibles): big golden button objects that glint. Interact to collect.\n- **The Banner of Mending:** interact opens the Banner screen: town name, level, a Cheer bar to the next level, the room list, residents and their mood, and the next unlock.\n\n### 6.8 Rooms (`DQ.Rooms`)\n**The rule** (as in the source): a room is an enclosed floor area of **2 to 64 cells**, surrounded on every side by walls **at least 2 blocks high**. It must have **at least one door** in its perimeter and **at least one light** inside. A roof is optional. The room must be inside the town border to count for Cheer, but rooms are detected anywhere so the HUD can say \"Rooms only count inside the town border!\"\n\n**Algorithm** (debounced 0.3 s after any `blockPlaced`, `blockBroken`, `objectPlaced` or `objectRemoved` inside the border + 2 cells):\n1. Collect candidate floor levels L in the region: levels where a cell has solid(L−1) and air at L and L+1, or an object at L that is not a door.\n2. For each L, classify every cell in the region:\n   - WALL: opaque solid blocks at both L and L+1.\n   - DOOR: a door object at L, set in a straight wall: the two cells beside it along the wall are WALL or DOOR. A door on a corner does not count.\n   - FLOOR: not solid at L and L+1, solid below, an object allowed.\n   - OPEN: anything else, including low walls of 1 block and void.\n3. Flood-fill FLOOR cells (4-connected). A component is a room if it:\n   - never reaches an OPEN cell, a region edge or the border;\n   - has a size of 2–64;\n   - touches ≥ 1 DOOR;\n   - contains ≥ 1 light object.\n4. Classify it by the objects on its cells against `DQ.ROOMS`, in priority order. The first match wins; otherwise it is a **Snug**.\n5. Match it to the previous room set by cell overlap ≥ 70%. Emit `roomFormed` (new, or its type changed) and `roomLost`.\n6. **Cheer** is awarded the first time a room of each type forms in a \"slot\". A room's identity is its type plus a hash of its rounded centre, so rebuilding the same room does not pay again; each type pays at most `maxAwards` times (§7.6).\n7. On `roomFormed`: a big centred banner (\"ROOM COMPLETE! Bedroom\") in stitched letters, a fanfare jingle, confetti scraps over the room, and a room-name label floating over its centre for 3 s. Residents react with `cheer` poses.\n\nThe overlay \"Dev → Show rooms\" tints room cells by type and shows OPEN leak cells in red with a cross icon. Use it to explain failures.\n\n**Leak hints:** when a nearly enclosed area fails, the builder looks at the Banner screen or uses Builder's Eye over it. The HUD names the first missing requirement: \"Missing: a door\" / \"a light\" / \"walls must be 2 blocks high\" / \"too big (max 64)\".\n\n### 6.9 Town: Banner, border, Cheer, levels, Patterns (`DQ.Town`)\n- **Planting.** Use the Banner on the marked plaza spot in Thimbleton. That runs a cutscene: the banner rises, a golden light ring expands, and the **border stitch** runs around the town square.\n  - The border is a square of side `2r+1` around the banner, with `r` from §7.7.","numLines":250,"startLine":250,"totalLines":1675}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EPW1Z9PzU5hSNnCRVtLxoY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghHvcMqGWYjMAbo3UJiR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012q1NanVCHn6Mq1Js9a2xzX","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","offset":500,"limit":260},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10433,"cache_read_input_tokens":24533,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10433},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"cdf33676-27b0-43cd-958c-334edc280ef8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:55:41.453Z","request_id":"req_011CghHvc8wbPoNqT5Hz1hTp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012q1NanVCHn6Mq1Js9a2xzX":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","offset":500,"limit":260}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012q1NanVCHn6Mq1Js9a2xzX","type":"tool_result","content":"500\t  - On the ground it is an animated dashed running stitch (cream thread, 2-px dashes moving along the edge), drawn at each border cell's surface height.\n501\t  - In the void, at night, it glows faintly.\n502\t- **Cheer sources:** rooms (§7.6), quest rewards, and Cheer bubbles from residents (+5 each, §6.10).\n503\t  - Collecting a bubble: it flies to the HUD banner icon, which pulses, with a two-note \"ding\".\n504\t  - On reaching a level threshold:\n505\t    1. Town Level up (a cutscene of about 4 s): the banner glows, a new stripe stitches itself onto it, and the border grows outward in an animated sweep.\n506\t    2. A popup lists the unlocks.\n507\t    3. The Lv-up jingle plays.\n508\t    4. The next resident arrives at the following dawn (or immediately if it is daytime, walking in from the east road).\n509\t- **Patterns** (blueprints):\n510\t  - **Pattern data:** `{ id, name, size:[w,d,h], layers: [ASCII rows per level], legend: { ch: blockKey | {obj, facing} }, reward }`.\n511\t  - **Placement:** the player uses a Pattern scroll (key item) and a ghost footprint follows the cursor. Turn it 90° with L1/R1 (pad), or the mouse wheel or [ / ] (keyboard); the camera still turns with Q/E and the right stick. Confirm on valid ground inside the border: flat ± 1 level, no objects in the way (blocks in the way are OK; they are shown as \"remove\").\n512\t  - **Ghosts:** each unbuilt cell draws a translucent ghost of its target block (`px.blend(.35)`) with a dashed outline; wrong blocks get a red cross icon. The HUD shows a panel: \"Quilters' Hall: 37/129\", then the materials still needed (have/need, e.g. Cobble 12/35).\n513\t  - **Building:** placing the right block into a ghost cell counts it, with a sparkle and a short arpeggio. Objects in ghost cells (door, lanterns) count the same way.\n514\t  - **Helpers:** residents with the `helper` job (Professor, Wren, and Mortimer by day) take the needed materials from the Town Chest. Each walks to an unbuilt ghost cell they can reach and places a block with a hammer animation and a \"tok\". Rate: `tuning.helperRate` blocks/min each (default 6). If the chest lacks materials, they say so in a balloon (\"Need Planks!\") and idle.\n515\t  - **Completion:** when every cell matches, a celebration cutscene plays (§7.10) and the reward and Cheer are granted. The structure counts as a special room (\"Quilters' Hall\") if its rules match (§7.6).\n516\t\n517\t### 6.10 Residents (`DQ.Residents`)\n518\t- Each resident is a `VoxelBody` plus a `Humanoid` or `Blob` rig, with a profile from `DQ.NPCS` (§7.8): `job`, `schedule`, `needs`, `voice`, `fighter` stats, and dialog nodes.\n519\t- **Schedule** (hours of the day clock; each block picks a target):\n520\t\n521\t  | Time | Activity |\n522\t  |---|---|\n523\t  | 06–08 | Wake, stretch (`wave` pose), go to the plaza |\n524\t  | 08–18 | Job: see below; between tasks, wander inside the border |\n525\t  | 18–20 | Supper: sit at a Mess Hall stool if one exists and eat (+bubble) |\n526\t  | 20–21 | Socialise near the Banner (talk balloons `note`, `dots`) |\n527\t  | 21–06 | Sleep in a free bed in a Bedroom (+bubble at 06:00); without one, sleep on the ground beside the Banner (mood −1, a \"No bed...\" balloon) |\n528\t- **Jobs:**\n529\t  - **Wren** (gatherer): each morning she puts 3 random basics (straw, branch, cotton) into the Town Chest. In sieges she uses a slingshot (4 damage, range 6 blocks, 1.2 s).\n530\t  - **Mortimer** (guard): patrols the border at night with a lantern and fights monsters inside the border with a club (8 damage, `overhead`). In sieges he leads the defence. He needs a Guard Post to stand watch from (+bubble).\n531\t  - **Marigold** (cook): with a Kitchen, cooks at the Hearth Pot (stirring loop) twice a day from Town Chest ingredients into the chest. At 18:00 she sets a dish on the Mess Hall table. In sieges she throws hot pies (5 damage, arc).\n532\t  - **Professor Selvage** (helper): builds Patterns from the Town Chest at the helper rate, or reads in the Workshop. In sieges he hides in the Hall.\n533\t  - **Lintwick** (fluff-farmer): puts 2 Lint into the Town Chest daily and dances (a Blob hop loop) when happy. In sieges he rolls into monsters (3 damage + knockback).\n534\t- **Needs and Cheer bubbles.** At most once per day per need, each satisfied need spawns a floating pink heart bubble (+5 Cheer) over the resident, which stays until collected:\n535\t  - sleep: slept in a bed\n536\t  - meal: ate a dish\n537\t  - comfort: their favourite room exists (Wren: Bedroom, Mortimer: Guard Post, Marigold: Kitchen, Professor: Workshop, Lintwick: a Bedroom with a Felt block in its walls)\n538\t\n539\t  Collect a bubble by walking within 2 blocks of it. Unhappy residents show a `dots` balloon and say why when you talk to them.\n540\t- **Requests:** a resident with an available quest shows a bouncing \"!\" balloon (`story.emote`). Talking starts it, and its steps appear in the Journal and HUD. When the steps are done, a \"?\" balloon shows; talk to them to turn it in.\n541\t- **Arrival cutscene:** the resident walks in along the east road, the camera pans to them, and they say their intro line. \"Mortimer has moved into Thimbleton!\" shows with a jingle.\n542\t- **Navigation** (`DQ.Nav`):\n543\t  - **Graph:** nodes are standable `(x, y, L)`. Edges:\n544\t    - 8-neighbour moves (diagonals only if both orthogonal neighbours are open), cost 1 (diagonal 1.41)\n545\t    - step up 1 level (a hop), cost 2\n546\t    - drop down 1–3 levels, cost 1.5\n547\t    - doors open for residents, closed for monsters\n548\t  - **A\\*** with a binary heap, capped at 3,000 expanded nodes. Smooth paths by line-of-walk. Re-path when an edit touches a path cell.\n549\t  - **Stuck rule:** if a resident has not progressed for 8 s and is off-screen, teleport them to the target. On screen, try a hop, then re-path.\n550\t- **Talk:** dialog with a portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`) and the resident's voice. Lines vary by flags, time of day, mood and town level (`E.Story` nodes with `if` conditions).\n551\t\n552\t### 6.11 Monsters and combat (`DQ.Monsters`, `DQ.Combat`)\n553\t- **AI states:** `idle/wander → notice` (a \"!\" over the head plus a squeak, 0.3 s) `→ chase → windup` (telegraph: flash, crouch or squash, a sound) `→ attack → recover`. Then `→ flee` (below 20% HP for Lintlings) or `→ hurt` (stagger 0.25 s) `→ dead` (pop, particles, drops, `monsterKilled`).\n554\t  - Aggro uses radius `tuning.aggro`, scaled by type (§7.9), plus `world.raycast` sight from eye height.\n555\t  - Leash: return home after chasing 24 blocks away from home.\n556\t- **Spawning:**\n557\t  - Region tables (§7.1) by day or night. Spawn on standable cells under open sky, ≥ 10 blocks from the builder, outside the town border, never inside rooms or during cutscenes.\n558\t  - Rate: 1 spawn per `tuning.spawnEvery` s (day 8, night 5) while the active count is below `tuning.maxMonsters` (default 10).\n559\t  - Despawn: more than 40 blocks away for 20 s.\n560\t- **Damage:**\n561\t  - `dmg = max(1, round((atk − def/2) × U(0.9, 1.1)))`, where U is drawn from the combat stream.\n562\t  - Builder crits: 5%, ×1.5 (Spin Stitch never crits).\n563\t  - Multipliers: `tuning.monsterHp`, `tuning.monsterDmg`, `tuning.builderAtk`, and difficulty (Relaxed 0.5 damage taken, Normal 1, Hard 1.4).\n564\t- **Hit feedback:**\n565\t  - `game.hitFx(x,y,z,{ power, damage, angle })` (hit-stop, shake, impact star, damage number), plus `E.knockback(builder, mon, 120, 40)`.\n566\t  - Monster hits on the builder: knockback 90, i-frames 0.8 s, a red vignette flash under `game.flash` limits.\n567\t  - Each move has its own sound.\n568\t- **Drops:** rolled on death from the drop table (`tuning.dropRate` multiplier on chances); items fly out.\n569\t\n570\t### 6.12 Siege and bosses (`DQ.Siege`, `DQ.Bosses`)\n571\t- **Siege director** (used once in the story, M13–M14, and in the Bee round). It is a `game.script` generator:\n572\t  1. **Prep.** Mortimer's rally line, then a 90 s countdown banner (\"The Knotted army approaches! 1:30\"). Build defences meanwhile; the music turns tense with the drum layer.\n573\t  2. **Autosave checkpoint** `siege`.\n574\t  3. **Waves** W1 → W2 → W3 (§7.9). Each wave spawns over 10 s from the east road (96,76), the Rift (96,80) and the north path (72,52). It ends when all of its monsters are defeated. Between waves: a 25 s breather with \"Wave cleared!\" and a repair prompt.\n575\t  4. **Boss.** Gnarlgrim enters through the Rift (cutscene, §7.10).\n576\t- **Monster targets in sieges:** first the Banner (HP 300; its HP bar is on the HUD during the siege), then ballistas, then the nearest town-structure block in the way (flagged `town` when placed inside the border), then residents, then the builder.\n577\t  - Monsters path with **breaker A\\***: wall blocks count as passable at cost 8 per block, and doors at cost 4.\n578\t  - On reaching a blocking block or door, a monster attacks it (Lintling 3, Pinhog 6, Frayed Knight 8, Knotbrute 25, Gnarlgrim 60 block damage per hit).\n579\t- **Fail:** the Banner falls, or the builder is KO'd while Gnarlgrim is active. Then \"The Banner has fallen...\" / \"You've come undone...\", and a choice: \"Try again from the start of the siege\" (reload the checkpoint) or \"Return to title\". Waves already cleared stay cleared only within a single attempt.\n580\t- **Sir Buttonsworth** (mini-boss, Button Quarry floor):\n581\t  - Intro: he rises from a heap of buttons.\n582\t  - Moves:\n583\t    - **Slam:** wind .6 s; a shockwave ring of radius 3 blocks expands over .5 s. Jump to avoid it; it deals 12.\n584\t    - **Button Volley:** wind .4 s; 5 buttons in a 50° spread (`E.Bullets`, 110 u/s, 8 damage).\n585\t    - **Stomp Charge:** wind .7 s; charges 140 u/s toward the builder, breaks blocks in its path, and is dizzy for 2 s after hitting a wall. Dizzy takes double damage.\n586\t  - Below 50% HP he summons 3 Lintlings once.\n587\t  - The arena has 4 cobble pillars that break under charges.\n588\t  - Defeat: he pops into buttons and drops the Golden Needle and 3 Steel Thread.\n589\t- **Gnarlgrim the Knotted** (final boss, in and around the Thimbleton plaza), HP 600 in three phases (100–66%, 66–33%, 33–0%):\n590\t  - **Tightly wound.** Def 25, so normal hits do about 1 damage. Mortimer and Granny hint \"Use the Ballistas!\" after 3 such hits.\n591\t  - **Tangled.** 2 manned-ballista hits within 8 s, or 1 hit during his charge, make him Tangled for 5 s: he sits in a heap with `down: .6`, his chest **Heart Knot** glows, and his def drops to 0.\n592\t    - Hits on the Heart Knot (front arc) with the Seam Ripper do ×4, other weapons ×2.\n593\t    - A stagger plays at 100 damage per Tangle; leaving the Tangle triggers a roar knockback.\n594\t  - **Phase 1:** Yarn Slam (ring, radius 3.5) and Tangle Toss (lobbed yarn ball via `E.arc`, landing in 1.1 s on a shadow telegraph; 10 damage and leaves a temporary Tangle block for 20 s).\n595\t  - **Phase 2:** adds Charge (breaks blocks in its path and targets a ballista) and summons 4 Lintlings at the phase start.\n596\t  - **Phase 3:** all moves 25% faster, a double Tangle Toss, and a Knotbrute summon.\n597\t  - Phase transitions: a roar (`shout` expression) and a knockback ring; the music switches to the next layer.\n598\t  - Defeat cutscene: he unravels into heaps of yarn (thread particles) and the Golden Thread fragment flies into the Banner. The rift heals (§7.10).\n599\t\n600\t### 6.13 Day, night, lighting, weather (`DQ.Clock`)\n601\t- One day = `tuning.dayMinutes` (12) real minutes; the story starts on Day 1 at 08:00.\n602\t- **Phases:** dawn 05–07, day 07–17, dusk 17–19, night 19–05.\n603\t- `game.lights.enabled = true` always. `ambient` follows the hour: night .30 with a cool blue tint, dawn and dusk .65 with a warm tint, day 1.0. Use `lights.tint`, setting `lights.dark` per phase, then `lights.refresh()`.\n604\t- Light sources add light pools (§6.7). At night, stars twinkle in the void, Mothlets spawn and Frayed Knights roam the Rift.\n605\t- **HUD clock:** a sun/moon dial with \"Day 3\".\n606\t- **Weather (cosmetic, engine `game.weather`):** the Fraying always has `ash` (purple tint). The Glen rains lightly some days (Day 2 afternoon, Day 4 morning). Weather resets per region when it changes.\n607\t- **Sleeping** is in §6.7. **Night-time Granny line:** the first night she warns \"Monsters get bolder after dark, dearie. A lit room is a safe room!\"\n608\t\n609\t### 6.14 Health and hunger\n610\t- The HUD shows HP as a red patch bar with numbers (\"HP 32/40\") and Belly as a bread-loaf bar.\n611\t- Low HP (< 25%) adds a heartbeat sfx and a red vignette (follows the reduce-flash setting).\n612\t- Food is in §7.3. Eating restores Belly and HP; raw Thimbleberries restore Belly +5.\n613\t- Starving: HP −1 every 4 s, with the prompt \"Your belly is empty! Eat something!\"\n614\t\n615\t### 6.15 Story, quests, dialog, cutscenes (`DQ.Quests`, `DQ.Cutscenes`)\n616\t- **Engine pieces:** `E.Story` (story module) runs conversations as data nodes, with `who`, `say`, `choices`, `set`, `quest`, `done`, `emote` and `event`. One `E.Dialog` instance is skinned as a Dragon Quest-style box: a dark indigo panel with a cream stitched border (dashed 1-px line 2 px inside the edge), a name tab, a portrait at the left, typed text with Animalese blips, and a bobbing arrow sprite at page end (the font has no ▼).\n617\t- **Granny Gossamer's box:** a golden stitched border with gold text and a glowing spool portrait (a custom portrait function).\n618\t- **Text:** 3 lines per page, at most 52 characters per line, `E.font` scale 1 (≈ 31 px tall on a 1080p TV).\n619\t- **Quest data** (§7.10): `{ id, title, giver, steps: [{ text, done: condition }], reward, next, unlock }`. Conditions are data:\n620\t  - `{ have: {item: n} }`, `{ crafted: id }`, `{ placed: objId }`, `{ room: type }`, `{ level: n }`, `{ talk: npc }`\n621\t  - `{ killed: {monster: n} }`, `{ at: poi, radius }`, `{ pattern: id }`, `{ flag: k }`, `{ siegeDone: true }`, `{ boss: id }`\n622\t- **Quest director:** evaluates the current steps on relevant events (cheap) and once per second as a fallback. It advances steps and emits `questStep`/`questDone`. Rewards: `{ cheer, items, notions, flags, heartpatch }`. HUD: \"Quest complete: Wren's Bed\" with a jingle.\n623\t- **Objective tracker** (top centre; switch it off per quest with Track on/off in the Journal, or entirely with the HUD tracker setting): the quest title, the current step text, and a marker. The marker is the engine's `r.marker(x,y,z,{label})`, which becomes an edge arrow off screen, with the distance in blocks. Targets: the POI, NPC or Pattern from the step, or for gather steps the nearest matching plant or block within 40 blocks (scanned once per second).\n624\t- **Cutscenes** are `game.script` generators: camera tweens on a proxy, dialog awaits (`yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`), NPC walks (autopilot), and effects.\n625\t  - Skip: hold ○, Esc or right click for 1 s (a fill ring shows) to jump to the end state.\n626\t  - Every cutscene has `apply()`, which sets its end state. Skipping and tests call it.\n627\t- **Writing style** (Dragon Quest-like):\n628\t  - Short, punchy lines; exclamations; earnest heroism softened with jokes; sewing puns; NPCs with verbal tics.\n629\t  - The silent Builder answers only through choices (\"Of course!\" / \"What's a bed?\").\n630\t  - System lines are cheerful: \"You got 3 Straw!\", \"Thimbleton is now Level 2!\", \"A Lintling bounces into view!\"\n631\t  - Avoid modern slang. Keep it family-friendly. Never reuse Dragon Quest names, places or iconic phrases verbatim.\n632\t  - **Font-safe text.** In-game strings use only ASCII plus the font's extra glyphs (♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • × ♪ ▸ ©). Use no em dashes, curly quotes, ✓ ✗ ▼ ♭ or controller symbols inside strings. Button prompts embed tokens such as `{btn:swing}`; the HUD text renderer swaps each token for the `DQ.Glyphs` icon of the current device, built from `input.label`. A test scans every string table for characters outside the allowed set.\n633\t\n634\t### 6.16 UI screens and HUD (10-foot, controller first; `DQ.Hud`, `DQ.Menus`)\n635\tAll UI is pixel art drawn with `E.ui`, `E.font` and `px` inside `r.overlay`. Panels are dark felt (`E.ui.box` gradient `#2a2350 → #1a1636`) with stitched cream borders. Text is at least scale 1 at 240p; headers scale 2; the title logo scale 3–4. At most about 6–8 entries per screen and generous spacing: no dense tables. Information is never conveyed by colour alone (icons plus text).\n636\t\n637\t- **HUD (in play):**\n638\t  - Top left: HP bar, Belly bar, and the equipped weapon icon.\n639\t  - Top centre: the objective tracker.\n640\t  - Top right, *below* `game.cornerArea()`: the clock dial with the day, and the Banner badge (level, Cheer bar).\n641\t  - Bottom centre: the hotbar. 8 slots of 22×22 px; the selected slot is raised and outlined, with its name and count above. L1/R1 or wheel glyphs sit at the ends.\n642\t  - Bottom left: context prompts.\n643\t  - Bottom right: pickup toasts.\n644\t  - Centre: Notion, Room, Level and Quest popups (queued, 2.5 s each).\n645\t  - Siege: the Banner HP bar and the wave label. Boss: a big HP bar with the name, phase pips and a \"TANGLED!\" flash.\n646\t  - **Fades:** the HUD fades to 30% when idle for 6 s outside combat, and returns at full opacity on any input, damage or popup.\n647\t- **Title:**\n648\t  - Behind the menu, a living diorama: the finished Thimbleton built from the attract stamp, at dusk, with a slow camera pan. The logo reads \"DRAGON QUILT BUILDERS\", each letter a patchwork fabric colour with a stitched outline and extrusion (`E.font.title`), and a needle-and-thread dragon emblem.\n649\t  - Menu (`E.Menu`, centred bottom): Continue (if a save exists), New Game, Load Game (`saves.show('load')`), Quilting Bee (solo challenge), Settings (`game.menu('settings')`), About & Credits.\n650\t  - \"Press ✕ / Enter\" prompt; a version label (`E.versionLabel()`).\n651\t  - After 10 s without input, the attract mode starts.\n652\t- **New Game:** name entry (letter grid plus keyboard typing), look A/B, 4 palettes, difficulty (Relaxed/Normal/Hard). The live rig preview turns.\n653\t- **Intro crawl:** 4 pages over the quilt void with Granny's voice and gentle music. Skippable.\n654\t- **Satchel** (Create / Tab / M / N), with tabs switched by L1/R1 or Q/E:\n655\t  - **Bag:** a category strip (Materials, Building, Furniture, Food, Tools, Key) and a 6×4 grid of large slots. Detail pane: icon at ×3, name, a Dragon Quest-style description, the count. Actions: ✕ Assign to hotbar, △ Use/Eat/Equip, □ Drop 1/All. The \"Pocket Notions\" button opens pocket crafting.\n656\t  - **Map:** described below.\n657\t  - **Journal:** active quests (giver portrait, steps with tick icons), completed ones, and the list of known Notions.\n658\t- **Map screen:**\n659\t  - The world drawn top-down at 2 px per column from top-block base colours, shaded by height. Water is blue satin; void is navy with stitched edges; unexplored areas are blank linen (fog revealed within 10 blocks as you go).\n660\t  - Markers: you (an arrow), the Banner, residents, discovered portals, quest targets, the Lost Bundle.\n661\t  - Controls: the stick pans, R2/L2 zoom, ✕ sets a waypoint marker, ○ goes back. Mouse: drag to pan, wheel to zoom, click to set a waypoint.\n662\t- **Crafting, Chest, Travel and Banner screens:** §6.5–6.9.\n663\t- **Pause menu:** the engine standard, plus Satchel, Dev Tools (hidden in hub sessions) and About & Credits. Save/Load come through `menu: { saves }` (enabled when `saves.canSave`: no monsters within 12 blocks, no siege or cutscene).\n664\t- **Results** (chapter clear): the Builder's name and a portrait card. Stats: play time, blocks placed, blocks broken, items crafted, rooms built, monsters defeated, Cheer earned. Then \"Continue to credits\".\n665\t- **Credits:** a scroll over the finale party, ending on \"To be continued... Chapter Two: The Calico Coast\" (a story teaser, not a stub) and \"Thank you for playing!\". Then back to free play in Thimbleton (`credits.onDone → play`), with all systems active.\n666\t- **About & Credits** (title menu and pause menu): pages for Credits, Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback and Licenses (§6.24).\n667\t\n668\t### 6.17 Audio (`DQ.AudioX`)\n669\t- **Music** (all original, in the engine's step sequencer format; §7.11 lists every song):\n670\t  - The director picks the song by context: title, intro, cave, field per region, town (day/night), siege, mini-boss, boss, Bee round, results, credits. It crossfades with `music(song, { fade: 1 })`.\n671\t  - **Dynamic town theme:** extra layers come in with town level (`audio.layer('strings', 1, 2)` at Lv2, `'bells'` at Lv3, `'drums'` at Lv4).\n672\t  - Jingles use `audio.sting(song, { at: 'beat' })` over ducked music.\n673\t- **SFX:** custom voices defined with `audio.define(name, voice)` (§7.12). Footsteps by top material, every 0.28 s while walking. Sound comes from code only, never files.\n674\t- **Animalese voices** (`DQ.AudioX.voice`), driven by the Dialog `onLetter` hook:\n675\t  - **Letters and pauses.** For each letter shown (skip spaces): punctuation adds a 90 ms pause, and `?` lifts the last 3 syllables by 20%.\n676\t  - **Each letter becomes one syllable**, 55–70 ms (shorter at fast text speed):\n677\t    1. A **consonant onset**:\n678\t       - s/z/c/x: highpass noise at 4 kHz, 18 ms\n679\t       - t/d/k/g/p/b/q: click (bandpass noise at 1.5 kHz, 8 ms, plus a low thump)\n680\t       - m/n/l/r/w/y/v/f/h/j: a soft voiced onset (lowpass 600 Hz)\n681\t    2. A **vowel body**: a sawtooth or pulse at the speaker's pitch × (1 + letter-hash × range), through two `BiquadFilter` bandpasses at the vowel formants. The vowel is the next vowel in the word.\n682\t\n683\t       | Vowel | F1 (Hz) | F2 (Hz) |\n684\t       |---|---|---|\n685\t       | a | 800 | 1200 |\n686\t       | e | 500 | 1900 |\n687\t       | i | 300 | 2300 |\n688\t       | o | 500 | 900 |\n689\t       | u | 350 | 800 |\n690\t\n691\t       Formants scale with the speaker's `formant` factor.\n692\t  - **Contour:** +8% at word starts, falling 6% across a sentence. ALL-CAPS words are 15% louder and higher.\n693\t  - **Determinism:** pitch jitter comes from `hash(char, index, speakerSeed)`, never `Math.random`.\n694\t  - Routed through `_voiceBus` (the \"Voices\" volume setting). Profiles are in §7.8. The Builder is silent.\n695\t  - In headless runs, voice calls are no-ops (`audio.ctx` is null).\n696\t\n697\t### 6.18 Save and load (`DQ.Save`)\n698\t- Uses `game.saves` (3 slots plus autosave, with place and level labels).\n699\t- `write()` returns:\n700\t  ```\n701\t  { data: { v: 1, seed, world: world.diff(), objects: Objects.serialize(),\n702\t            builder: {...}, bag, hotbar, chests,\n703\t            town: {cheer, level, patterns, townChest},\n704\t            residents: [...], story: story.state(), quests,\n705\t            clock: {day, hour}, stats, explored (RLE),\n706\t            lostBundle, flags },\n707\t    place: regionName, level: 'Town Lv ' + level }\n708\t  ```\n709\t- `read()` regenerates the base world from the seed, then applies the diff and the objects, then recomputes rooms and the nav graph.\n710\t- **Autosave:** on `questDone`, after sleeping, at the siege checkpoint, and every 5 minutes when `canSave`.\n711\t- The title's Continue uses `saves.latest()`. `migrate(data, from)` is a stub for future versions; v1 is the only version, so implement it as identity with a version check.\n712\t- In a hub session nothing is saved; the client's storage is in-memory anyway.\n713\t\n714\t### 6.19 Dev mode (`DQ.Dev`, from the pause menu → \"Dev Tools\")\n715\tA full-screen pixel panel with tabs (L1/R1 or the mouse). Every toggle and slider takes effect immediately and is saved under `E.store('dqb-dev')`. It is hidden and disabled in hub sessions.\n716\t\n717\t| Tab | Tools |\n718\t|---|---|\n719\t| **Cheats** | God mode; Creative bag (infinite materials, every recipe known); Fly / no-clip (Space up, Shift down); Instant break; No hunger; One-hit kills; Freeze time of day; Invisible to monsters |\n720\t| **Warp** | Teleport list: Lint Hollow, Thimbleton plaza, Corduroy Crags, Button Quarry, Tweedwood Lint Camp, Calico Coast shipwreck, Frayed Verge, Knot Gate, Knotted Spire top, the Rift. Plus \"Teleport to cursor\" |\n721\t| **Spawn** | Any monster (×1/×5), either boss, any item ×1/×10/×99, any resident (arrive now) |\n722\t| **Story** | Jump to beat: Start / Banner / Lv2 / Lv3 / Lv4 / Hall built / Seam Ripper / Spire / Siege / Boss / Chapter clear. Each beat calls `DQ.Quests.applyBeat(id)`, which sets flags, quests, residents, rooms (via stamps) and inventory to a consistent state. Also: complete the current quest step, set the town level, add Cheer ±50, set the hour, start or skip a siege wave |\n723\t| **Tuning** | Live sliders (below) with **Reset to defaults** (all, or a single slider with △) |\n724\t| **Debug** | Overlays: collision cells, room detector, nav graph and NPC paths, monster aggro rings, cutaway radius, light radii, Pattern ghost status. Perf readout: frame ms, queued runs, actors, lights. Time scale 0.25×–4×. Frame step (while paused, ✕ advances 1 step). Cutaway auto/always/off. Hub net stats (msgs/s, bytes), shown only in Bee solo/loopback |\n725\t| **World** | Export a stamp: mark two corners with the cursor, then copy JSON to the clipboard and offer a download. Save and load a dev snapshot. Regenerate the world |\n726\t\n727\t**Sliders** (`DQ.TUNING_DEFS`: key, label, min, max, step, default):\n728\t\n729\t| Slider | Default | Range |\n730\t|---|---|---|\n731\t| Walk speed | 78 | 40–140 |\n732\t| Sprint multiplier | 1.45 | 1–2.5 |\n733\t| Jump velocity | 215 | 150–320 |\n734\t| Gravity | 800 | 400–1400 |\n735\t| Reach (blocks) | 4.5 | 2–8 |\n736\t| Builder attack × | 1.0 | 0.25–4 |\n737\t| Block damage × | 1.0 | 0.25–5 |\n738\t| Max HP base | 40 | 10–200 |\n739\t| Belly drain × | 1.0 | 0–4 |\n740\t| Monster HP × | 1.0 | 0.25–4 |\n741\t| Monster damage × | 1.0 | 0–4 |\n742\t| Monster speed × | 1.0 | 0.5–2 |\n743\t| Aggro radius (blocks) | 8 | 2–20 |\n744\t| Max monsters | 10 | 0–40 |\n745\t| Spawn interval night (s) | 5 | 1–30 |\n746\t| Day length (min) | 12 | 2–60 |\n747\t| Cheer × | 1.0 | 0.25–5 |\n748\t| Drop rate × | 1.0 | 0–5 |\n749\t| Siege wave size × | 1.0 | 0.25–3 |\n750\t| Helper build rate (blocks/min) | 6 | 0–60 |\n751\t| Ballista reload (s) | 2.5 | 0.5–6 |\n752\t| Tangle window (s) | 8 | 2–20 |\n753\t\n754\tGameplay code reads `DQ.tuning.get(key)` every time (no cached copies), so sliders are live.\n755\t\n756\t### 6.20 Attract mode (`DQ.Attract`)\n757\t- **Start and exit.** It starts after 10 s without input on the title screen or its menus (main menu, Load list, About). It does not start on the Settings screen or during name entry, where the player is busy choosing. **Any input exits it** to the title with a fade: any key, mouse button, mouse movement over 8 px, gamepad button, or stick past 0.5. On touch-only devices it also runs, and a tap exits.\n758\t- **Isolation.** It builds its own `ctx` with a separate world (generated from the seed, plus `stampDemoTown()`: a pre-built Lv4 Thimbleton with rooms, the Hall, ballistas and residents). It never touches the player's save or settings. Sound plays at 60% volume, if unlocked.\n759\t- **The AI builder** is `DQ.Autopilot` running the vignettes below with cheats: invulnerable, infinite materials, placement at 0.15 s per block, teleporting between vignettes behind a wipe transition."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"44f17e3a-784e-4079-8c44-c0c32364068b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:55:41.579Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","content":"  - On the ground it is an animated dashed running stitch (cream thread, 2-px dashes moving along the edge), drawn at each border cell's surface height.\n  - In the void, at night, it glows faintly.\n- **Cheer sources:** rooms (§7.6), quest rewards, and Cheer bubbles from residents (+5 each, §6.10).\n  - Collecting a bubble: it flies to the HUD banner icon, which pulses, with a two-note \"ding\".\n  - On reaching a level threshold:\n    1. Town Level up (a cutscene of about 4 s): the banner glows, a new stripe stitches itself onto it, and the border grows outward in an animated sweep.\n    2. A popup lists the unlocks.\n    3. The Lv-up jingle plays.\n    4. The next resident arrives at the following dawn (or immediately if it is daytime, walking in from the east road).\n- **Patterns** (blueprints):\n  - **Pattern data:** `{ id, name, size:[w,d,h], layers: [ASCII rows per level], legend: { ch: blockKey | {obj, facing} }, reward }`.\n  - **Placement:** the player uses a Pattern scroll (key item) and a ghost footprint follows the cursor. Turn it 90° with L1/R1 (pad), or the mouse wheel or [ / ] (keyboard); the camera still turns with Q/E and the right stick. Confirm on valid ground inside the border: flat ± 1 level, no objects in the way (blocks in the way are OK; they are shown as \"remove\").\n  - **Ghosts:** each unbuilt cell draws a translucent ghost of its target block (`px.blend(.35)`) with a dashed outline; wrong blocks get a red cross icon. The HUD shows a panel: \"Quilters' Hall: 37/129\", then the materials still needed (have/need, e.g. Cobble 12/35).\n  - **Building:** placing the right block into a ghost cell counts it, with a sparkle and a short arpeggio. Objects in ghost cells (door, lanterns) count the same way.\n  - **Helpers:** residents with the `helper` job (Professor, Wren, and Mortimer by day) take the needed materials from the Town Chest. Each walks to an unbuilt ghost cell they can reach and places a block with a hammer animation and a \"tok\". Rate: `tuning.helperRate` blocks/min each (default 6). If the chest lacks materials, they say so in a balloon (\"Need Planks!\") and idle.\n  - **Completion:** when every cell matches, a celebration cutscene plays (§7.10) and the reward and Cheer are granted. The structure counts as a special room (\"Quilters' Hall\") if its rules match (§7.6).\n\n### 6.10 Residents (`DQ.Residents`)\n- Each resident is a `VoxelBody` plus a `Humanoid` or `Blob` rig, with a profile from `DQ.NPCS` (§7.8): `job`, `schedule`, `needs`, `voice`, `fighter` stats, and dialog nodes.\n- **Schedule** (hours of the day clock; each block picks a target):\n\n  | Time | Activity |\n  |---|---|\n  | 06–08 | Wake, stretch (`wave` pose), go to the plaza |\n  | 08–18 | Job: see below; between tasks, wander inside the border |\n  | 18–20 | Supper: sit at a Mess Hall stool if one exists and eat (+bubble) |\n  | 20–21 | Socialise near the Banner (talk balloons `note`, `dots`) |\n  | 21–06 | Sleep in a free bed in a Bedroom (+bubble at 06:00); without one, sleep on the ground beside the Banner (mood −1, a \"No bed...\" balloon) |\n- **Jobs:**\n  - **Wren** (gatherer): each morning she puts 3 random basics (straw, branch, cotton) into the Town Chest. In sieges she uses a slingshot (4 damage, range 6 blocks, 1.2 s).\n  - **Mortimer** (guard): patrols the border at night with a lantern and fights monsters inside the border with a club (8 damage, `overhead`). In sieges he leads the defence. He needs a Guard Post to stand watch from (+bubble).\n  - **Marigold** (cook): with a Kitchen, cooks at the Hearth Pot (stirring loop) twice a day from Town Chest ingredients into the chest. At 18:00 she sets a dish on the Mess Hall table. In sieges she throws hot pies (5 damage, arc).\n  - **Professor Selvage** (helper): builds Patterns from the Town Chest at the helper rate, or reads in the Workshop. In sieges he hides in the Hall.\n  - **Lintwick** (fluff-farmer): puts 2 Lint into the Town Chest daily and dances (a Blob hop loop) when happy. In sieges he rolls into monsters (3 damage + knockback).\n- **Needs and Cheer bubbles.** At most once per day per need, each satisfied need spawns a floating pink heart bubble (+5 Cheer) over the resident, which stays until collected:\n  - sleep: slept in a bed\n  - meal: ate a dish\n  - comfort: their favourite room exists (Wren: Bedroom, Mortimer: Guard Post, Marigold: Kitchen, Professor: Workshop, Lintwick: a Bedroom with a Felt block in its walls)\n\n  Collect a bubble by walking within 2 blocks of it. Unhappy residents show a `dots` balloon and say why when you talk to them.\n- **Requests:** a resident with an available quest shows a bouncing \"!\" balloon (`story.emote`). Talking starts it, and its steps appear in the Journal and HUD. When the steps are done, a \"?\" balloon shows; talk to them to turn it in.\n- **Arrival cutscene:** the resident walks in along the east road, the camera pans to them, and they say their intro line. \"Mortimer has moved into Thimbleton!\" shows with a jingle.\n- **Navigation** (`DQ.Nav`):\n  - **Graph:** nodes are standable `(x, y, L)`. Edges:\n    - 8-neighbour moves (diagonals only if both orthogonal neighbours are open), cost 1 (diagonal 1.41)\n    - step up 1 level (a hop), cost 2\n    - drop down 1–3 levels, cost 1.5\n    - doors open for residents, closed for monsters\n  - **A\\*** with a binary heap, capped at 3,000 expanded nodes. Smooth paths by line-of-walk. Re-path when an edit touches a path cell.\n  - **Stuck rule:** if a resident has not progressed for 8 s and is off-screen, teleport them to the target. On screen, try a hop, then re-path.\n- **Talk:** dialog with a portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`) and the resident's voice. Lines vary by flags, time of day, mood and town level (`E.Story` nodes with `if` conditions).\n\n### 6.11 Monsters and combat (`DQ.Monsters`, `DQ.Combat`)\n- **AI states:** `idle/wander → notice` (a \"!\" over the head plus a squeak, 0.3 s) `→ chase → windup` (telegraph: flash, crouch or squash, a sound) `→ attack → recover`. Then `→ flee` (below 20% HP for Lintlings) or `→ hurt` (stagger 0.25 s) `→ dead` (pop, particles, drops, `monsterKilled`).\n  - Aggro uses radius `tuning.aggro`, scaled by type (§7.9), plus `world.raycast` sight from eye height.\n  - Leash: return home after chasing 24 blocks away from home.\n- **Spawning:**\n  - Region tables (§7.1) by day or night. Spawn on standable cells under open sky, ≥ 10 blocks from the builder, outside the town border, never inside rooms or during cutscenes.\n  - Rate: 1 spawn per `tuning.spawnEvery` s (day 8, night 5) while the active count is below `tuning.maxMonsters` (default 10).\n  - Despawn: more than 40 blocks away for 20 s.\n- **Damage:**\n  - `dmg = max(1, round((atk − def/2) × U(0.9, 1.1)))`, where U is drawn from the combat stream.\n  - Builder crits: 5%, ×1.5 (Spin Stitch never crits).\n  - Multipliers: `tuning.monsterHp`, `tuning.monsterDmg`, `tuning.builderAtk`, and difficulty (Relaxed 0.5 damage taken, Normal 1, Hard 1.4).\n- **Hit feedback:**\n  - `game.hitFx(x,y,z,{ power, damage, angle })` (hit-stop, shake, impact star, damage number), plus `E.knockback(builder, mon, 120, 40)`.\n  - Monster hits on the builder: knockback 90, i-frames 0.8 s, a red vignette flash under `game.flash` limits.\n  - Each move has its own sound.\n- **Drops:** rolled on death from the drop table (`tuning.dropRate` multiplier on chances); items fly out.\n\n### 6.12 Siege and bosses (`DQ.Siege`, `DQ.Bosses`)\n- **Siege director** (used once in the story, M13–M14, and in the Bee round). It is a `game.script` generator:\n  1. **Prep.** Mortimer's rally line, then a 90 s countdown banner (\"The Knotted army approaches! 1:30\"). Build defences meanwhile; the music turns tense with the drum layer.\n  2. **Autosave checkpoint** `siege`.\n  3. **Waves** W1 → W2 → W3 (§7.9). Each wave spawns over 10 s from the east road (96,76), the Rift (96,80) and the north path (72,52). It ends when all of its monsters are defeated. Between waves: a 25 s breather with \"Wave cleared!\" and a repair prompt.\n  4. **Boss.** Gnarlgrim enters through the Rift (cutscene, §7.10).\n- **Monster targets in sieges:** first the Banner (HP 300; its HP bar is on the HUD during the siege), then ballistas, then the nearest town-structure block in the way (flagged `town` when placed inside the border), then residents, then the builder.\n  - Monsters path with **breaker A\\***: wall blocks count as passable at cost 8 per block, and doors at cost 4.\n  - On reaching a blocking block or door, a monster attacks it (Lintling 3, Pinhog 6, Frayed Knight 8, Knotbrute 25, Gnarlgrim 60 block damage per hit).\n- **Fail:** the Banner falls, or the builder is KO'd while Gnarlgrim is active. Then \"The Banner has fallen...\" / \"You've come undone...\", and a choice: \"Try again from the start of the siege\" (reload the checkpoint) or \"Return to title\". Waves already cleared stay cleared only within a single attempt.\n- **Sir Buttonsworth** (mini-boss, Button Quarry floor):\n  - Intro: he rises from a heap of buttons.\n  - Moves:\n    - **Slam:** wind .6 s; a shockwave ring of radius 3 blocks expands over .5 s. Jump to avoid it; it deals 12.\n    - **Button Volley:** wind .4 s; 5 buttons in a 50° spread (`E.Bullets`, 110 u/s, 8 damage).\n    - **Stomp Charge:** wind .7 s; charges 140 u/s toward the builder, breaks blocks in its path, and is dizzy for 2 s after hitting a wall. Dizzy takes double damage.\n  - Below 50% HP he summons 3 Lintlings once.\n  - The arena has 4 cobble pillars that break under charges.\n  - Defeat: he pops into buttons and drops the Golden Needle and 3 Steel Thread.\n- **Gnarlgrim the Knotted** (final boss, in and around the Thimbleton plaza), HP 600 in three phases (100–66%, 66–33%, 33–0%):\n  - **Tightly wound.** Def 25, so normal hits do about 1 damage. Mortimer and Granny hint \"Use the Ballistas!\" after 3 such hits.\n  - **Tangled.** 2 manned-ballista hits within 8 s, or 1 hit during his charge, make him Tangled for 5 s: he sits in a heap with `down: .6`, his chest **Heart Knot** glows, and his def drops to 0.\n    - Hits on the Heart Knot (front arc) with the Seam Ripper do ×4, other weapons ×2.\n    - A stagger plays at 100 damage per Tangle; leaving the Tangle triggers a roar knockback.\n  - **Phase 1:** Yarn Slam (ring, radius 3.5) and Tangle Toss (lobbed yarn ball via `E.arc`, landing in 1.1 s on a shadow telegraph; 10 damage and leaves a temporary Tangle block for 20 s).\n  - **Phase 2:** adds Charge (breaks blocks in its path and targets a ballista) and summons 4 Lintlings at the phase start.\n  - **Phase 3:** all moves 25% faster, a double Tangle Toss, and a Knotbrute summon.\n  - Phase transitions: a roar (`shout` expression) and a knockback ring; the music switches to the next layer.\n  - Defeat cutscene: he unravels into heaps of yarn (thread particles) and the Golden Thread fragment flies into the Banner. The rift heals (§7.10).\n\n### 6.13 Day, night, lighting, weather (`DQ.Clock`)\n- One day = `tuning.dayMinutes` (12) real minutes; the story starts on Day 1 at 08:00.\n- **Phases:** dawn 05–07, day 07–17, dusk 17–19, night 19–05.\n- `game.lights.enabled = true` always. `ambient` follows the hour: night .30 with a cool blue tint, dawn and dusk .65 with a warm tint, day 1.0. Use `lights.tint`, setting `lights.dark` per phase, then `lights.refresh()`.\n- Light sources add light pools (§6.7). At night, stars twinkle in the void, Mothlets spawn and Frayed Knights roam the Rift.\n- **HUD clock:** a sun/moon dial with \"Day 3\".\n- **Weather (cosmetic, engine `game.weather`):** the Fraying always has `ash` (purple tint). The Glen rains lightly some days (Day 2 afternoon, Day 4 morning). Weather resets per region when it changes.\n- **Sleeping** is in §6.7. **Night-time Granny line:** the first night she warns \"Monsters get bolder after dark, dearie. A lit room is a safe room!\"\n\n### 6.14 Health and hunger\n- The HUD shows HP as a red patch bar with numbers (\"HP 32/40\") and Belly as a bread-loaf bar.\n- Low HP (< 25%) adds a heartbeat sfx and a red vignette (follows the reduce-flash setting).\n- Food is in §7.3. Eating restores Belly and HP; raw Thimbleberries restore Belly +5.\n- Starving: HP −1 every 4 s, with the prompt \"Your belly is empty! Eat something!\"\n\n### 6.15 Story, quests, dialog, cutscenes (`DQ.Quests`, `DQ.Cutscenes`)\n- **Engine pieces:** `E.Story` (story module) runs conversations as data nodes, with `who`, `say`, `choices`, `set`, `quest`, `done`, `emote` and `event`. One `E.Dialog` instance is skinned as a Dragon Quest-style box: a dark indigo panel with a cream stitched border (dashed 1-px line 2 px inside the edge), a name tab, a portrait at the left, typed text with Animalese blips, and a bobbing arrow sprite at page end (the font has no ▼).\n- **Granny Gossamer's box:** a golden stitched border with gold text and a glowing spool portrait (a custom portrait function).\n- **Text:** 3 lines per page, at most 52 characters per line, `E.font` scale 1 (≈ 31 px tall on a 1080p TV).\n- **Quest data** (§7.10): `{ id, title, giver, steps: [{ text, done: condition }], reward, next, unlock }`. Conditions are data:\n  - `{ have: {item: n} }`, `{ crafted: id }`, `{ placed: objId }`, `{ room: type }`, `{ level: n }`, `{ talk: npc }`\n  - `{ killed: {monster: n} }`, `{ at: poi, radius }`, `{ pattern: id }`, `{ flag: k }`, `{ siegeDone: true }`, `{ boss: id }`\n- **Quest director:** evaluates the current steps on relevant events (cheap) and once per second as a fallback. It advances steps and emits `questStep`/`questDone`. Rewards: `{ cheer, items, notions, flags, heartpatch }`. HUD: \"Quest complete: Wren's Bed\" with a jingle.\n- **Objective tracker** (top centre; switch it off per quest with Track on/off in the Journal, or entirely with the HUD tracker setting): the quest title, the current step text, and a marker. The marker is the engine's `r.marker(x,y,z,{label})`, which becomes an edge arrow off screen, with the distance in blocks. Targets: the POI, NPC or Pattern from the step, or for gather steps the nearest matching plant or block within 40 blocks (scanned once per second).\n- **Cutscenes** are `game.script` generators: camera tweens on a proxy, dialog awaits (`yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`), NPC walks (autopilot), and effects.\n  - Skip: hold ○, Esc or right click for 1 s (a fill ring shows) to jump to the end state.\n  - Every cutscene has `apply()`, which sets its end state. Skipping and tests call it.\n- **Writing style** (Dragon Quest-like):\n  - Short, punchy lines; exclamations; earnest heroism softened with jokes; sewing puns; NPCs with verbal tics.\n  - The silent Builder answers only through choices (\"Of course!\" / \"What's a bed?\").\n  - System lines are cheerful: \"You got 3 Straw!\", \"Thimbleton is now Level 2!\", \"A Lintling bounces into view!\"\n  - Avoid modern slang. Keep it family-friendly. Never reuse Dragon Quest names, places or iconic phrases verbatim.\n  - **Font-safe text.** In-game strings use only ASCII plus the font's extra glyphs (♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • × ♪ ▸ ©). Use no em dashes, curly quotes, ✓ ✗ ▼ ♭ or controller symbols inside strings. Button prompts embed tokens such as `{btn:swing}`; the HUD text renderer swaps each token for the `DQ.Glyphs` icon of the current device, built from `input.label`. A test scans every string table for characters outside the allowed set.\n\n### 6.16 UI screens and HUD (10-foot, controller first; `DQ.Hud`, `DQ.Menus`)\nAll UI is pixel art drawn with `E.ui`, `E.font` and `px` inside `r.overlay`. Panels are dark felt (`E.ui.box` gradient `#2a2350 → #1a1636`) with stitched cream borders. Text is at least scale 1 at 240p; headers scale 2; the title logo scale 3–4. At most about 6–8 entries per screen and generous spacing: no dense tables. Information is never conveyed by colour alone (icons plus text).\n\n- **HUD (in play):**\n  - Top left: HP bar, Belly bar, and the equipped weapon icon.\n  - Top centre: the objective tracker.\n  - Top right, *below* `game.cornerArea()`: the clock dial with the day, and the Banner badge (level, Cheer bar).\n  - Bottom centre: the hotbar. 8 slots of 22×22 px; the selected slot is raised and outlined, with its name and count above. L1/R1 or wheel glyphs sit at the ends.\n  - Bottom left: context prompts.\n  - Bottom right: pickup toasts.\n  - Centre: Notion, Room, Level and Quest popups (queued, 2.5 s each).\n  - Siege: the Banner HP bar and the wave label. Boss: a big HP bar with the name, phase pips and a \"TANGLED!\" flash.\n  - **Fades:** the HUD fades to 30% when idle for 6 s outside combat, and returns at full opacity on any input, damage or popup.\n- **Title:**\n  - Behind the menu, a living diorama: the finished Thimbleton built from the attract stamp, at dusk, with a slow camera pan. The logo reads \"DRAGON QUILT BUILDERS\", each letter a patchwork fabric colour with a stitched outline and extrusion (`E.font.title`), and a needle-and-thread dragon emblem.\n  - Menu (`E.Menu`, centred bottom): Continue (if a save exists), New Game, Load Game (`saves.show('load')`), Quilting Bee (solo challenge), Settings (`game.menu('settings')`), About & Credits.\n  - \"Press ✕ / Enter\" prompt; a version label (`E.versionLabel()`).\n  - After 10 s without input, the attract mode starts.\n- **New Game:** name entry (letter grid plus keyboard typing), look A/B, 4 palettes, difficulty (Relaxed/Normal/Hard). The live rig preview turns.\n- **Intro crawl:** 4 pages over the quilt void with Granny's voice and gentle music. Skippable.\n- **Satchel** (Create / Tab / M / N), with tabs switched by L1/R1 or Q/E:\n  - **Bag:** a category strip (Materials, Building, Furniture, Food, Tools, Key) and a 6×4 grid of large slots. Detail pane: icon at ×3, name, a Dragon Quest-style description, the count. Actions: ✕ Assign to hotbar, △ Use/Eat/Equip, □ Drop 1/All. The \"Pocket Notions\" button opens pocket crafting.\n  - **Map:** described below.\n  - **Journal:** active quests (giver portrait, steps with tick icons), completed ones, and the list of known Notions.\n- **Map screen:**\n  - The world drawn top-down at 2 px per column from top-block base colours, shaded by height. Water is blue satin; void is navy with stitched edges; unexplored areas are blank linen (fog revealed within 10 blocks as you go).\n  - Markers: you (an arrow), the Banner, residents, discovered portals, quest targets, the Lost Bundle.\n  - Controls: the stick pans, R2/L2 zoom, ✕ sets a waypoint marker, ○ goes back. Mouse: drag to pan, wheel to zoom, click to set a waypoint.\n- **Crafting, Chest, Travel and Banner screens:** §6.5–6.9.\n- **Pause menu:** the engine standard, plus Satchel, Dev Tools (hidden in hub sessions) and About & Credits. Save/Load come through `menu: { saves }` (enabled when `saves.canSave`: no monsters within 12 blocks, no siege or cutscene).\n- **Results** (chapter clear): the Builder's name and a portrait card. Stats: play time, blocks placed, blocks broken, items crafted, rooms built, monsters defeated, Cheer earned. Then \"Continue to credits\".\n- **Credits:** a scroll over the finale party, ending on \"To be continued... Chapter Two: The Calico Coast\" (a story teaser, not a stub) and \"Thank you for playing!\". Then back to free play in Thimbleton (`credits.onDone → play`), with all systems active.\n- **About & Credits** (title menu and pause menu): pages for Credits, Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback and Licenses (§6.24).\n\n### 6.17 Audio (`DQ.AudioX`)\n- **Music** (all original, in the engine's step sequencer format; §7.11 lists every song):\n  - The director picks the song by context: title, intro, cave, field per region, town (day/night), siege, mini-boss, boss, Bee round, results, credits. It crossfades with `music(song, { fade: 1 })`.\n  - **Dynamic town theme:** extra layers come in with town level (`audio.layer('strings', 1, 2)` at Lv2, `'bells'` at Lv3, `'drums'` at Lv4).\n  - Jingles use `audio.sting(song, { at: 'beat' })` over ducked music.\n- **SFX:** custom voices defined with `audio.define(name, voice)` (§7.12). Footsteps by top material, every 0.28 s while walking. Sound comes from code only, never files.\n- **Animalese voices** (`DQ.AudioX.voice`), driven by the Dialog `onLetter` hook:\n  - **Letters and pauses.** For each letter shown (skip spaces): punctuation adds a 90 ms pause, and `?` lifts the last 3 syllables by 20%.\n  - **Each letter becomes one syllable**, 55–70 ms (shorter at fast text speed):\n    1. A **consonant onset**:\n       - s/z/c/x: highpass noise at 4 kHz, 18 ms\n       - t/d/k/g/p/b/q: click (bandpass noise at 1.5 kHz, 8 ms, plus a low thump)\n       - m/n/l/r/w/y/v/f/h/j: a soft voiced onset (lowpass 600 Hz)\n    2. A **vowel body**: a sawtooth or pulse at the speaker's pitch × (1 + letter-hash × range), through two `BiquadFilter` bandpasses at the vowel formants. The vowel is the next vowel in the word.\n\n       | Vowel | F1 (Hz) | F2 (Hz) |\n       |---|---|---|\n       | a | 800 | 1200 |\n       | e | 500 | 1900 |\n       | i | 300 | 2300 |\n       | o | 500 | 900 |\n       | u | 350 | 800 |\n\n       Formants scale with the speaker's `formant` factor.\n  - **Contour:** +8% at word starts, falling 6% across a sentence. ALL-CAPS words are 15% louder and higher.\n  - **Determinism:** pitch jitter comes from `hash(char, index, speakerSeed)`, never `Math.random`.\n  - Routed through `_voiceBus` (the \"Voices\" volume setting). Profiles are in §7.8. The Builder is silent.\n  - In headless runs, voice calls are no-ops (`audio.ctx` is null).\n\n### 6.18 Save and load (`DQ.Save`)\n- Uses `game.saves` (3 slots plus autosave, with place and level labels).\n- `write()` returns:\n  ```\n  { data: { v: 1, seed, world: world.diff(), objects: Objects.serialize(),\n            builder: {...}, bag, hotbar, chests,\n            town: {cheer, level, patterns, townChest},\n            residents: [...], story: story.state(), quests,\n            clock: {day, hour}, stats, explored (RLE),\n            lostBundle, flags },\n    place: regionName, level: 'Town Lv ' + level }\n  ```\n- `read()` regenerates the base world from the seed, then applies the diff and the objects, then recomputes rooms and the nav graph.\n- **Autosave:** on `questDone`, after sleeping, at the siege checkpoint, and every 5 minutes when `canSave`.\n- The title's Continue uses `saves.latest()`. `migrate(data, from)` is a stub for future versions; v1 is the only version, so implement it as identity with a version check.\n- In a hub session nothing is saved; the client's storage is in-memory anyway.\n\n### 6.19 Dev mode (`DQ.Dev`, from the pause menu → \"Dev Tools\")\nA full-screen pixel panel with tabs (L1/R1 or the mouse). Every toggle and slider takes effect immediately and is saved under `E.store('dqb-dev')`. It is hidden and disabled in hub sessions.\n\n| Tab | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| **Cheats** | God mode; Creative bag (infinite materials, every recipe known); Fly / no-clip (Space up, Shift down); Instant break; No hunger; One-hit kills; Freeze time of day; Invisible to monsters |\n| **Warp** | Teleport list: Lint Hollow, Thimbleton plaza, Corduroy Crags, Button Quarry, Tweedwood Lint Camp, Calico Coast shipwreck, Frayed Verge, Knot Gate, Knotted Spire top, the Rift. Plus \"Teleport to cursor\" |\n| **Spawn** | Any monster (×1/×5), either boss, any item ×1/×10/×99, any resident (arrive now) |\n| **Story** | Jump to beat: Start / Banner / Lv2 / Lv3 / Lv4 / Hall built / Seam Ripper / Spire / Siege / Boss / Chapter clear. Each beat calls `DQ.Quests.applyBeat(id)`, which sets flags, quests, residents, rooms (via stamps) and inventory to a consistent state. Also: complete the current quest step, set the town level, add Cheer ±50, set the hour, start or skip a siege wave |\n| **Tuning** | Live sliders (below) with **Reset to defaults** (all, or a single slider with △) |\n| **Debug** | Overlays: collision cells, room detector, nav graph and NPC paths, monster aggro rings, cutaway radius, light radii, Pattern ghost status. Perf readout: frame ms, queued runs, actors, lights. Time scale 0.25×–4×. Frame step (while paused, ✕ advances 1 step). Cutaway auto/always/off. Hub net stats (msgs/s, bytes), shown only in Bee solo/loopback |\n| **World** | Export a stamp: mark two corners with the cursor, then copy JSON to the clipboard and offer a download. Save and load a dev snapshot. Regenerate the world |\n\n**Sliders** (`DQ.TUNING_DEFS`: key, label, min, max, step, default):\n\n| Slider | Default | Range |\n|---|---|---|\n| Walk speed | 78 | 40–140 |\n| Sprint multiplier | 1.45 | 1–2.5 |\n| Jump velocity | 215 | 150–320 |\n| Gravity | 800 | 400–1400 |\n| Reach (blocks) | 4.5 | 2–8 |\n| Builder attack × | 1.0 | 0.25–4 |\n| Block damage × | 1.0 | 0.25–5 |\n| Max HP base | 40 | 10–200 |\n| Belly drain × | 1.0 | 0–4 |\n| Monster HP × | 1.0 | 0.25–4 |\n| Monster damage × | 1.0 | 0–4 |\n| Monster speed × | 1.0 | 0.5–2 |\n| Aggro radius (blocks) | 8 | 2–20 |\n| Max monsters | 10 | 0–40 |\n| Spawn interval night (s) | 5 | 1–30 |\n| Day length (min) | 12 | 2–60 |\n| Cheer × | 1.0 | 0.25–5 |\n| Drop rate × | 1.0 | 0–5 |\n| Siege wave size × | 1.0 | 0.25–3 |\n| Helper build rate (blocks/min) | 6 | 0–60 |\n| Ballista reload (s) | 2.5 | 0.5–6 |\n| Tangle window (s) | 8 | 2–20 |\n\nGameplay code reads `DQ.tuning.get(key)` every time (no cached copies), so sliders are live.\n\n### 6.20 Attract mode (`DQ.Attract`)\n- **Start and exit.** It starts after 10 s without input on the title screen or its menus (main menu, Load list, About). It does not start on the Settings screen or during name entry, where the player is busy choosing. **Any input exits it** to the title with a fade: any key, mouse button, mouse movement over 8 px, gamepad button, or stick past 0.5. On touch-only devices it also runs, and a tap exits.\n- **Isolation.** It builds its own `ctx` with a separate world (generated from the seed, plus `stampDemoTown()`: a pre-built Lv4 Thimbleton with rooms, the Hall, ballistas and residents). It never touches the player's save or settings. Sound plays at 60% volume, if unlocked.\n- **The AI builder** is `DQ.Autopilot` running the vignettes below with cheats: invulnerable, infinite materials, placement at 0.15 s per block, teleporting between vignettes behind a wipe transition.","numLines":260,"startLine":500,"totalLines":1675}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012q1NanVCHn6Mq1Js9a2xzX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghHvcMqGWYjMAbo3UJiR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P7jFK7S4rkD6en6GfKJZyb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","offset":760,"limit":240},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10433,"cache_read_input_tokens":24533,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10433},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"21c37991-8879-4b5d-9f92-b810ba9520a5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:55:42.322Z","request_id":"req_011CghHvc8wbPoNqT5Hz1hTp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01P7jFK7S4rkD6en6GfKJZyb":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","offset":760,"limit":240}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P7jFK7S4rkD6en6GfKJZyb","type":"tool_result","content":"760\t- **Overlay:** a small logo top left, \"DEMO\" top right (below the corner area), and a blinking \"Press any button\" with the current device's glyph.\n761\t- **Vignettes**, looping forever with a 4 s logo card between loops (each written as a `game.script` and also run by a test):\n762\t\n763\t| # | Vignette | Length |\n764\t|---|---|---|\n765\t| 1 | \"Morning in Thimbleton\": camera glide; residents at their jobs; banner flutter | 12 s |\n766\t| 2 | \"Gathering\": breaks turf and tufts in the Glen; pickups; a Notion popup | 14 s |\n767\t| 3 | \"Crafting\": opens the Mending Bench menu (scripted focus moves) and crafts a Straw Bed | 10 s |\n768\t| 4 | \"Raising a room\": walls 2 high, door, bed, torch, then \"ROOM COMPLETE! Bedroom\" and Cheer bubbles | 24 s |\n769\t| 5 | \"Skirmish\": a combo and a Spin Stitch against Lintlings and a Pinhog; drops | 16 s |\n770\t| 6 | \"Nightfall\": the hour jumps to 21:00; lanterns glow; Mothlets; residents go to bed | 10 s |\n771\t| 7 | \"Siege\": a wave at the walls; ballista fire; Mortimer fights; a Knotbrute smashes a wall | 18 s |\n772\t| 8 | \"Gnarlgrim\": Gnarlgrim at 30% HP; manned ballista hits → TANGLED → Seam Ripper strike → he unravels; confetti | 16 s |\n773\t| 9 | \"Quilting Bee\": 3 bot builders in seat colours raise the Quilt Barn on the Bee world | 10 s |\n774\t\n775\t### 6.21 Multiplayer: AI Hub sessions and the Quilting Bee (`DQ.Net`, `DQ.Bee`)\n776\t**Mode:** `shared-world` (co-op building is the source's multiplayer), 1–4 players, 8-minute rounds. Head tags, written by `build.mjs`:\n777\t```html\n778\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n779\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n780\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n781\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"8\">\n782\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Raise the Quilt Barn together, then defend it\">\n783\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move stick/WASD · Jump ✕/Space · Break □/Click · Place △/Right-click\">\n784\t```\n785\tAlso include a description meta, `hub:type` (\"Game\") and `hub:players` (\"1\": players per device, as in the hub's example pages; sessions take 1–4 through `hub:session-players`). `session.js` (unmodified, between its banner and last line) is the first script after `<meta charset>`.\n786\t\n787\t**Session flow** (only when `AIHub.active`; otherwise nothing changes):\n788\t1. No title, attract, menus or saves. Boot straight into `bee` with the Bee world generated (fixed seed `0xBEE` plus the Bee stamp; identical on every copy).\n789\t2. `info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`, fall back to the normal game (title).\n790\t3. The **game's own card** (`hubcard` scene):\n791\t   - The logo, the players lined up as builders in seat colours (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`; names drawn with the pixel font, never inserted as HTML), the goal, this player's control glyphs, ready ticks, and the countdown from the `card` event (`left`).\n792\t   - **Ready:** Enter, ✕ or a click → `AIHub.answer('ready')`. **More time:** △, T or a click → `AIHub.answer('more')` (a second press takes it back).\n793\t   - Nothing here changes the round. Any card randomness uses `AIHub.rng('card')`.\n794\t4. Call `AIHub.ready()` once, as soon as the world is built (no key press).\n795\t5. On `start`: a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Play begins when `AIHub.time() ≥ 0`.\n796\t6. **Round clock:** 8:00, or `limitMs` if shorter.\n797\t   - **Build phase** (5:00): raise the Quilt Barn Pattern, scaled by player count:\n798\t\n799\t     | Players | Barn walls | Blocks |\n800\t     |---|---|---|\n801\t     | 1 | 2 high | 38 |\n802\t     | 2 | 3 high | 57 |\n803\t     | 3–4 | 3 high + 10 Felt crenellations | 67 |\n804\t\n805\t     Plus a door and 2 torches.\n806\t   - **Raid phase** (3:00): 3 waves from the corners. Knotbrutes target the barn and the Banner (HP 200).\n807\t7. **Scores** (per player):\n808\t   - +3 per Pattern block placed (the host credits the placer)\n809\t   - +5 per monster defeated (Knotbrute +15)\n810\t   - +5 per door or torch crafted and placed in the Pattern\n811\t   - +1 per 5 items gathered\n812\t8. **Host ends the round.** At the time limit, on `time-up` (within `graceMs`), or when every player has left, the host calls `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won, stats })`:\n813\t   - `standings`: arrays of ids by score, ties grouped.\n814\t   - `won`: the Pattern is complete and the Banner HP > 0.\n815\t   - `stats`: per id `{ Blocks, Monsters }`.\n816\t   - Awards: \"Master Quilter\" for the most blocks, \"Knot Cutter\" for the most kills.\n817\t9. On `end`: stop input, `audio.mute(true)` and `music(null)`, show \"Round over\", then do nothing more. On `leave`: remove that builder (its placed blocks stay). In **server mode** (`AIHub.server`): run the host simulation with no local builder, camera or input, and skip drawing except a 1-fps static \"Hosting the Bee\" frame.\n818\t10. **The round never pauses.** Build the game with `autoPause: false`, `corner: false` and `menu: false` when `AIHub.active`, and make the `bee` scene `pausable: false` in a session. Options/Esc only show the toast \"Round in progress!\". There are no settings menus mid-round, and settings are at their defaults in a session, because the hub gives the game empty storage.\n819\t11. **A hidden tab keeps the world running.** `requestAnimationFrame` stops in hidden tabs, so while `document.hidden`, a `setInterval` of 250 ms calls `game.advance(elapsed)` (capped at 1 s per call). The host keeps simulating and every copy keeps sending; the hub voids a round only after 20 s hidden.\n820\t12. **Names** come from the hub and are drawn with the pixel font. Characters it lacks become `?`. They are never inserted as HTML.\n821\t\n822\t**Authority and messages.** Every copy runs the voxel world locally and keeps it in sync through edits. The host owns monsters, drops, Pattern progress, the clock and scores. Each client owns its own builder's movement, inventory and hotbar.\n823\t\n824\t| Msg | Direction | Reliable | Rate | Payload (compact arrays, integers) |\n825\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n826\t| `p` | client → host | no | 10 Hz | `[t, x, y, z, facing×100, anim, heldItem, hp]` |\n827\t| `a` | client → host | yes | ≤ 6 Hz, batched | `ops: [['place', x,y,z, block, rot], ['break', x,y,z], ['hit', monId, dmg, kx, ky], ['take', dropId], ['craft', item]]` |\n828\t| `s` | host → all | no | 10 Hz | `{ t, P: [[id,x,y,z,f,anim,held,hp]...], M: [[id,kind,x,y,z,f,hp,state]...], ph, left, prog, bhp }`, kept under 1,100 bytes (if more than 24 monsters, alternate halves) |\n829\t| `e` | host → all | yes | ≤ 6 Hz, batched | `ev: [['vox', x,y,z, id, by], ['drop', id, item, n, x,y,z], ['gone', dropId], ['got', to, item, n], ['hurt', to, dmg, kx, ky], ['fx', kind, x,y,z], ['phase', name], ['score', id, n]]` |\n830\t\n831\t- Per copy that is ≤ 16 messages/s, under the 20/s cap. Every payload stays far below 16 KB; assert this in tests.\n832\t- **Prediction:** a client applies its own place/break immediately and marks the cell pending; the host's `vox` event confirms or corrects it. Monsters on clients interpolate between snapshots (100 ms buffer).\n833\t- **Hits:** a client sends `hit` when its local swing overlaps a monster's interpolated position, and the host applies damage. Old or out-of-order snapshots are dropped by `t`.\n834\t- **Networking goes only through `AIHub.send`/`on`.** No sockets, fetch, `alert`, `window.open` or navigation. \"Quit to title\" does nothing in a session.\n835\t\n836\t**Outside the hub (the game on its own).** The title's **Quilting Bee** runs the same round solo: the local copy is the host, with a `LoopbackNet` that implements the same `send`/`on` surface in process. Optionally 1–3 **bot builders** run on `DQ.Autopilot` (\"Bee with friends: bots on/off\"). This exercises the host code paths outside the hub. Best scores go to `E.store`.\n837\t\n838\t**The Bee world: Bumblebee Meadow** (stamp `beeMeadow`, 48×48×16, fixed seed `0xBEE`, a 2-column void ring):\n839\t\n840\t| Feature | Location / rule |\n841\t|---|---|\n842\t| Banner (HP 200) | (24,26) |\n843\t| Pattern site (Quilt Barn, 7×5) | (26..32, 16..20), pre-placed when the round starts |\n844\t| Mending Bench and Hearth Pot | (20,26) |\n845\t| Supply Crate | (22,28). Interact for 10 Planks, once per 60 s per player |\n846\t| Trees | 6, NE (34..44, 6..14) |\n847\t| Stone outcrops with gravel | 4, NW (4..12, 6..14) |\n848\t| Cotton puffs and berry bushes | 6, S (16..32, 38..44) |\n849\t| Tufts | W (4..10, 24..34) |\n850\t| Monster spawns | the 4 corners |\n851\t\n852\t- Every Bee recipe is known from the start; there are no town levels.\n853\t- Each player keeps their own bag.\n854\t- The barn needs Cobble, Planks and Felt (Red only: berries give dye), plus a Lattice Door and 2 Wick Torches. Lint for the torches comes from the Supply Crate (2 Lint on each player's first use) and from the few Lintlings that roam the meadow during the build phase.\n855\t\n856\t### 6.22 Touch-only devices\n857\tDetection: `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`, and no gamepad seen and no `keydown` yet. Re-evaluate when a pad connects or a key is pressed.\n858\t\n859\tOn such devices:\n860\t- Every scene has `touch: []` and `input.touchEnabled = false`: **no touch controls**.\n861\t- The title shows a stitched panel, \"**Controller or keyboard required**\", with the line \"Connect a controller or keyboard to play.\" The engine's corner **settings/menu button** stays (`corner` on).\n862\t- The attract mode runs as usual, and a tap returns to the title panel.\n863\t\n864\t### 6.23 Settings and accessibility (additions to the standard Settings)\n865\t| Setting | Default |\n866\t|---|---|\n867\t| Button glyphs | PlayStation, or Xbox (sets `input.glyphs`) |\n868\t| Camera view | Isometric, or Three-quarter |\n869\t| Auto-hop | On |\n870\t| Sprint | Hold, or Toggle |\n871\t| Text speed | Slow, Normal, Fast |\n872\t| Voices volume | 80% |\n873\t| HUD tracker | On |\n874\t| Difficulty | Normal (Relaxed / Hard) |\n875\t| Cutaway | Auto (Always, Off) |\n876\t| Screen shake and flashes | Engine standard |\n877\t\n878\tReduce motion: the engine handles it; the game also disables the camera crossfade and reduces confetti density.\n879\t\n880\t### 6.24 About/Credits screen and exit interview\n881\t- `tools/build.mjs` reads `./exit-interview.json`, if present, and embeds its `sourceInformation`, `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback` and `promptFeedback` as `DQ.ABOUT`. The About pages render them.\n882\t  - If the file is missing during development, a dev-only notice says so. A release build fails without it.\n883\t  - Order matters: write `exit-interview.json` before the final build.\n884\t- **Credits page:**\n885\t  - \"Dragon Quilt Builders: an original game inspired by Dragon Quest Builders (Square Enix). Not affiliated with or endorsed by Square Enix. No assets from the original.\"\n886\t  - Design, code, art, music and writing: the AI model (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5) under direction.\n887\t  - Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT) by michaelcrosato, with the list of our engine changes.\n888\t  - AI Hub Session client 1.4.0.\n889\t  - Plan: PLAN.md.\n890\t- **Source Information** fields (TASK.md), with \"unknown\" where something cannot be confirmed:\n891\t  - Date `2026-11-03 17:47:40`\n892\t  - Source `Dragon Quest Builders`\n893\t  - Plan `PLAN.md`\n894\t  - Genre and perspective: \"Sandbox action RPG (block building, crafting, town building); isometric 2.5D third person, rotatable\"\n895\t  - Tech, art direction, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment (`Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown`), model (`Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max`), usage\n896\t  - Research: the plan stage read the engine handoff (API card, guide, the renderer, TileMap, Body and Dialog source) and the AI Hub protocol and examples. It did no web research on the source game. Add whatever research the build stage does.\n897\t- **Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback** answer TASK.md's questions in order. The same text goes in the final chat reply.\n898\t- **`exit-interview.json`** (the exact shape in TASK.md). Validate it with `node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`. Suggested `aiHub` values:\n899\t\n900\t  | Field | Value |\n901\t  |---|---|\n902\t  | name | \"Dragon Quilt Builders\" |\n903\t  | tagline | \"Stitch a frayed world back together\" |\n904\t  | description | \"Gather, craft and build block by block to mend a patchwork kingdom unravelled by a dragon. Raise rooms for quirky residents, defend your town and unpick a knotted boss, or raise a barn together in co-op Quilting Bee rounds.\" |\n905\t  | type | \"Game\" |\n906\t  | genre | \"RPG\" |\n907\t  | source | \"Dragon Quest Builders\" |\n908\t  | series | \"Dragon Quest\" |\n909\t  | players | \"1–4\" |\n910\t  | controls | \"Stick/WASD move · ✕/Space jump · □/Click break · △/Right-click place · L1 R1/wheel items · ○/F talk\" |\n911\t  | tags | [\"building\", \"crafting\", \"co-op\", \"fantasy\", \"comedy\", \"pixel art\"] |\n912\t\n913\t### 6.25 Autopilot: the shared AI (`DQ.Autopilot`)\n914\tThe attract mode, the scripted playthrough test, the Bee bots and cutscene NPC walks all use one task runner. It emits **intents** (§6.4), so the AI plays through the real builder, physics, crafting and UI code; it never edits state directly unless a cheat says so.\n915\t- **Setup:** `const ap = new DQ.Autopilot(ctx, builder, { cheats: { god, teleport, infinite, fastPlace }, ui: true })`. Each task is a `game.script` generator; `ap.run(task)` returns its script, and `ap.intent()` is read once per step in place of `Controls.intent()`.\n916\t- **Tasks:**\n917\t\n918\t  | Task | What it does |\n919\t  |---|---|\n920\t  | `walkTo(x, y, z, { radius = 1 })` | Paths with `DQ.Nav` and steers along it, hopping on step-ups. With `cheats.teleport`, a path longer than 40 blocks, or one that fails, becomes a teleport behind a `'wipe'` transition |\n921\t  | `face(x, y)` | Turns the builder toward a point |\n922\t  | `breakAt(x, y, z)` | Walks into reach, sets `intent.cursor` and swings until the block is gone |\n923\t  | `placeAt(x, y, z, item, rot)` | Walks into reach, selects the hotbar slot (it adds the item to the hotbar if needed) and places |\n924\t  | `buildStamp(stamp, origin, { rate })` | Places a stamp's cells bottom-up, near to far, never trapping itself (it stands outside the footprint) |\n925\t  | `craft(recipeId, n)` | With `ui: true`, walks to the station, interacts and drives the crafting screen through MENU actions; with `ui: false`, calls `Crafting.craft` beside the station |\n926\t  | `talk(npcId, choices = [])` | Walks to the NPC, interacts, confirms pages, and picks choices by index |\n927\t  | `fight({ filter, spin = true })` | Approaches the nearest match, combos, uses a Spin Stitch when 2 or more are within 2 blocks, and backs off on a wind-up telegraph |\n928\t  | `manBallista(id, target)` | Mounts the ballista, aims and fires until the target is Tangled or dead |\n929\t  | `wait(s)`, `until(fn, timeout)` | Waits |\n930\t\n931\t- **Failure:** every task has a timeout (walk 30 s, break 8 s, craft 10 s, fight 60 s) and fails with an `apFail` event `{ task, reason, at }`. Tests assert there are none. The attract mode skips to the next vignette instead.\n932\t- **Cheats** are applied by the Autopilot before the task: `god` sets the dev god flag, `infinite` tops up the bag, `fastPlace` sets the place cooldown to 0.15 s, and `teleport` is described under `walkTo`.\n933\t\n934\t---\n935\t## 7. Content\n936\t\n937\t### 7.1 The world: the Patchwork Realm around Thimbleton (144 × 144 columns, 24 levels, seed 20261103)\n938\t\n939\t```\n940\t x→ 0                 48                 96                 143\n941\ty 0 +-------------------+------------------+------------------+\n942\t  | CORDUROY CRAGS      |                  |   TWEEDWOOD      |\n943\t  |  Button Quarry(22,18)                  |   Lint Camp      |\n944\t  |  ledge button(48,8) |                  |   (116,30)       |\n945\t  |   Lint Hollow(38,40)>exit(50,42)      |   hollow-tree    |\n946\t48|...................  GINGHAM GLEN  .....|...button(134,52)|\n947\t  |  stream ~           (meadow)           |                  |\n948\t  |      ~       THIMBLETON  banner(72,76)  Rift(96,80)        |\n949\t  |        ~     border r=10..20       east road → (143,76)   |\n950\t96|   ~                                     |FRAYED VERGE| THE FRAYING\n951\t  | CALICO COAST         (glen south)       |x92-98 |KnotWall x99-100\n952\t  |  shipwreck+button(22,126)               |       | Knotted Spire(124,124)\n953\t  |  sea x<14, y>132                        |       | gate y112-114\n954\t143+-------------------+------------------+------------------+\n955\t```\n956\tSurface levels are approximate (generated with `E.noise2` plus per-region shaping). The outer 2-column ring is void with frayed fringe.\n957\t\n958\t| Region | Area | Surface | Top / under | Features | Spawns (day / night) |\n959\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n960\t| **Lint Hollow** (start) | centre (38,40), floor 12×10 | floor L7, rim L13–14 | earth, stone walls | Dead bushes (branches), 6 loose earth blocks, one Lintling. A dark 6-block tunnel (2-high, roofed: tests the cutaway) with stairs up to the exit at (50,42). A \"darkness zone\" (ambient .15 until a torch is placed at the marked spot) | 1 scripted Lintling |\n961\t| **Gingham Glen** | x 44–100, y 44–104 (minus the town) | L9 ± 2, gentle hills | grass / 3 earth / stone | Tufts (1 per 12 cells), cotton puffs, thimbleberry bushes near the town, buttercups, a shallow stream (1-deep water) from (30,58) winding to the coast at (18,132), trees in small groves | Lintling 70%, Dyed Lintling 30% / Lintling 40%, Mothlet 60% |\n962\t| **Thimbleton** | banner spot (72,76) on a cobble plaza 5×5 | L9 flattened in radius 12 | grass, cobble ruins | Ruined foundations: three 1-high cobble outlines of old houses (hints for room shapes), a broken well, a pond 3×4 (decor water), the east road (planks path) to the world's eastern edge (141,76), the Thimbleton Bobbin Portal at (70,80) | none inside the border |\n963\t| **Corduroy Crags** | x 4–60, y 4–56 | L11–20 ridges | stone, gravel on slopes, grass in hollows | Exposed copper (levels 7–12) and coal (8–14) veins as blobs, 4–7 blocks each, Poisson-spaced. The Button Quarry. A Bobbin Portal at the Hollow exit (52,44) and at the Quarry rim (34,20). The Ancient Button on a stone pillar ledge at (48,8), L19 (a stairs puzzle) | Lintling, Pinhog / Lintling, Pinhog |\n964\t| **Button Quarry** | open pit (22,18), 16×14, floor L6 | rim L15 | stone terraces | Ramp from the east, terraces with coal and copper, 4 cobble pillars, boss arena floor 12×10. Sir Buttonsworth sleeps here (wakes on entry after M10) | Pinhog (before the boss) |\n965\t| **Tweedwood** | x 92–140, y 6–60 | L10 ± 2 | grass / earth | Dense voxel trees: trunk 3–5 Plaid Logs, Pompom leaf blob radius 2, Poisson spacing ~4. Puffballs in shade, bluebells at the edges. The Lint Camp at (116,30): Lintwick in a Knot Cage (a 1×1 object, HP 30, tier 2), 3 Dyed Lintlings, knot-fence decor. A hollow tree with the Ancient Button at (134,52). A Bobbin Portal at (110,36) | Dyed Lintling / Mothlet, Dyed Lintling |\n966\t| **Calico Coast** | x 4–60, y 100–140 | sand L7–8; sea L5–6 water where height < 7 | sand / sand / stone | Beach, dunes, cotton puffs, a shipwreck stamp (plank hull) holding the Ancient Button at (22,126), a Bobbin Portal at (30,116) | Pinhog / Pinhog, Mothlet |\n967\t| **Frayed Verge** | x 92–98, y 92–140 | L9 | frayed cloth patches | The approach to the Knot Wall; a Bobbin Portal at (94,110) | Frayed Knight (night only, plus 2 fixed guards by day near the gate) |\n968\t| **The Knot Wall** | x 99–100, y 92–141, up to L17 | — | knot (tier 3) | The **Knot Gate** at y 112–114 (3 wide × 5 high) is the only way through (Seam Ripper) | — |\n969\t| **The Fraying** | x 101–140, y 94–140 | L9 ± 3 with void holes | frayed / earth / stone | Void holes (columns emptied entirely, Backing Cloth included: falling in = unravel respawn), dead trees, ash weather, purple ambient. The **Knotted Spire** at (124,124): a 9×9 base of knot and frayed blocks up to L22, with an outer spiral ramp 2 wide (stairs blocks), and a pedestal with the Golden Thread fragment on top | Frayed Knight, Knotbrute (rare) / the same, ×1.5 |\n970\t| **The Rift** | (96,80), radius 3 | void | — | A frayed hole east of town that is the siege's spawn point. Re-stitched at the chapter's end (a wave turns it into meadow) | Frayed Knight at night |\n971\t\n972\tStamps (`DQ.WORLD.stamps`, ASCII layers bottom to top with a legend; `' '` means keep the existing block, `.` air):\n973\t- `lintHollow`, `thimbletonRuins`, `buttonQuarry`, `lintCamp`, `shipwreck`, `knotGate`, `knottedSpire`, `rift`\n974\t- `portalPad` (×6), `demoTown` (the attract/title diorama: Lv4 Thimbleton), `beeMeadow` (48×48 Bee world)\n975\t- `beat_*` stamps for dev story beats (the rooms a player would have built by then)\n976\t\n977\tWorldgen order:\n978\t1. heightmap\n979\t2. layers\n980\t3. ores\n981\t4. water fill\n982\t5. trees\n983\t6. plants (objects)\n984\t7. stamps\n985\t8. portals\n986\t9. `escape` sweep\n987\t10. nav build\n988\t11. `world.base` snapshot\n989\t\n990\tMust be deterministic: test it by `world.hash()` equality across two runs.\n991\t\n992\t### 7.2 Blocks (`DQ.BLOCKS`)\n993\tTexture names refer to the patterns in §8.1. \"tier\" is the minimum tool tier to break it (∞ = never). Every face has stitched seams.\n994\t\n995\t| id | key | Name | hp | tier | Drops | Placeable | Texture (top / side) | Notes |\n996\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n997\t| 1 | backing | Backing Cloth | ∞ | ∞ | — | no | navy felt with a square stitch grid | level 0 |\n998\t| 2 | earth | Corduroy Earth | 6 | 1 | earth | yes | brown cord crosshatch / vertical cord ribs | |\n999\t| 3 | grass | Gingham Turf | 6 | 1 | earth | no (gives earth) | green gingham checks / earth with a 3-texel felt fringe | |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"9ae64516-7b0a-4ef0-a735-8418ec9d8363","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:55:42.331Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","content":"- **Overlay:** a small logo top left, \"DEMO\" top right (below the corner area), and a blinking \"Press any button\" with the current device's glyph.\n- **Vignettes**, looping forever with a 4 s logo card between loops (each written as a `game.script` and also run by a test):\n\n| # | Vignette | Length |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | \"Morning in Thimbleton\": camera glide; residents at their jobs; banner flutter | 12 s |\n| 2 | \"Gathering\": breaks turf and tufts in the Glen; pickups; a Notion popup | 14 s |\n| 3 | \"Crafting\": opens the Mending Bench menu (scripted focus moves) and crafts a Straw Bed | 10 s |\n| 4 | \"Raising a room\": walls 2 high, door, bed, torch, then \"ROOM COMPLETE! Bedroom\" and Cheer bubbles | 24 s |\n| 5 | \"Skirmish\": a combo and a Spin Stitch against Lintlings and a Pinhog; drops | 16 s |\n| 6 | \"Nightfall\": the hour jumps to 21:00; lanterns glow; Mothlets; residents go to bed | 10 s |\n| 7 | \"Siege\": a wave at the walls; ballista fire; Mortimer fights; a Knotbrute smashes a wall | 18 s |\n| 8 | \"Gnarlgrim\": Gnarlgrim at 30% HP; manned ballista hits → TANGLED → Seam Ripper strike → he unravels; confetti | 16 s |\n| 9 | \"Quilting Bee\": 3 bot builders in seat colours raise the Quilt Barn on the Bee world | 10 s |\n\n### 6.21 Multiplayer: AI Hub sessions and the Quilting Bee (`DQ.Net`, `DQ.Bee`)\n**Mode:** `shared-world` (co-op building is the source's multiplayer), 1–4 players, 8-minute rounds. Head tags, written by `build.mjs`:\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"8\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Raise the Quilt Barn together, then defend it\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move stick/WASD · Jump ✕/Space · Break □/Click · Place △/Right-click\">\n```\nAlso include a description meta, `hub:type` (\"Game\") and `hub:players` (\"1\": players per device, as in the hub's example pages; sessions take 1–4 through `hub:session-players`). `session.js` (unmodified, between its banner and last line) is the first script after `<meta charset>`.\n\n**Session flow** (only when `AIHub.active`; otherwise nothing changes):\n1. No title, attract, menus or saves. Boot straight into `bee` with the Bee world generated (fixed seed `0xBEE` plus the Bee stamp; identical on every copy).\n2. `info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`, fall back to the normal game (title).\n3. The **game's own card** (`hubcard` scene):\n   - The logo, the players lined up as builders in seat colours (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`; names drawn with the pixel font, never inserted as HTML), the goal, this player's control glyphs, ready ticks, and the countdown from the `card` event (`left`).\n   - **Ready:** Enter, ✕ or a click → `AIHub.answer('ready')`. **More time:** △, T or a click → `AIHub.answer('more')` (a second press takes it back).\n   - Nothing here changes the round. Any card randomness uses `AIHub.rng('card')`.\n4. Call `AIHub.ready()` once, as soon as the world is built (no key press).\n5. On `start`: a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Play begins when `AIHub.time() ≥ 0`.\n6. **Round clock:** 8:00, or `limitMs` if shorter.\n   - **Build phase** (5:00): raise the Quilt Barn Pattern, scaled by player count:\n\n     | Players | Barn walls | Blocks |\n     |---|---|---|\n     | 1 | 2 high | 38 |\n     | 2 | 3 high | 57 |\n     | 3–4 | 3 high + 10 Felt crenellations | 67 |\n\n     Plus a door and 2 torches.\n   - **Raid phase** (3:00): 3 waves from the corners. Knotbrutes target the barn and the Banner (HP 200).\n7. **Scores** (per player):\n   - +3 per Pattern block placed (the host credits the placer)\n   - +5 per monster defeated (Knotbrute +15)\n   - +5 per door or torch crafted and placed in the Pattern\n   - +1 per 5 items gathered\n8. **Host ends the round.** At the time limit, on `time-up` (within `graceMs`), or when every player has left, the host calls `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won, stats })`:\n   - `standings`: arrays of ids by score, ties grouped.\n   - `won`: the Pattern is complete and the Banner HP > 0.\n   - `stats`: per id `{ Blocks, Monsters }`.\n   - Awards: \"Master Quilter\" for the most blocks, \"Knot Cutter\" for the most kills.\n9. On `end`: stop input, `audio.mute(true)` and `music(null)`, show \"Round over\", then do nothing more. On `leave`: remove that builder (its placed blocks stay). In **server mode** (`AIHub.server`): run the host simulation with no local builder, camera or input, and skip drawing except a 1-fps static \"Hosting the Bee\" frame.\n10. **The round never pauses.** Build the game with `autoPause: false`, `corner: false` and `menu: false` when `AIHub.active`, and make the `bee` scene `pausable: false` in a session. Options/Esc only show the toast \"Round in progress!\". There are no settings menus mid-round, and settings are at their defaults in a session, because the hub gives the game empty storage.\n11. **A hidden tab keeps the world running.** `requestAnimationFrame` stops in hidden tabs, so while `document.hidden`, a `setInterval` of 250 ms calls `game.advance(elapsed)` (capped at 1 s per call). The host keeps simulating and every copy keeps sending; the hub voids a round only after 20 s hidden.\n12. **Names** come from the hub and are drawn with the pixel font. Characters it lacks become `?`. They are never inserted as HTML.\n\n**Authority and messages.** Every copy runs the voxel world locally and keeps it in sync through edits. The host owns monsters, drops, Pattern progress, the clock and scores. Each client owns its own builder's movement, inventory and hotbar.\n\n| Msg | Direction | Reliable | Rate | Payload (compact arrays, integers) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `p` | client → host | no | 10 Hz | `[t, x, y, z, facing×100, anim, heldItem, hp]` |\n| `a` | client → host | yes | ≤ 6 Hz, batched | `ops: [['place', x,y,z, block, rot], ['break', x,y,z], ['hit', monId, dmg, kx, ky], ['take', dropId], ['craft', item]]` |\n| `s` | host → all | no | 10 Hz | `{ t, P: [[id,x,y,z,f,anim,held,hp]...], M: [[id,kind,x,y,z,f,hp,state]...], ph, left, prog, bhp }`, kept under 1,100 bytes (if more than 24 monsters, alternate halves) |\n| `e` | host → all | yes | ≤ 6 Hz, batched | `ev: [['vox', x,y,z, id, by], ['drop', id, item, n, x,y,z], ['gone', dropId], ['got', to, item, n], ['hurt', to, dmg, kx, ky], ['fx', kind, x,y,z], ['phase', name], ['score', id, n]]` |\n\n- Per copy that is ≤ 16 messages/s, under the 20/s cap. Every payload stays far below 16 KB; assert this in tests.\n- **Prediction:** a client applies its own place/break immediately and marks the cell pending; the host's `vox` event confirms or corrects it. Monsters on clients interpolate between snapshots (100 ms buffer).\n- **Hits:** a client sends `hit` when its local swing overlaps a monster's interpolated position, and the host applies damage. Old or out-of-order snapshots are dropped by `t`.\n- **Networking goes only through `AIHub.send`/`on`.** No sockets, fetch, `alert`, `window.open` or navigation. \"Quit to title\" does nothing in a session.\n\n**Outside the hub (the game on its own).** The title's **Quilting Bee** runs the same round solo: the local copy is the host, with a `LoopbackNet` that implements the same `send`/`on` surface in process. Optionally 1–3 **bot builders** run on `DQ.Autopilot` (\"Bee with friends: bots on/off\"). This exercises the host code paths outside the hub. Best scores go to `E.store`.\n\n**The Bee world: Bumblebee Meadow** (stamp `beeMeadow`, 48×48×16, fixed seed `0xBEE`, a 2-column void ring):\n\n| Feature | Location / rule |\n|---|---|\n| Banner (HP 200) | (24,26) |\n| Pattern site (Quilt Barn, 7×5) | (26..32, 16..20), pre-placed when the round starts |\n| Mending Bench and Hearth Pot | (20,26) |\n| Supply Crate | (22,28). Interact for 10 Planks, once per 60 s per player |\n| Trees | 6, NE (34..44, 6..14) |\n| Stone outcrops with gravel | 4, NW (4..12, 6..14) |\n| Cotton puffs and berry bushes | 6, S (16..32, 38..44) |\n| Tufts | W (4..10, 24..34) |\n| Monster spawns | the 4 corners |\n\n- Every Bee recipe is known from the start; there are no town levels.\n- Each player keeps their own bag.\n- The barn needs Cobble, Planks and Felt (Red only: berries give dye), plus a Lattice Door and 2 Wick Torches. Lint for the torches comes from the Supply Crate (2 Lint on each player's first use) and from the few Lintlings that roam the meadow during the build phase.\n\n### 6.22 Touch-only devices\nDetection: `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`, and no gamepad seen and no `keydown` yet. Re-evaluate when a pad connects or a key is pressed.\n\nOn such devices:\n- Every scene has `touch: []` and `input.touchEnabled = false`: **no touch controls**.\n- The title shows a stitched panel, \"**Controller or keyboard required**\", with the line \"Connect a controller or keyboard to play.\" The engine's corner **settings/menu button** stays (`corner` on).\n- The attract mode runs as usual, and a tap returns to the title panel.\n\n### 6.23 Settings and accessibility (additions to the standard Settings)\n| Setting | Default |\n|---|---|\n| Button glyphs | PlayStation, or Xbox (sets `input.glyphs`) |\n| Camera view | Isometric, or Three-quarter |\n| Auto-hop | On |\n| Sprint | Hold, or Toggle |\n| Text speed | Slow, Normal, Fast |\n| Voices volume | 80% |\n| HUD tracker | On |\n| Difficulty | Normal (Relaxed / Hard) |\n| Cutaway | Auto (Always, Off) |\n| Screen shake and flashes | Engine standard |\n\nReduce motion: the engine handles it; the game also disables the camera crossfade and reduces confetti density.\n\n### 6.24 About/Credits screen and exit interview\n- `tools/build.mjs` reads `./exit-interview.json`, if present, and embeds its `sourceInformation`, `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback` and `promptFeedback` as `DQ.ABOUT`. The About pages render them.\n  - If the file is missing during development, a dev-only notice says so. A release build fails without it.\n  - Order matters: write `exit-interview.json` before the final build.\n- **Credits page:**\n  - \"Dragon Quilt Builders: an original game inspired by Dragon Quest Builders (Square Enix). Not affiliated with or endorsed by Square Enix. No assets from the original.\"\n  - Design, code, art, music and writing: the AI model (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5) under direction.\n  - Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT) by michaelcrosato, with the list of our engine changes.\n  - AI Hub Session client 1.4.0.\n  - Plan: PLAN.md.\n- **Source Information** fields (TASK.md), with \"unknown\" where something cannot be confirmed:\n  - Date `2026-11-03 17:47:40`\n  - Source `Dragon Quest Builders`\n  - Plan `PLAN.md`\n  - Genre and perspective: \"Sandbox action RPG (block building, crafting, town building); isometric 2.5D third person, rotatable\"\n  - Tech, art direction, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment (`Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown`), model (`Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max`), usage\n  - Research: the plan stage read the engine handoff (API card, guide, the renderer, TileMap, Body and Dialog source) and the AI Hub protocol and examples. It did no web research on the source game. Add whatever research the build stage does.\n- **Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback** answer TASK.md's questions in order. The same text goes in the final chat reply.\n- **`exit-interview.json`** (the exact shape in TASK.md). Validate it with `node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`. Suggested `aiHub` values:\n\n  | Field | Value |\n  |---|---|\n  | name | \"Dragon Quilt Builders\" |\n  | tagline | \"Stitch a frayed world back together\" |\n  | description | \"Gather, craft and build block by block to mend a patchwork kingdom unravelled by a dragon. Raise rooms for quirky residents, defend your town and unpick a knotted boss, or raise a barn together in co-op Quilting Bee rounds.\" |\n  | type | \"Game\" |\n  | genre | \"RPG\" |\n  | source | \"Dragon Quest Builders\" |\n  | series | \"Dragon Quest\" |\n  | players | \"1–4\" |\n  | controls | \"Stick/WASD move · ✕/Space jump · □/Click break · △/Right-click place · L1 R1/wheel items · ○/F talk\" |\n  | tags | [\"building\", \"crafting\", \"co-op\", \"fantasy\", \"comedy\", \"pixel art\"] |\n\n### 6.25 Autopilot: the shared AI (`DQ.Autopilot`)\nThe attract mode, the scripted playthrough test, the Bee bots and cutscene NPC walks all use one task runner. It emits **intents** (§6.4), so the AI plays through the real builder, physics, crafting and UI code; it never edits state directly unless a cheat says so.\n- **Setup:** `const ap = new DQ.Autopilot(ctx, builder, { cheats: { god, teleport, infinite, fastPlace }, ui: true })`. Each task is a `game.script` generator; `ap.run(task)` returns its script, and `ap.intent()` is read once per step in place of `Controls.intent()`.\n- **Tasks:**\n\n  | Task | What it does |\n  |---|---|\n  | `walkTo(x, y, z, { radius = 1 })` | Paths with `DQ.Nav` and steers along it, hopping on step-ups. With `cheats.teleport`, a path longer than 40 blocks, or one that fails, becomes a teleport behind a `'wipe'` transition |\n  | `face(x, y)` | Turns the builder toward a point |\n  | `breakAt(x, y, z)` | Walks into reach, sets `intent.cursor` and swings until the block is gone |\n  | `placeAt(x, y, z, item, rot)` | Walks into reach, selects the hotbar slot (it adds the item to the hotbar if needed) and places |\n  | `buildStamp(stamp, origin, { rate })` | Places a stamp's cells bottom-up, near to far, never trapping itself (it stands outside the footprint) |\n  | `craft(recipeId, n)` | With `ui: true`, walks to the station, interacts and drives the crafting screen through MENU actions; with `ui: false`, calls `Crafting.craft` beside the station |\n  | `talk(npcId, choices = [])` | Walks to the NPC, interacts, confirms pages, and picks choices by index |\n  | `fight({ filter, spin = true })` | Approaches the nearest match, combos, uses a Spin Stitch when 2 or more are within 2 blocks, and backs off on a wind-up telegraph |\n  | `manBallista(id, target)` | Mounts the ballista, aims and fires until the target is Tangled or dead |\n  | `wait(s)`, `until(fn, timeout)` | Waits |\n\n- **Failure:** every task has a timeout (walk 30 s, break 8 s, craft 10 s, fight 60 s) and fails with an `apFail` event `{ task, reason, at }`. Tests assert there are none. The attract mode skips to the next vignette instead.\n- **Cheats** are applied by the Autopilot before the task: `god` sets the dev god flag, `infinite` tops up the bag, `fastPlace` sets the place cooldown to 0.15 s, and `teleport` is described under `walkTo`.\n\n---\n## 7. Content\n\n### 7.1 The world: the Patchwork Realm around Thimbleton (144 × 144 columns, 24 levels, seed 20261103)\n\n```\n x→ 0                 48                 96                 143\ny 0 +-------------------+------------------+------------------+\n  | CORDUROY CRAGS      |                  |   TWEEDWOOD      |\n  |  Button Quarry(22,18)                  |   Lint Camp      |\n  |  ledge button(48,8) |                  |   (116,30)       |\n  |   Lint Hollow(38,40)>exit(50,42)      |   hollow-tree    |\n48|...................  GINGHAM GLEN  .....|...button(134,52)|\n  |  stream ~           (meadow)           |                  |\n  |      ~       THIMBLETON  banner(72,76)  Rift(96,80)        |\n  |        ~     border r=10..20       east road → (143,76)   |\n96|   ~                                     |FRAYED VERGE| THE FRAYING\n  | CALICO COAST         (glen south)       |x92-98 |KnotWall x99-100\n  |  shipwreck+button(22,126)               |       | Knotted Spire(124,124)\n  |  sea x<14, y>132                        |       | gate y112-114\n143+-------------------+------------------+------------------+\n```\nSurface levels are approximate (generated with `E.noise2` plus per-region shaping). The outer 2-column ring is void with frayed fringe.\n\n| Region | Area | Surface | Top / under | Features | Spawns (day / night) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Lint Hollow** (start) | centre (38,40), floor 12×10 | floor L7, rim L13–14 | earth, stone walls | Dead bushes (branches), 6 loose earth blocks, one Lintling. A dark 6-block tunnel (2-high, roofed: tests the cutaway) with stairs up to the exit at (50,42). A \"darkness zone\" (ambient .15 until a torch is placed at the marked spot) | 1 scripted Lintling |\n| **Gingham Glen** | x 44–100, y 44–104 (minus the town) | L9 ± 2, gentle hills | grass / 3 earth / stone | Tufts (1 per 12 cells), cotton puffs, thimbleberry bushes near the town, buttercups, a shallow stream (1-deep water) from (30,58) winding to the coast at (18,132), trees in small groves | Lintling 70%, Dyed Lintling 30% / Lintling 40%, Mothlet 60% |\n| **Thimbleton** | banner spot (72,76) on a cobble plaza 5×5 | L9 flattened in radius 12 | grass, cobble ruins | Ruined foundations: three 1-high cobble outlines of old houses (hints for room shapes), a broken well, a pond 3×4 (decor water), the east road (planks path) to the world's eastern edge (141,76), the Thimbleton Bobbin Portal at (70,80) | none inside the border |\n| **Corduroy Crags** | x 4–60, y 4–56 | L11–20 ridges | stone, gravel on slopes, grass in hollows | Exposed copper (levels 7–12) and coal (8–14) veins as blobs, 4–7 blocks each, Poisson-spaced. The Button Quarry. A Bobbin Portal at the Hollow exit (52,44) and at the Quarry rim (34,20). The Ancient Button on a stone pillar ledge at (48,8), L19 (a stairs puzzle) | Lintling, Pinhog / Lintling, Pinhog |\n| **Button Quarry** | open pit (22,18), 16×14, floor L6 | rim L15 | stone terraces | Ramp from the east, terraces with coal and copper, 4 cobble pillars, boss arena floor 12×10. Sir Buttonsworth sleeps here (wakes on entry after M10) | Pinhog (before the boss) |\n| **Tweedwood** | x 92–140, y 6–60 | L10 ± 2 | grass / earth | Dense voxel trees: trunk 3–5 Plaid Logs, Pompom leaf blob radius 2, Poisson spacing ~4. Puffballs in shade, bluebells at the edges. The Lint Camp at (116,30): Lintwick in a Knot Cage (a 1×1 object, HP 30, tier 2), 3 Dyed Lintlings, knot-fence decor. A hollow tree with the Ancient Button at (134,52). A Bobbin Portal at (110,36) | Dyed Lintling / Mothlet, Dyed Lintling |\n| **Calico Coast** | x 4–60, y 100–140 | sand L7–8; sea L5–6 water where height < 7 | sand / sand / stone | Beach, dunes, cotton puffs, a shipwreck stamp (plank hull) holding the Ancient Button at (22,126), a Bobbin Portal at (30,116) | Pinhog / Pinhog, Mothlet |\n| **Frayed Verge** | x 92–98, y 92–140 | L9 | frayed cloth patches | The approach to the Knot Wall; a Bobbin Portal at (94,110) | Frayed Knight (night only, plus 2 fixed guards by day near the gate) |\n| **The Knot Wall** | x 99–100, y 92–141, up to L17 | — | knot (tier 3) | The **Knot Gate** at y 112–114 (3 wide × 5 high) is the only way through (Seam Ripper) | — |\n| **The Fraying** | x 101–140, y 94–140 | L9 ± 3 with void holes | frayed / earth / stone | Void holes (columns emptied entirely, Backing Cloth included: falling in = unravel respawn), dead trees, ash weather, purple ambient. The **Knotted Spire** at (124,124): a 9×9 base of knot and frayed blocks up to L22, with an outer spiral ramp 2 wide (stairs blocks), and a pedestal with the Golden Thread fragment on top | Frayed Knight, Knotbrute (rare) / the same, ×1.5 |\n| **The Rift** | (96,80), radius 3 | void | — | A frayed hole east of town that is the siege's spawn point. Re-stitched at the chapter's end (a wave turns it into meadow) | Frayed Knight at night |\n\nStamps (`DQ.WORLD.stamps`, ASCII layers bottom to top with a legend; `' '` means keep the existing block, `.` air):\n- `lintHollow`, `thimbletonRuins`, `buttonQuarry`, `lintCamp`, `shipwreck`, `knotGate`, `knottedSpire`, `rift`\n- `portalPad` (×6), `demoTown` (the attract/title diorama: Lv4 Thimbleton), `beeMeadow` (48×48 Bee world)\n- `beat_*` stamps for dev story beats (the rooms a player would have built by then)\n\nWorldgen order:\n1. heightmap\n2. layers\n3. ores\n4. water fill\n5. trees\n6. plants (objects)\n7. stamps\n8. portals\n9. `escape` sweep\n10. nav build\n11. `world.base` snapshot\n\nMust be deterministic: test it by `world.hash()` equality across two runs.\n\n### 7.2 Blocks (`DQ.BLOCKS`)\nTexture names refer to the patterns in §8.1. \"tier\" is the minimum tool tier to break it (∞ = never). Every face has stitched seams.\n\n| id | key | Name | hp | tier | Drops | Placeable | Texture (top / side) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | backing | Backing Cloth | ∞ | ∞ | — | no | navy felt with a square stitch grid | level 0 |\n| 2 | earth | Corduroy Earth | 6 | 1 | earth | yes | brown cord crosshatch / vertical cord ribs | |\n| 3 | grass | Gingham Turf | 6 | 1 | earth | no (gives earth) | green gingham checks / earth with a 3-texel felt fringe | |","numLines":240,"startLine":760,"totalLines":1675}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01P7jFK7S4rkD6en6GfKJZyb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
