{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CEhNTe9MNF8sSEfEo1TEV4","type":"tool_result","content":"SOURCE: Dragon Quest Builders\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521.html]. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"8c9130c3-d8f5-4e76-ade3-16eed3046ef9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:36:09.009Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"SOURCE: Dragon Quest Buil