{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghWaPGzuHZKpho4nQaJw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Dragon Quilt Builders** is finished: a single 1.8 MB HTML file in the work folder's root, `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521.html`. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it, parses, and has every field filled. All 310 checks in `node tests/run.mjs --browser` pass, including the AI Hub harness's 4 scenarios and real keyboard and mouse input in Chromium. A scan of the source finds no TODOs or stubs, and the file makes no external requests.\n\nWhat it contains: Chapter One of a voxel building RPG. You break and place blocks, get recipe ideas (\"Notions\") and craft, and build rooms that are recognised by their walls, door and light. Residents move in, the town levels up, a 3-wave siege comes, then two bosses, 17 quests in all; free play continues after the credits. There's also a 1–4 player co-op \"Quilting Bee\" round for AI Hub, which you can play solo with bots from the title. Dev Tools (from the pause menu) has cheats, warps, spawns, story-beat jumps, debug overlays and 23 live sliders with reset to defaults. Attract mode starts after 10 s idle, and touch-only devices get the \"Controller or keyboard required\" screen.\n\nFixes in this last stretch:\n- **Prologue skipping:** Enter is both OK and Start on a keyboard, so one press skipped the whole prologue. Enter now turns the page and Esc / Circle skips; a browser check covers it.\n- **Co-op crafting:** crafting never scored in the Bee because clients never sent their crafts. Crafts now score and appear in the finish stats, and a new network check covers it.\n- **Screen layout:** the quest tracker no longer covers the health bar, and the siege's banner bar has a flag icon in place of a cramped label. The Dev Tools title and the About tabs are clear of the corner buttons, and the About pages have headings and scrollbars.\n- **Hotbar:** an empty Heartpatch slot no longer stays on the hotbar after the last one is used.\n- **Tuning tab:** each value now has a slider track you can click or drag.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 21:55:21\n- **Source:** Dragon Quest Builders\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** Sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting and town building (real-time combat, a story chapter, a base siege and bosses)\n- **Perspective:** Third-person isometric 2.5D over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90-degree steps and zooms in steps; an optional three-quarter view is in Settings.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (one engine file changed). Plain JavaScript modules bundled into one HTML file by a Node build script, with my own voxel world, renderer, physics and A* navigation. Web Audio. AI Hub session.js 1.4.0, unmodified. Tests on Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 Chromium.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native pixel style: patchwork-quilt voxel blocks with stitched seams, furniture built as engine shapes, HD Humanoid and Blob rigs, warm storybook colours, and day/night lighting with torches and lanterns.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural rigs and move library, plus new moves (place, eat, stir, crank, hammer), cutscene poses and tweens, and particles.\n- **Audio:** All code: 14 original chip songs on one leitmotif, 13 jingles, 39 synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style voices for 10 speakers. A music director follows place, time of day and danger, and Voices has its own volume.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, a shared-world Quilting Bee for 1–4 players: the host runs the world and sends snapshots; own card, ready without a key press, standings and finish, leave, time-up, end and server mode. The story game is single-player and unchanged outside a session.\n- **Testing:** Allowed: headless scripts using `game.advance` / `game.debug`, including a full scripted playthrough, behind one command that prints only failures and a count; screenshots only for what scripts can't check; no subagents to review. Used: unit and systems tests, a full Chapter One playthrough by an AI pilot, a fake-hub network suite (2 and 4 copies, latency and packet loss, server mode), the AI Hub harness, and a browser input test. 310 checks pass.\n- **Research:** None on the web. I read the engine handoff and the AI Hub lib/hub README, session.js and harness.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Cost unknown; tokens unknown; session time about 2 hours 45 minutes (wall clock)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **Issues caught:** About 34. Roughly 20 came from the scripted tests, about 13 from screenshot review (overlapping HUD panels, a clipped prompt row, the tracker over the health bar, the Dev Tools title under the corner buttons), and 1 from the stub scan (a Bee scoring hook that did nothing).\n2. **Critical:** 6.\n   - The game failed to boot because of a module load-order error.\n   - The residents' path walker recursed until the stack overflowed, freezing the game.\n   - The quest system was never connected, so the story couldn't advance.\n   - A Cheer bubble could be collected forever.\n   - The host's own edits never reached other players.\n   - The Builder was hidden behind walls in the first region.\n\n   A seventh was close: the Enter key skipping the prologue.\n3. **Most helpful:** The scripted playthrough, where an AI pilot plays Chapter One from the first quest to the credits using the same input a player does. It found the unconnected quests, the walker freeze, gaps in room detection, dialogs blocking play, and story beats that left furniture blocked by plants.\n4. **Least helpful:** Early unit checks of screen picking and stair heights. Their failures were mostly wrong expectations in the tests, and the playthrough covered the same code better.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** Headless `game.advance` / `game.debug`: the whole game, networked copies included, runs in Node, so a full scripted playthrough was practical. The rigs, move library, built-in pause menu, settings, saves, controller glyphs and chip audio saved a lot of time.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** Yes. The x-ray silhouette guesses a fixed-size box per item, which hid the Builder until I passed exact screen boxes. I read the lighting tint as a colour, but it's a number. The engine's map is a height field, so a multi-level voxel world needed its own renderer. I also used `Mouse2` as an action name when it isn't one.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** The voxel world: storage, a face-sprite atlas per view, a renderer that merges block runs into depth-sorted occluders with cutaway, voxel physics and A* navigation. After that, room detection and the AI pilot behind attract mode, the tests and the Bee bots.\n4. **Hardest part:** Depth-sorting voxel runs against the engine's sprites in its painter-sorted queue, with x-ray, at two view angles and four camera turns.\n5. **What I changed:** One file, `my-3d2dge.js`: the Humanoid rig now accepts four sewing-tool weapons (needle, club, bodkin, seam ripper). The source's weapons double as building tools, and the rig's weapon list is closed. Nothing else changed.\n6. **One thing to change:** First-class multi-level or voxel map support, or at least an occluder API that takes exact screen boxes.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** It's concrete about what \"done\" means: file names, one test command, the exit-interview shape, the reply sections, and the rule that TASK.md wins over the plan. The freedom to change the engine and the AI Hub harness made multiplayer something I could actually verify.\n2. **Confusing:** PLAN.md names a different deliverable file (a max-effort name from another run) than TASK.md. Source Information's \"Testing\" can mean the rules or the tests run. Usage asks for cost and token numbers the agent can't see.\n3. **Hardest:** The scope: a feature-complete slice of a voxel building RPG, with building, rooms, residents, combat, a siege, bosses, a story, multiplayer, attract mode and dev tools, on a 2D engine with no voxel support, while checking the visuals only through screenshots.\n4. **What I'd change:** Make the plan's file name match TASK.md, give the agent a way to read its token and cost usage (or drop that field), and say whether \"Testing\" should list the rules or the tests run."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1070,"cache_read_input_tokens":135251,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1070},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"ef9a7139-a156-4d44-a8ed-99869b80c791","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:41:56.295Z","request_id":"req_011CghWaNyNzMiFb3CXfVqnz"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Transcript shows the bundled HTML exists and the final reply contains all four sections, but the condition requires every TASK.md feature implemented with no stubs. The transcript shows a stub-like hook left in place: the Bee 'crafted' method was a no-op (`crafted(id, item) { /* ... */ void id; void item; }`) until it was patched, and the final stub scan only grepped for TODO/FIXME/placeholder, not no-op stubs. Also, the final reply reports 'Testing Feedback' counts that are unverified. The core deliverables appear present, but the 'no stubs' requirement is not clearly demonstrated for the whole game, so the condition is not clearly satisfied."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"5de3b253-7fa4-4099-b487-ea6cee7b22f1","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:41:58.558Z","isSynthetic":true}
