ound the unwired quests, the path walker freeze, room detection gaps, dialogs blocking play, and story beats that left furniture blocked by plants.\",\"Early unit checks of screen picking and stair heights: their failures were mostly wrong expectations in the tests themselves, not bugs in the game, and the playthrough covered the same code better.\"],\"engineFeedback\":[\"The headless game.advance and game.debug: the whole game, including networked copies, runs in Node without a browser, so a full scripted playthrough was practical. The Humanoid and Blob rigs with the move library give lively characters with no sprite work, and the built-in pause menu, settings, saves, controller glyphs and chip audio saved a lot of time.\",\"Yes. The x-ray silhouette mask guesses a fixed-size box for each queued item, which hid the Builder behind tall voxel walls until I passed exact screen boxes. I misread the lighting tint as a colour (it is a number). The engine map is a height field, so a multi-level voxel world needed its own renderer. I also used an input name that is not an action (Mouse2) before checking the held set.\",\"The voxel world: storage, a face-sprite atlas per view, a renderer that merges block runs into depth-sorted occluders with cutaway and a see-through slot toward the camera, voxel physics and A* navigation. After that, room detection and the AI pilot that drives the attract mode, the tests and the Bee bots.\",\"Depth sorting voxel runs against the engine's sprites in its painter-sorted queue (occluder, top and box rules, the lift order hook), with x-ray, at two view angles and four camera turns.\",\"One file, my-3d2dge.js: the Humanoid rig now accepts four more held items (a darning needle, a cobble club, a copper bodkin and a seam ripper) drawn in its HD drawer, because the source's weapons are also its building tools and the rig's weapon list is closed. Nothing else in the engine was changed.\",\"First-class multi-level or voxel map support in the rendere