{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghXZm5zzC1dQfVmH761V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The scan is clean. The 10 remaining hits are all intended:\n\n- the word `autoDone`, which contains \"todo\";\n- a `todo` variable holding the Bee's unfinished cells;\n- a dummy hit handler used only to measure tab widths;\n- the clipboard error catch.\n\nThe release build is rebuilt and all 312 checks pass.\n\nThe hook was right: a TODO grep wasn't enough. A wider scan for empty function bodies, unused values and cutscene skip paths found real gaps, now fixed:\n\n- **Half-built landing squash:** the Builder's hard-landing squash was set but never drawn. Landings now use the engine rig's knee-bend and jumps get a stretch.\n- **Cutscene skips:** skipping the spin demo part-way left the training dummy in the world, Mortimer frozen and no \"Spin Stitch\" note. Skipping a resident's arrival left them out of town with no move-in note. Both now finish properly, and a new systems check holds Back through the spin demo. Three cutscenes had empty skip hooks; two only move the camera, so those hooks were removed rather than filled.\n- **Dev Tools rows:** pressing Enter on a slider or the town-level row did nothing. It now steps the value up.\n- **Regression:** making the skip hooks optional broke the boot, because one caller still called them directly. The next test run caught it and it's fixed.\n\nThe Testing Feedback counts are now checkable: `DEVLOG.md` lists all 40 issues, how each was caught, and which 7 were critical. The exit interview, the in-game About screen and the sections below use the same numbers.\n\nThe deliverable is `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521.html` in the work folder's root, with `exit-interview.json` beside it. The JSON parses and every field is filled; the HTML makes no external requests and its title reads \"Dragon Quilt Builders\".\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 21:55:21\n- **Source:** Dragon Quest Builders\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** Sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting and town building (real-time combat, a story chapter, a base siege and bosses)\n- **Perspective:** Third-person isometric 2.5D over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90-degree steps and zooms in steps; an optional three-quarter view is in Settings.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (one engine file changed). Plain JavaScript modules bundled into one HTML file by a Node build script, with my own voxel world, renderer, physics and A* navigation. Web Audio. AI Hub session.js 1.4.0, unmodified. Tests on Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 Chromium.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native pixel style: patchwork-quilt voxel blocks with stitched seams, furniture built as engine shapes, HD Humanoid and Blob rigs, warm storybook colours, and day/night lighting with torches and lanterns.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural rigs and move library, plus new moves (place, eat, stir, crank, hammer), cutscene poses and tweens, and particles.\n- **Audio:** All code: 14 original chip songs on one leitmotif, 13 jingles, 39 synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style voices for 10 speakers. A music director follows place, time of day and danger, and Voices has its own volume.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, a shared-world Quilting Bee for 1–4 players: the host runs the world and sends snapshots; own card, ready without a key press, standings and finish, leave, time-up, end and server mode. Playable solo with bots from the title. The story game is single-player and unchanged outside a session.\n- **Testing:** Allowed: headless scripts using `game.advance` / `game.debug`, including a full scripted playthrough, behind one command that prints only failures and a count; screenshots only for what scripts can't check; no subagents to review. Used: unit and systems tests, a full Chapter One playthrough by an AI pilot, a fake-hub network suite (2 and 4 copies, latency and packet loss, server mode), the AI Hub harness's 4 scenarios plus standalone, and a browser keyboard and mouse test. 312 checks pass.\n- **Research:** None on the web. I read the engine handoff and the AI Hub lib/hub README, session.js and harness.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Cost unknown; tokens unknown; session time about 3 hours (wall clock)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **Issues caught:** 40, listed in `DEVLOG.md`:\n   - 21 by the scripted tests;\n   - 14 by screenshot review (overlapping HUD panels, the tracker over the health bar, clipped prompt rows, tabs past the screen edge);\n   - 1 by reading code after a screenshot (Enter both turning and skipping the prologue);\n   - 4 by scans for stubs (the Bee's scoring hook that did nothing, the landing squash, cutscene skips, empty Dev row actions).\n2. **Critical:** 7.\n   - The game failed to boot because of a module load-order error.\n   - The residents' path walker recursed until the stack overflowed, freezing the game.\n   - The quest system was never connected, so the story couldn't advance.\n   - A Cheer bubble could be collected forever.\n   - The host's own edits never reached other players.\n   - The Builder was hidden behind walls in the first region.\n   - A late change broke the boot; the next test run caught it.\n3. **Most helpful:** The scripted playthrough, where an AI pilot plays Chapter One from the first quest to the credits using the same input a player does. It found the unconnected quests, the walker freeze, gaps in room detection, dialogs blocking play, and story jumps that left furniture blocked by plants.\n4. **Least helpful:** Early unit checks of screen picking and stair heights. Their failures were mostly wrong expectations in the tests, and the playthrough covered the same code better.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** Headless `game.advance` / `game.debug`: the whole game, networked copies included, runs in Node, so a full scripted playthrough was practical. The rigs, move library, built-in pause menu, settings, saves, controller glyphs and chip audio saved a lot of time.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** Yes. The x-ray silhouette guesses a fixed-size box per item, which hid the Builder until I passed exact screen boxes. I read the lighting tint as a colour, but it's a number. The engine's map is a height field, so a multi-level voxel world needed its own renderer. I also used `Mouse2` as an action name when it isn't one.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** The voxel world: storage, a face-sprite atlas per view, a renderer that merges block runs into depth-sorted occluders with cutaway, voxel physics and A* navigation. After that, room detection and the AI pilot behind attract mode, the tests and the Bee bots.\n4. **Hardest part:** Depth-sorting voxel runs against the engine's sprites in its painter-sorted queue, with x-ray, at two view angles and four camera turns.\n5. **What I changed:** One file, `my-3d2dge.js`: the Humanoid rig now accepts four sewing-tool weapons (needle, club, bodkin, seam ripper). The source's weapons double as building tools, and the rig's weapon list is closed. Nothing else changed.\n6. **One thing to change:** First-class multi-level or voxel map support, or at least an occluder API that takes exact screen boxes.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** It's concrete about what \"done\" means: file names, one test command, the exit-interview shape, the reply sections, and the rule that TASK.md wins over the plan. The freedom to change the engine and the AI Hub harness made multiplayer something I could actually verify.\n2. **Confusing:** PLAN.md names a different deliverable file (a max-effort name from another run) than TASK.md. Source Information's \"Testing\" can mean the rules or the tests run. Usage asks for cost and token numbers the agent can't see.\n3. **Hardest:** The scope: a feature-complete slice of a voxel building RPG, with building, rooms, residents, combat, a siege, bosses, a story, multiplayer, attract mode and dev tools, on a 2D engine with no voxel support, while checking the visuals only through screenshots.\n4. **What I'd change:** Make the plan's file name match TASK.md, give the agent a way to read its token and cost usage (or drop that field), and say whether \"Testing\" should list the rules or the tests run."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1284,"cache_read_input_tokens":180421,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1284},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"8ba16976-dd55-4a6a-b424-06c5df43c375","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:54:54.290Z","request_id":"req_011CghXZkgwX3Ssfky3ZZJCN"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not clearly satisfied. The bundled HTML exists and the final reply contains the four sections, but the no-stubs requirement is not demonstrated for the whole game. The last scan only covered TODO/FIXME/placeholder wording plus empty bodies and void sinks, and the final reply itself says the earlier hook scan found real gaps (a landing squash never drawn, cutscene skips leaving state behind, Dev rows with empty actions) that were only found and fixed after the fact. The Testing Feedback counts were also changed to match a DEVLOG list written after the fact, so they are not independently verified. Two empty-by-design hooks (the menu measuring dummy and the clipboard catch) were judged intended, not checked against TASK.md feature by feature. Because the transcript has no feature-by-feature check that every TASK.md item is implemented, it cannot clearly show the condition holds."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9acc64b9-af41-4198-9bbd-536ec13fbed1","uuid":"d1cd0992-c089-4281-8b7c-0089b4abfee9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:54:57.066Z","isSynthetic":true}
