st completion broke the boot (caught by the next test run).\",\"The scripted playthrough: an AI pilot plays Chapter One from the first quest to the credits and the side quests through the same input intents a player uses. It found the unwired quests, the path walker freeze, room detection gaps, dialogs blocking play, and story beats that left furniture blocked by plants. The content audit came second: it proves nothing defined is unused, and plants a probe of each kind to show it would catch one.\",\"Early unit checks of screen picking and stair heights: their failures were mostly wrong expectations in the tests themselves, not bugs in the game, and the playthrough covered the same code better.\"],\"engineFeedback\":[\"The headless game.advance and game.debug: the whole game, including networked copies, runs in Node without a browser, so a full scripted playthrough was practical. The Humanoid and Blob rigs with the move library give lively characters with no sprite work, and the built-in pause menu, settings, saves, controller glyphs and chip audio saved a lot of time.\",\"Yes. The x-ray silhouette mask guesses a fixed-size box for each queued item, which hid the Builder behind tall voxel walls until I passed exact screen boxes. I misread the lighting tint as a colour (it is a number). The engine map is a height field, so a multi-level voxel world needed its own renderer. I also used an input name that is not an action (Mouse2) before checking the held set.\",\"The voxel world: storage, a face-sprite atlas per view, a renderer that merges block runs into depth-sorted occluders with cutaway and a see-through slot toward the camera, voxel physics and A* navigation. After that, room detection and the AI pilot that drives the attract mode, the tests and the Bee bots.\",\"Depth sorting voxel runs against the engine's sprites in its painter-sorted queue (occluder, top and box rules, the lift order hook), with x-ray, at two view angles and four camera turns.\",\"One file, my-3d2dge.js: the Humanoid rig now accepts four more held items (a darning needle, a cobble club, a copper bodkin and a seam ripper) drawn in its HD drawer, because the source's weapons are also its building tools and the rig's weapon list is closed. Nothing else in the engine was changed.\",\"First-class multi-level or voxel map support in the renderer, or at least an occluder API that takes exact screen boxes, so a block-building game does not need its own renderer.\"],\"promptFeedback\":[\"It is concrete about what done means: the file names, one test command that prints only failures, the exit interview shape, the final reply sections, and a clear rule that TASK.md wins over the plan. The freedom to change the engine, and an AI Hub spec with a harness to test against, made the multiplayer verifiable.\",\"The plan names a different deliverable file (a max-effort name from another run) than TASK.md does; TASK.md wins, but it is easy to get wrong. The Source Information field Testing asks for the testing parameters allowed, which reads two ways (the rules, or what was run). Usage asks for cost and token numbers the agent cannot see.\",\"The scope: a feature-complete vertical slice of a voxel building RPG (building, rooms, residents, combat, a siege, bosses, a story chapter, multiplayer, attract mode, dev tools) on a 2D engine with no voxel support, while checking the visuals only through screenshots.\",\"Make the plan's deliverable name match TASK.md, give the agent a way to read its own token and cost usage or drop that field, and say whether Testing should list the rules or the tests run.\"],\"aiHub\":{\"name\":\"Dragon Quilt Builders\",\"tagline\":\"Stitch a frayed world back together\",\"description\":\"The dragon Frayvern pulled out the Golden Thread and swallowed everyone's knack for making things, and you are the last Builder. Break and place blocks, craft from Notions, build rooms that residents move into, raise the town of Thimbleton, defend it in a siege and unpick the knotted boss Gnarlgrim, or build a Quilt Barn together in a co-op Quilting Bee round.\",\"type\":\"Game\",\"genre\":\"RPG\",\"source\":\"Dragon Quest Builders\",\"series\":\"Dragon Quest\",\"players\":\"1–4\",\"controls\":\"Left stick or WASD move, Cross or Space jump, Square or left click swing and break, Triangle or right click place, L1 and R1 or the mouse wheel pick items, Circle or F talk, Q and E or the right stick turn the camera\",\"tags\":[\"building\",\"crafting\",\"co-op\",\"fantasy\",\"comedy\",\"pixel art\"]}};</script>"," <script>/* src/00-core.js */"," // Dragon Quilt Builders: core. The DQ namespace, constants, hashing, seeded streams, the event bus, tuning, the play"," // context. Every later module is an IIFE that adds to DQ and reads only what earlier files defined."]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521/dev/index.html","hunks":[{"oldStart":20,"oldLines":7,"newStart":20,"newLines":7,"lines":[" <div id=\"e3-boot\" role=\"status\" style=\"position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:40;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:flex-end;gap:6px;padding:20px;background:#06051a;color:#c8b8e8;font:12px/1.5 ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace\"><style>@keyframes e3-run{from{transform:translateX(-100%)}to{transform:translateX(340%)}}@keyframes e3-late{to{opacity:1}}</style><b style=\"color:#ffe7a0;font-size:16px;letter-spacing:.08em\">DRAGON QUILT BUILDERS</b><span>Unrolling the quilt...</span><span style=\"display:block;height:4px;max-width:440px;overflow:hidden;background:#ffffff14\"><span style=\"display:block;width:30%;height:100%;background:#e8c870;animation:e3-run 1.2s ease-in-out infinite\"></span></span><span style=\"opacity:0;animation:e3-late .3s 15s forwards\">Still starting. If this stays, the game's script did not run: reload, or open the browser's console.</span></div>"," <canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>"," <script src=\"../engine/my-3d2dge.js\"></script>","-<script>globalThis.DQ_ABOUT = {\"sourceInformation\":{\"date\":\"2026-11-03 21:55:21\",\"source\":\"Dragon Quest Builders\",\"plan\":\"PLAN.md\",\"genre\":\"Sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting and town building (real-time combat, a story chapter, a base siege and bosses)\",\"perspective\":\"Third-person isometric 2.5D over a true voxel world; the camera turns in 90-degree steps and zooms in steps; an optional three-quarter view in Settings\",\"tech\":\"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (Canvas 2D pixel renderer, rigs, audio, input, saves; one engine file changed), plain JavaScript modules bundled into one HTML file by a Node build script, my own voxel world, renderer, physics and A* navigation on top of the engine, Web Audio, AI Hub Session client session.js 1.4.0 (unmodified); tests on Node 24 (vm, game.advance and game.debug) and Playwright 1.63.0 Chromium\",\"artDirection\":\"The engine's native pixel style: patchwork-quilt voxel blocks with stitched seams and fabric textures, furniture and props built as engine shapes, HD Humanoid and Blob rigs for the Builder, residents and monsters, warm storybook colours, day and night lighting with torches and lanterns\",\"animation\":\"The engine's procedural rigs and move library (E.MOVES, attacks and combos) plus new moves for placing, eating, stirring, cranking the ballista and hammering at stations, poses and tweens for cutscenes, particles for Cheer bubbles, glints, stitches and fraying\",\"audio\":\"All code, no audio files: 14 original chip songs built on one leitmotif, 13 jingles (Notion, room, level up and more), 39 synthesized sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices with a pitch and timbre per speaker (10 voices), a music director that follows place, time of day and danger, and a separate Voices volume\",\"online\":\"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world Quilting Bee round for 1-4 players: host-authoritative world edits and snapshots, the game's own card, ready without a key press, standings and finish, leave, time-up, end and server mode; playable solo from the title with bot helpers; the story game is single-player and unchanged outside a session\",\"testing\":\"Allowed: headless scripts that play the game and check its state (game.advance and game.debug), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; screenshots only for what a script cannot check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Used: node tests/run.mjs (unit, systems, full Chapter One playthrough by an AI pilot, a fake-hub network suite with 2 and 4 copies, latency and loss, server mode) and --browser (the AI Hub harness 4 scenarios plus standalone, real keyboard and mouse input in Chromium); 312 checks pass\",\"research\":\"None on the web. Read the engine handoff (API notes and the renderer, body, input and dialog source) and the AI Hub lib/hub README, session.js and harness\",\"environment\":\"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\"model\":\"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\",\"usage\":\"Cost unknown; tokens unknown; session time about 3 hours (2026-11-03 21:55 to about 2026-11-04 00:55, wall clock)\"},\"testingFeedback\":[\"40, listed in DEVLOG.md: 21 caught by the scripted tests (headless suites, the network suite and the browser checks), 14 by screenshot review (HUD panels overlapping, the quest tracker covering the health bar, clipped prompt rows, tabs past the screen edge), 1 by reading code after a screenshot (Enter both turning and skipping the prologue), and 4 by scans for stubs (a Bee scoring hook that did nothing, a landing squash never drawn, cutscene skips that left a training dummy or skipped a note, Dev rows with empty actions).\",\"7: the game failing to boot from a module load-order error, the residents' path walker recursing until the stack overflowed (a freeze), the quest system never being wired so the story could not advance, a Cheer bubble that could be collected forever, the host's own edits never reaching other players (a desync in multiplayer), the Builder hidden behind walls in the first region by the x-ray and cutaway, and a late regression where a quiet quest completion broke the boot (caught by the next test run).\",\"The scripted playthrough: an AI pilot plays Chapter One from the first quest to the credits and the side quests through the same input intents a player uses. It found the unwired quests, the path walker freeze, room detection gaps, dialogs blocking play, and story beats that left furniture blocked by plants.\",\"Early unit checks of screen picking and stair heights: their failures were mostly wrong expectations in the tests themselves, not bugs in the game, and the playthrough covered the same code better.\"],\"engineFeedback\":[\"The headless game.advance and game.debug: the whole game, including networked copies, runs in Node without a browser, so a full scripted playthrough was practical. The Humanoid and Blob rigs with the move library give lively characters with no sprite work, and the built-in pause menu, settings, saves, controller glyphs and chip audio saved a lot of time.\",\"Yes. The x-ray silhouette mask guesses a fixed-size box for each queued item, which hid the Builder behind tall voxel walls until I passed exact screen boxes. I misread the lighting tint as a colour (it is a number). The engine map is a height field, so a multi-level voxel world needed its own renderer. I also used an input name that is not an action (Mouse2) before checking the held set.\",\"The voxel world: storage, a face-sprite atlas per view, a renderer that merges block runs into depth-sorted occluders with cutaway and a see-through slot toward the camera, voxel physics and A* navigation. After that, room detection and the AI pilot that drives the attract mode, the tests and the Bee bots.\",\"Depth sorting voxel runs against the engine's sprites in its painter-sorted queue (occluder, top and box rules, the lift order hook), with x-ray, at two view angles and four camera turns.\",\"One file, my-3d2dge.js: the Humanoid rig now accepts four more held items (a darning needle, a cobble club, a copper bodkin and a seam ripper) drawn in its HD drawer, because the source's weapons are also its building tools and the rig's weapon list is closed. Nothing else in the engine was changed.\",\"First-class multi-level or voxel map support in the renderer, or at least an occluder API that takes exact screen boxes, so a block-building game does not need its own renderer.\"],\"promptFeedback\":[\"It is concrete about what done means: the file names, one test command that prints only failures, the exit interview shape, the final reply sections, and a clear rule that TASK.md wins over the plan. The freedom to change the engine, and an AI Hub spec with a harness to test against, made the multiplayer verifiable.\",\"The plan names a different deliverable file (a max-effort name from another run) than TASK.md does; TASK.md wins, but it is easy to get wrong. The Source Information field Testing asks for the testing parameters allowed, which reads two ways (the rules, or what was run). Usage asks for cost and token numbers the agent cannot see.\",\"The scope: a feature-complete vertical slice of a voxel building RPG (building, rooms, residents, combat, a siege, bosses, a story chapter, multiplayer, attract mode, dev tools) on a 2D engine with no voxel support, while checking the visuals only through screenshots.\",\"Make the plan's deliverable name match TASK.md, give the agent a way to read its own token and cost usage or drop that field, and say whether Testing should list the rules or the tests run.\"],\"aiHub\":{\"name\":\"Dragon Quilt Builders\",\"tagline\":\"Stitch a frayed world back together\",\"description\":\"The dragon Frayvern pulled out the Golden Thread and swallowed everyone's knack for making things, and you are the last Builder. Break and place blocks, craft from Notions, build rooms that residents move into, raise the town of Thimbleton, defend it in a siege and unpick the knotted boss Gnarlgrim, or build a Quilt Barn together in a co-op Quilting Bee round.\",\"type\":\"Game\",\"genre\":\"RPG\",\"source\":\"Dragon Quest Builders\",\"series\":\"Dragon Quest\",\"players\":\"1–4\",\"controls\":\"Left stick or WASD move, Cross or Space jump, Square or left click swing and break, Triangle or right click place, L1 and R1 or the mouse wheel pick items, Circle or F talk, Q and E or the right stick turn the camera\",\"tags\":[\"building\",\"crafting\",\"co-op\",\"fantasy\",\"comedy\",\"pixel art\"]}};</script>","+<script>globalThis.DQ_ABOUT = {\"sourceInformation\":{\"date\":\"2026-11-03 21:55:21\",\"source\":\"Dragon Quest Builders\",\"plan\":\"PLAN.md\",\"genre\":\"Sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting and town building (real-time combat, a story chapter, a base siege and bosses)\",\"perspective\":\"Third-person isometric 2.5D over a true voxel world; the camera turns in 90-degree steps and zooms in steps; an optional three-quarter view in Settings\",\"tech\":\"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff (Canvas 2D pixel renderer, rigs, audio, input, saves; one engine file changed), plain JavaScript modules bundled into one HTML file by a Node build script, my own voxel world, renderer, physics and A* navigation on top of the engine, Web Audio, AI Hub Session client session.js 1.4.0 (unmodified); tests on Node 24 (vm, game.advance and game.debug) and Playwright 1.63.0 Chromium\",\"artDirection\":\"The engine's native pixel style: patchwork-quilt voxel blocks with stitched seams and fabric textures, furniture and props built as engine shapes, HD Humanoid and Blob rigs for the Builder, residents and monsters, warm storybook colours, day and night lighting with torches and lanterns\",\"animation\":\"The engine's procedural rigs and move library (E.MOVES, attacks and combos) plus new moves for placing, eating, stirring, cranking the ballista and hammering at stations, poses and tweens for cutscenes, particles for Cheer bubbles, glints, stitches and fraying\",\"audio\":\"All code, no audio files: 14 original chip songs built on one leitmotif, 13 jingles (Notion, room, level up and more), 39 synthesized sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices with a pitch and timbre per speaker (10 voices), a music director that follows place, time of day and danger, and a separate Voices volume\",\"online\":\"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world Quilting Bee round for 1-4 players: host-authoritative world edits and snapshots, the game's own card, ready without a key press, standings and finish, leave, time-up, end and server mode; playable solo from the title with bot helpers; the story game is single-player and unchanged outside a session\",\"testing\":\"Allowed: headless scripts that play the game and check its state (game.advance and game.debug), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; screenshots only for what a script cannot check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Used: node tests/run.mjs (unit, systems, full Chapter One playthrough by an AI pilot, a fake-hub network suite with 2 and 4 copies, latency and loss, server mode) and --browser (the AI Hub harness 4 scenarios plus standalone, real keyboard and mouse input in Chromium); 356 checks pass, including a TASK.md requirements suite and a content audit (REQUIREMENTS.md maps each TASK.md line to its checks)\",\"research\":\"None on the web. Read the engine handoff (API notes and the renderer, body, input and dialog source) and the AI Hub lib/hub README, session.js and harness\",\"environment\":\"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\"model\":\"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\",\"usage\":\"Cost unknown; tokens unknown; session time about 3 hours 20 minutes (2026-11-03 21:55 to about 2026-11-04 01:15, wall clock)\"},\"testingFeedback\":[\"43, listed in DEVLOG.md: 21 caught by the scripted tests (headless suites, the network suite and the browser checks), 14 by screenshot review (HUD panels overlapping, the quest tracker covering the health bar, clipped prompt rows, tabs past the screen edge), 1 by reading code after a screenshot (Enter both turning and skipping the prologue), 4 by scans for stubs (a Bee scoring hook that did nothing, a landing squash never drawn, cutscene skips that left a training dummy or skipped a note, Dev rows with empty actions), and 3 by a content audit that requires everything defined to be used (a Golden Thimble with no effect, a jingle never played, a rock decoration never placed).\",\"7: the game failing to boot from a module load-order error, the residents' path walker recursing until the stack overflowed (a freeze), the quest system never being wired so the story could not advance, a Cheer bubble that could be collected forever, the host's own edits never reaching other players (a desync in multiplayer), the Builder hidden behind walls in the first region by the x-ray and cutaway, and a late regression where a quiet quest completion broke the boot (caught by the next test run).\",\"The scripted playthrough: an AI pilot plays Chapter One from the first quest to the credits and the side quests through the same input intents a player uses. It found the unwired quests, the path walker freeze, room detection gaps, dialogs blocking play, and story beats that left furniture blocked by plants. The content audit came second: it proves nothing defined is unused, and plants a probe of each kind to show it would catch one.\",\"Early unit checks of screen picking and stair heights: their failures were mostly wrong expectations in the tests themselves, not bugs in the game, and the playthrough covered the same code better.\"],\"engineFeedback\":[\"The headless game.advance and game.debug: the whole game, including networked copies, runs in Node without a browser, so a full scripted playthrough was practical. The Humanoid and Blob rigs with the move library give lively characters with no sprite work, and the built-in pause menu, settings, saves, controller glyphs and chip audio saved a lot of time.\",\"Yes. The x-ray silhouette mask guesses a fixed-size box for each queued item, which hid the Builder behind tall voxel walls until I passed exact screen boxes. I misread the lighting tint as a colour (it is a number). The engine map is a height field, so a multi-level voxel world needed its own renderer. I also used an input name that is not an action (Mouse2) before checking the held set.\",\"The voxel world: storage, a face-sprite atlas per view, a renderer that merges block runs into depth-sorted occluders with cutaway and a see-through slot toward the camera, voxel physics and A* navigation. After that, room detection and the AI pilot that drives the attract mode, the tests and the Bee bots.\",\"Depth sorting voxel runs against the engine's sprites in its painter-sorted queue (occluder, top and box rules, the lift order hook), with x-ray, at two view angles and four camera turns.\",\"One file, my-3d2dge.js: the Humanoid rig now accepts four more held items (a darning needle, a cobble club, a copper bodkin and a seam ripper) drawn in its HD drawer, because the source's weapons are also its building tools and the rig's weapon list is closed. Nothing else in the engine was changed.\",\"First-class multi-level or voxel map support in the renderer, or at least an occluder API that takes exact screen boxes, so a block-building game does not need its own renderer.\"],\"promptFeedback\":[\"It is concrete about what done means: the file names, one test command that prints only failures, the exit interview shape, the final reply sections, and a clear rule that TASK.md wins over the plan. The freedom to change the engine, and an AI Hub spec with a harness to test against, made the multiplayer verifiable.\",\"The plan names a different deliverable file (a max-effort name from another run) than TASK.md does; TASK.md wins, but it is easy to get wrong. The Source Information field Testing asks for the testing parameters allowed, which reads two ways (the rules, or what was run). Usage asks for cost and token numbers the agent cannot see.\",\"The scope: a feature-complete vertical slice of a voxel building RPG (building, rooms, residents, combat, a siege, bosses, a story chapter, multiplayer, attract mode, dev tools) on a 2D engine with no voxel support, while checking the visuals only through screenshots.\",\"Make the plan's deliverable name match TASK.md, give the agent a way to read its own token and cost usage or drop that field, and say whether Testing should list the rules or the tests run.\"],\"aiHub\":{\"name\":\"Dragon Quilt Builders\",\"tagline\":\"Stitch a frayed world back together\",\"description\":\"The dragon Frayvern pulled out the Golden Thread and swallowed everyone's knack for making things, and you are the last Builder. Break and place blocks, craft from Notions, build rooms that residents move into, raise the town of Thimbleton, defend it in a siege and unpick the knotted boss Gnarlgrim, or build a Quilt Barn together in a co-op Quilting Bee round.\",\"type\":\"Game\",\"genre\":\"RPG\",\"source\":\"Dragon Quest Builders\",\"series\":\"Dragon Quest\",\"players\":\"1–4\",\"controls\":\"Left stick or WASD move, Cross or Space jump, Square or left click swing and break, Triangle or right click place, L1 and R1 or the mouse wheel pick items, Circle or F talk, Q and E or the right stick turn the camera\",\"tags\":[\"building\",\"crafting\",\"co-op\",\"fantasy\",\"comedy\",\"pixel art\"]}};</script>"," <script src=\"../src/00-core.js\"></script>"," <script src=\"../src/01-data-blocks.js\"></script>"," <script src=\"../src/02-data-items.js\"></script>"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453.html","hunks":[{"oldStart":24831,"oldLines":26,"newStart":24831,"newLines":26,"lines":["     ['Tech', 'my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, patched: see Engine Feedback), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, Canvas 2D (WebGPU lighting where the browser has it), the engine\\'s Mocap player with a cut set of 31 clips (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion, CMU), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Built and tested with Node 24, Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) and Python 3\\'s http.server for the hub harness. One self-contained HTML file, no network at run time.'],","     ['Art Direction', 'The engine\\'s native PS1-era pixel art: HD Humanoid rigs in a new athletic build, dressed in procedurally drawn swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 pixel patterns) and accessories; resort props written as data shapes; time-of-day skies, sea and lighting; a gold-and-teal 10-foot pixel UI with hand-drawn button glyphs.'],","     ['Animation', 'The engine\\'s procedural Humanoid rig, extended with 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay driven by the volleyball simulation (a spike\\'s jump starts at its contact time minus its rise), plus 31 motion-capture clips played through Mocap.drive for celebrations, idles, dances, swimming and reactions.'],","-    ['Audio', 'All in code with Web Audio: 9 original songs from a chord-aware song builder (steel, marimba, bell, organ and pluck instruments; a rally drum layer; a tempo lift at match point; key changes), 10 jingles, about 60 sound effects, ambience loops (waves, wind, crowd, pool, casino, crickets) and Animalese-style gibberish voices synthesized letter by letter for every speaker.'],","+    ['Audio', 'All in code with Web Audio: 9 original songs from a chord-aware song builder (steel, marimba, bell, organ and pluck instruments; a rally drum layer; a tempo lift at match point; key changes), 10 jingles, 50 sound effects, ambience loops (waves, wind, crowd, pool, casino, crickets) and Animalese-style gibberish voices synthesized letter by letter for every speaker.'],","     ['Online', 'AI Hub sessions (shared world, 1-4 players): the game\\'s own card, one match on the hub\\'s clock, host-authoritative rules and AI, every copy flying the ball from the same launches, client-judged touches the host validates, snapshots for positions, CPUs for empty or left seats, finish() with standings, awards and stats.'],","-    ['Testing', 'One command (node test/run.mjs) that prints only failures and a count: 19 suites and about 760 checks. Headless Node runs with game.advance and game.debug: units (ball flight, rules, judge, data), AI-vs-AI match statistics, the vacation, shops, story, minigames, UI, attract, dev mode, a fake-hub netcode test at 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, and a scripted playthrough of the whole vacation as all six fighters to both endings. Playwright from file:// (a tour, the attract, an emulated touch-only phone), the engine\\'s tools/check.mjs, the AI Hub harness on 127.0.0.1 (4 scenarios), and screenshots and rig sheets for art.'],","+    ['Testing', 'One command (node test/run.mjs) that prints only failures and a count: 22 suites and @CHECKS@ checks, all passing on the final run. Headless in Node with game.advance and game.debug: units (ball flight, rules, judge, data), AI-vs-AI match statistics, the vacation, shops, story, minigames, UI, attract, dev mode, saves, revisiting all 27 scene setups after random input, a fake-hub netcode test at 150 ms latency with jitter and 5% loss, and a scripted playthrough of the whole vacation as all six fighters, to both endings. In Chromium via Playwright from file://: a tour, the attract, an emulated touch-only phone, and a four-player Beach Match on two keyboard halves and two emulated controllers (navigator.getGamepads stubbed with standard-mapping pads). That run checks PlayStation and Xbox glyphs, Serve Cam in a live rally, every attract beat and every photo filter. No physical controller was tested. Also the engine\\'s tools/check.mjs, the AI Hub harness on 127.0.0.1 (4 scenarios), and screenshots and rig sheets for the art. TESTING-LOG.md lists every issue found.'],","     ['Research', 'No web research. Read the engine handoff (API card, guide, index, asset card), the sports kit, the engine source for the Humanoid, Dialog, audio, input and Shell, the AI Hub protocol README, client and harness checks, and the engine repository\\'s motion-capture catalogs. The source game was described from general knowledge, by the plan.'],","     ['Environment', 'Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown'],","     ['Model', 'Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh'],","-    ['Usage', 'unknown (no cost or token meter was available to the model; the build session ran from about 22:55 on 2026-11-03 to about 01:00 on 2026-11-04, roughly two hours)']","+    ['Usage', 'Cost and tokens unknown (no meter was available to the model). By the system clock the build ran from 2026-11-03 22:54:53 (TASK.md\\'s start time) to @END@ when this text was last updated, @SPAN@.']","   ],","   testing: [","-    ['How many issues did testing catch?', 'About 30. Scripted tests caught about 20 (rallies that never ended because the AI dug everything, a perfect bot losing because the CPU spiked into the net, a starter partner never assigned, scene state that stuck between visits, Pool Hop\\'s beat clock, the attract missing input during fades, a touch flag set only in browsers, a name clash between two modules) and screenshots about 10 (night lighting, overlapping text and panels, tiny text in the engine\\'s HTML menus, off-screen dev sliders).'],","-    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'Six: the Welcome Match never starting for Ingrid (no one liked her enough to partner up), the game freezing after the Welcome Match (a cutscene flag left set from the arrival), Pool Hop unwinnable when entered from the pool, the Final never being called on a second vacation, the credits never ending on a second run, and the title crashing while drawing its prompt in the browser (a name clash).'],","-    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: six full vacations, one per fighter, from the invitation through every day\\'s events, matches, minigames, shopping and gifts to both endings, the credits and the results. It found most of the critical bugs, all of them state that only breaks on a later step or a second run.'],","+    ['How many issues did testing catch?', '47 game issues, each listed in TESTING-LOG.md. Scripted tests caught 20, for example: rallies that never ended because the AI dug everything; a perfect player losing because the CPU spiked into the net; a starting partner never assigned; scene state carried from one visit to the next; Continue and Load crashing; Pool Hop\\'s beat clock; the attract missing input during fades. Screenshots and rig sheets caught 27, for example: the net seen edge-on; overlapping text and panels; tiny text in the engine\\'s HTML menus; two menu actions on one key for keyboard-half players; Serve Cam cutting off a player. Separately, 10 bugs in the tests themselves.'],","+    ['How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?', 'Seven: the Welcome Match never starting for Ingrid (no one liked her enough to partner up); the game freezing after the Welcome Match (a cutscene flag left set from the arrival); Pool Hop unwinnable when entered from the pool; the Final never called on a second vacation; the credits never ending on a second run; the title crashing while drawing its prompt in the browser (a name clash); and Continue and Load crashing (a missing function).'],","+    ['What was the most helpful test you ran?', 'The scripted playthrough: six full vacations, one per fighter, from the invitation through every day\\'s events, matches, minigames, shopping and gifts to both endings, the credits and the results. It found 5 of the 7 critical bugs, all of them state that only breaks on a later step or a second run. The revisit test (every scene entered twice, with random input in between) found the 7th.'],","     ['What was the least helpful test you ran?', 'The first version of \"no match phase stuck over 15 seconds\". It flagged long but legal rallies, not stuck states, until I redefined it as 15 seconds with no touch and no phase change.']","   ],","   engine: [","     ['What did you like most about the engine?', 'That the whole game runs headless in exact fixed steps. A full vacation, four networked copies of a match and the attract all run in Node in seconds and replay exactly, and the Humanoid is drawn from math, so new outfits and poses are code, not art files.'],","-    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'A few times. The Shell theme\\'s font is a font family, not a CSS shorthand (the menus came out tiny). Mocap is a top-level const, not window.Mocap. game.go with a fade switches scenes later, so tests must wait. Scene objects are reused on every visit, so flags left in them break the next visit. And at yaw 0 the Broadcast view saw the net edge-on.'],","+    ['Did you get stuck or guess wrong?', 'A few times. The Shell theme\\'s font is a font family, not a CSS shorthand (the menus came out tiny). Mocap is a top-level const, not window.Mocap. game.go with a fade switches scenes later, so tests must wait. Scene objects are reused on every visit, so flags left in them break the next visit. And at yaw 0 the Broadcast view saw the net edge-on. I also trusted Input.halfKeys with menu presets: it gave two menu actions the same key, and I only noticed in a four-player screenshot.'],","     ['What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?', 'The volleyball simulation: closed-form ball flight with precomputed net and landing events, contact planning (who plays the ball, when, where and at what height), the timing judge, the AI with its error model, and a design where the same launches drive local and online play.'],","     ['What was the hardest part about using the engine?', 'Dressing the Humanoid in swimsuits. Its torso is one polygon in one color, so cuts and patterns needed a per-pixel torso fill inside the engine\\'s draw code, kept in step with its depth sorting and shading.'],","-    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', '(1) PlayStation symbols, arrows, accents and punctuation in the pixel font, for prompts and the writing. (2) Button symbols as a Game setting, PlayStation by default with Xbox as the option. (3) A per-letter dialog voice hook, for the Animalese voices. (4) Audio: a voice bus and Voices volume, audio.bus(), PeriodicWave instruments, and decay, filter and detune for music tracks, for the tropical score. (5) Humanoid: an athletic build, swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 patterns), patterned shirts, shorts, six hair styles, a sun hat and a visor, an accessories hook, sandal straps, 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay, for the cast and the volleyball moves. (6) input.reserveCombo, so Create + Options stays the AI Hub\\'s. (7) touch: \\'never\\', for touch-only devices. (8) E.onStep, a hook that runs through fades and pauses, so the attract ends on any input.'],","+    ['What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)', '(1) PlayStation symbols, arrows, accents and punctuation in the pixel font, for prompts and the writing. (2) Button symbols as a Game setting, PlayStation by default with Xbox as the option. (3) A per-letter dialog voice hook, for the Animalese voices. (4) Audio: a voice bus and Voices volume, audio.bus(), PeriodicWave instruments, and decay, filter and detune for music tracks, for the tropical score. (5) Humanoid: an athletic build, swimsuits (7 cuts, 14 patterns), patterned shirts, shorts, six hair styles, a sun hat and a visor, an accessories hook, sandal straps, 14 held poses and a timed arm and body overlay, for the cast and the volleyball moves. (6) input.reserveCombo, so Create + Options stays the AI Hub\\'s. (7) touch: \\'never\\', for touch-only devices. (8) E.onStep, a hook that runs through fades and pauses, so the attract ends on any input. (9) Input.halfKeys gives each action a button no earlier action holds, so a menu preset\\'s alt and extra no longer share keys with confirm and cancel on a keyboard half.'],","     ['If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?', 'Make scenes fresh instances on every visit, or say loudly that they persist. Sticky scene state caused more game-breaking bugs than anything else.']","   ],","   prompt: ["]}]}],"moreFiles":29,"changedFiles":["/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521.html","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521/check-output/hub/game.html","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-215521__x5b1962/work/DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-215521/dev/index.html","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453.html","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453/ENGINE-CHANGES.md","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-225453__x8def6b/work/AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-225453/README-DEV.md","/home/micha